hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"7201","Al-Hanzali from Shaddad ibn Aws. Narrated Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Sufyan from Said al-Jurayri from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir from a man of Banu Hanzala from Shaddad ibn Aws, who said: Shall I not teach you what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us: O Allah, I ask You for steadfastness in the matter, and resolve upon guidance, and I ask You for gratitude for Your blessing, and excellence of Your worship, and I ask You for a sound heart, and a truthful tongue, and I ask You of the best of what You know, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what You know, and I seek Your forgiveness for what You know. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim servant who recites a sura of the Book of Allah at his sleep, but Allah entrusts to him an angel whom nothing approaches, until he wakes whenever he wakes."
"7202","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Khalid ibn Abd Allah from al-Jurayri from Abu al-Ala from al-Hanzali from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites a sura of the Quran when he takes his bed, an angel is entrusted to him guarding him until he wakes whenever he wakes. And I heard him say: I ask You for steadfastness in the matter, and I ask You for firm resolve of guidance, and I ask You for gratitude for Your blessing, and I ask You for excellence of Your worship, and I ask You for a sound heart and a truthful tongue, and I seek Your forgiveness for what You know, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what You know."
"7203","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Wahb ibn Baqiyya from Khalid from al-Jurayri from Abu al-Ala from al-Hanzali from Shaddad ibn Aws, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"7204","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal from Said al-Jurayri from Abu al-Ala from a man of Banu Mujashi from Shaddad ibn Aws, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us to say in our prayer: O Allah, I ask You for firmness in the matter, and I ask You for firm resolve of guidance... and he mentioned similar to it."
"7205","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Abu Umar al-Darir from Adi ibn al-Fadl from Said al-Jurayri from Abu al-Ala ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir from two men whom he named from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in his prayer: O Allah, I ask You for steadfastness in the matter, and firm resolve of guidance, and I ask You for gratitude for Your blessing, and excellence of Your worship, and I ask You for a sound heart, and a truthful tongue, and I ask You of the best of what You know, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what You know, and I seek Your forgiveness for what You know."
"7206","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Abu Umar al-Darir from Hammad ibn Salama, and narrated Abu Khalifa from Musa ibn Ismail from Hammad ibn Salama from Said al-Jurayri from Abu al-Ala from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in his prayer: O Allah, I ask You for steadfastness in the matter and firm resolve of guidance, and I ask You for gratitude for Your blessing and excellence of Your worship, and I ask You for a sound heart, and a truthful tongue, and I ask You of the best of what You know, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what You know, and I seek Your forgiveness for what You know."
"7207","Al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan from Shaddad ibn Aws. Narrated Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Rasibi from Muhallab ibn al-Ala from Shuayb ibn Bayan al-Saffar from Imran al-Qattan from Qatada from al-Hasan from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Poverty is more adorned upon the believer than a handsome cheek-strap on a horse's cheek."
"7208","Narrated Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Rasibi from Muhallab ibn al-Ala from Shuayb ibn Bayan al-Saffar from Imran al-Qattan from Qatada from al-Hasan from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first thing you will lose of your religion is trust."
"7209","Narrated Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Rasibi from Muhallab ibn al-Ala from Shuayb ibn Bayan from Imran al-Qattan from Qatada from al-Hasan from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first thing lifted from people is humility in prayer."
"7210","Narrated Ubayd al-Ijli from Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Madaini from Shababa ibn Sawwar from al-Mughira ibn Muslim from Matar al-Warraq from al-Hasan from Shaddad ibn Aws, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"7211","Al-Ala ibn Ziyad al-Adawi from Shaddad. Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Muhammad ibn Mirdas from Jariya ibn Harim from Ishaq ibn Suwayd from al-Ala ibn Ziyad from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no servant who, morning and evening, says: O Allah, You are my Lord; there is no god but You; You created me and I am Your servant, and upon Your covenant and Your promise as much as I can; I acknowledge Your blessing upon me, and I acknowledge my sin; so forgive my sins, for none forgives them but You; then if he dies that day he enters Paradise, and if he dies that night he enters Paradise."
"7212","Anbasa ibn Abi Sufyan from Shaddad. Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki from Yaqub ibn Humayd from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, freedman of Muzayna, from Safwan ibn Sulaym from Dawud ibn Salih from Muawiya ibn Said from Anbasa ibn Abi Sufyan, who said: Shaddad ibn Aws said - and he is the nephew of Hassan ibn Thabit al-Ansari, and he conquered Iliya for Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, while reviewing with Muawiya, may Allah be pleased with him - and he mentioned leadership, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention leadership, saying: The beginning of leadership is blame, its second is regret, and its third is punishment from Allah on the Day of Resurrection, except him whom He shows mercy and who is just. And he said: Like this and like this with his hand, with wealth. Then he fell silent as long as Allah willed, then said: How about justice with close kin?"
"7213","Umar ibn Rabia from Shaddad ibn Aws. Narrated Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki from Abd al-Aziz ibn Yahya al-Madani from Sulayman ibn Bilal from Kathir ibn Zayd from Umar ibn Rabia from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of the master of seeking forgiveness? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: O Allah, there is no god but You; You created me and I am Your servant, and upon Your covenant and Your promise as much as I can; I seek refuge in You from the evil of what I have done; I acknowledge to You Your blessing, and I acknowledge to You my sins; so forgive me, for none forgives sins but You. No one says it in the evening then dies that night but Paradise is guaranteed for him; and no one says it in the morning then death comes to him but Paradise is guaranteed for him."
"7214","Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit from Shaddad. Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Abd al-Warith from Layth from Abd al-Rahman from Shaddad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"7215","Al-Mughira ibn Said ibn Nawfal from Shaddad. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Zayd ibn Hubab - Kathir ibn Zayd al-Madani narrated to me - al-Mughira ibn Said ibn Nawfal narrated to me from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Shall I not guide you to the master of seeking forgiveness? That you say: O Allah, You are my God; You created me, and I am upon Your covenant and Your promise as much as I can; I seek refuge in You from the evil of what I have done, and I acknowledge to You Your blessing upon me, and I acknowledge to You my sins; for none forgives sins but You. There is no servant who says it, then his decreed time comes to him in his day before evening, or in his evening before morning, but he will be of the people of Paradise, if Allah wills."
"7216","Those named Shayba. Shayba ibn Uthman ibn Talha ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Uthman ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Dar ibn Qusayy al-Hajabi, kunya Abu Uthman; he became Muslim on the day of the Conquest, and it is said on the day of Hunayn, and he was of those whose hearts were reconciled. Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani from Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman from Abd Allah ibn Muslim ibn Hurmuz from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Zajjaj, who said: I said to Shayba ibn Uthman: O Abu Uthman, they claim that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the Kaba and did not pray in it. He said: They lied; he prayed two rakas between the two pillars, then pressed to them his belly and his back."
"7217","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib from Muhammad ibn Bukayr al-Hadrami, and narrated Abdan ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi from Qutayba ibn Said, both saying: Ayyub ibn Jabir narrated to us from Sadaqa ibn Said from Musab ibn Shayba from his father, who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn; by Allah, Islam did not bring me out, nor knowledge of him, but I disdained that Hawazin prevail over Quraysh; and I said while standing with him: O Messenger of Allah, I see piebald horses? He said: O Shayba, none sees them but a disbeliever. Then he struck his hand upon my chest, then said: O Allah, guide Shayba. Then he struck it the second, then said: O Allah, guide Shayba. Then he struck it the third, and said: O Allah, guide Shayba. By Allah, he did not lift his hand from my chest at the third until there was none of Allah's creation more beloved to me than him. He said: Then people met, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon a she-camel or mule, Umar holding its bridle, and al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib urging his mount; and the Muslims fled; so al-Abbas called in his loud voice, saying: Where are the first Muhajirun? Where are the companions of Surat al-Baqara? While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was saying forward: I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of Abd al-Muttalib. Then the Muslims turned and clashed with swords; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Now the oven has heated. He said: And Allah routed the polytheists."
"7218","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Ibn al-Asbahani from Ibn al-Mubarak from Abu Bakr al-Hudhali from Ikrima, who said: Shayba ibn Uthman said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Hunayn, I remembered my father and my uncle, whom Ali and Hamza had killed; and I said: Today I take my revenge upon Muhammad; so I came to him, and there was al-Abbas on his right, upon him a white coat of mail as if silver; and the dust was lifted from it; and I said: His uncle will not fail him; so I came to his left, and there I was with Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith; and I said: His cousin, and he will not fail him; so I came to him from behind, and drew near and drew near until nothing remained but to strike him with the sword, when there was raised to me a flame of fire like lightning, and I feared it would scorch me, so I stepped back; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to me and said: Come, O Shayb. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand upon my chest, and Allah extracted Satan from my heart; and I raised my sight to him, while he was more beloved to me than my hearing and my sight, and than such-and-such; and he said to me: O Shayb, fight the disbelievers. Then he said: O Abbas, call the Muhajirun who pledged beneath the tree, and the Ansar who sheltered and helped. And I likened the bending of the Ansar toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only to cattle toward their calves, until there descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as if a thicket; he said: The spears of the Ansar were in my view more fearful for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than the spears of the disbelievers. Then he said: O Abbas, hand me of the pebbles. He said: And Allah made the mule understand his speech, and she lowered with him until her belly nearly touched the ground; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took of the gravel, blew in their faces, and said: Ruined be the faces; Ha Mim, they shall not be helped."
"7219","Narrated Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Rasibi from Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Hisab from Muhammad ibn Humran - Abu Bishr informed me - from Musafi ibn Shayba from his father Shayba, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the Kaba and prayed two rakas; and he saw pictures in it, and said: O Shayba, suffice Me these. And that weighed heavily upon Shayba; so a man of the people of Persia said to him: If you wish, I shall coat and smear them with saffron; and he did."
"7220","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ayyub, author of al-Maghazi, narrated to me - from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash from Thabit al-Thumali from Muhayyisa from Shayba ibn Uthman, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer in the mosque of al-Khayf, and he said: Three over which a believer's heart does not harbor deceit: sincerity of deed, sincere counsel to the leaders of the Muslims, and adhering to their community; for their supplication encompasses those behind them."
"7221","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Abdan ibn Ahmad, both saying: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi from al-Shaybani Wasil al-Ahdab from Shaqiq, who said: A man sent with me dirhams as a gift to the House; so I entered the House while Shayba was sitting on a chair; and I handed them to him; and he said: Is this yours? I said: No, and had it been mine I would not have brought it to you. He said: Since you have said that, Umar ibn al-Khattab sat in the seat of yours in which you are, and said: I shall not go out until I divide what is in the Kaba among the poor Muslims. I said: You will not do it. He said: I surely will; and why? I said: Because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw its place, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him; and they were more in need of this wealth than you, yet they did not move it; so he rose as he was and went out."
"7222","Narrated Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi from Qabisa ibn Uqba from Sufyan, and narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki from Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Sufyan al-Thawri from Wasil al-Ahdab from Abu Wail Shaqiq ibn Salama, who said: I sat with Shayba ibn Uthman in the Sacred Mosque, and he said to me: Umar sat with me in this seat of yours, and said: I have intended to leave in it neither yellow nor white but I shall divide it - meaning the Kaba. Shayba said: And I said to him: You had two companions who did not do it - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: They are the two men I follow."
"7223","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yazdad al-Tawwazi from Muhammad ibn Sulayman Luwayn from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Abd Allah ibn Zurara from Musab ibn Shayba from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you reaches a gathering, if room is made for him let him sit; otherwise let him look to the widest place he sees, and sit."
"7224","Shayba ibn Utba ibn Rabia ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf, Abu Hashim, maternal uncle of Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with him; his mother was Khunas bint Malik ibn Malik ibn al-Mudarrib Hujayr ibn Abd Muays ibn Amir ibn Luayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik; and he was one-eyed, his eye gouged on the day of Yarmuk, and he died in the time of Muawiya, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father from Muhammad ibn Shuayb ibn Shabur - Khalid ibn Dihqan narrated to me - and narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi and Musa ibn Sahl Abu Imran al-Jawni, both saying: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us from Sadaqa ibn Khalid from Khalid ibn Dihqan - Khalid Sabalan informed me - from Kuhayl ibn Harmala from Abu Hurayra, that he came until he stayed with Abu Kulthum al-Dawsi; and they discussed the middle prayer; and he said: We differed in it as you differed, while we were in the courtyard of the house of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and among us was the righteous man Abu Hashim ibn Utba ibn Rabia ibn Abd Shams; and he said: I know that for you. So he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), being bold with him; and he sought permission, entered to him, then came out to us, and informed us that it is Asr prayer."
"7225","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Muawiya ibn Amr from Zaida from Mansur from Shaqiq - Samura ibn Sahm narrated to us - who said: I stayed with Abu Hashim ibn Utba while he was wounded; and Muawiya visited him to console him; and he wept; and Muawiya, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: What makes you weep - pain distressing you? Or for this world? For its best has gone. He said: For neither at all, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrusted to me a trust, and I wish I had followed it; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps you will see wealth divided among people, and only a servant and a mount suffice you of gathering wealth. And I found, and I gathered."
"7226","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Sufyan from Mansur, and narrated Hafs ibn Umar ibn al-Sabbah al-Raqqi from Abu Hudhayfa from Sufyan from Mansur and al-Amash from Abu Wail, who said: Muawiya visited his maternal uncle Abu Hashim ibn Utba ibn Rabia while he was ill weeping; and he said: O maternal uncle, what makes you weep? Pain distressing you? Or greed for this world? He said: Neither at all, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrusted to me a trust which I did not take; and he said: O Abu Hashim, you will see wealth given to people, and only a servant and a mount in the path of Allah suffice you of all this world. And I see today I have gathered."
"7227","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nasr al-Qattani al-Hamdani from Hashim ibn al-Walid al-Harawi from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash from Asim from Abu Wail, who said: Muawiya visited his maternal uncle; and when he saw him he said: What is wrong with you - panic and greed for this world? He said: No, but I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Perhaps you will meet people preferring wealth, and only a house and a mount in the path of Allah suffice one of you of this world."
"7228","Narrated al-Hasan ibn al-Abbas al-Razi from Muhammad ibn Harun al-Razi from al-Walid ibn Salama al-Urdunni from Yazid ibn Hassan from his father, that Abu Hashim ibn Utba ibn Rabia had a mustache he tied behind his nape; and I said: What is with your mustache, when there has come from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about trimming the mustache what has come? He said: I had trimmed my mustache, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and he passed his hand over me, and said: When did you trim your mustache? I said: Just now. He said: Then do not trim it until you meet me. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died before I met him; so I shall never cut it until I meet him."
"7229","Shayba ibn Abi Kathir al-Ashjai. Narrated Masada ibn Sad al-Attar al-Makki from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Muhammad ibn Umar al-Waqidi - Salama ibn Umar narrated to me - from Umar ibn Shayba ibn Abi Kathir al-Ashjai from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Flushing of the face from nabidh scatters good deeds from it."
"7230","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Warraq al-Wasiti from Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani from Yahya ibn Umayr al-Madani, who said: Umar ibn Shayba ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me from his father, who said: I was playing with my wife, and it left a trace in my hand, and she died; and that was during the expedition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Tabuk; so I came to him and informed him of my wife whom I had struck in error; and he said: You shall not inherit her."
"7231","Narrated Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri from Yahya ibn Bukayr, who said: Shurahbil ibn Hasana - and he is Shurahbil ibn Hasana ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Muta ibn Amr, and he is a man of al-Ghawth; and Ibn al-Kalbi said: Shurahbil ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Muta ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ghitrif ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Jaththama ibn Malik ibn Biladim ibn Malik ibn Duhm ibn Sad ibn Yashkur ibn Manshir ibn al-Ghawth ibn Murr, brother of Tamim ibn Murr; and it is said he is of Kinda."
"7232","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani and Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqa, both saying: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyabi narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Fulayh from Musa ibn Uqba: In the naming of those who emigrated to the land of Abyssinia, and stayed there until he came after Badr: Shurahbil ibn Abd Allah, whose mother is Hasana."
"7233","Narrated Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj - Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us - who said: Shurahbil ibn Hasana, kunya Abu Abd Allah, died in the year seventeen or eighteen; his age sixty-seven; and he was a governor for Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"7234","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abu Salih from Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm from the hadith of al-Harith ibn Umayra, who said: Abu Ubayda, Shurahbil ibn Hasana and Abu Malik were all stabbed in plague on a single day."
"7235","What Shurahbil ibn Hasana narrated. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Muslim ibn Ibrahim from Hammam from Qatada and Matar al-Warraq from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, who said: Plague fell in Syria; and Amr ibn al-As addressed us, saying: This plague is filth; so flee from it into valleys and mountain passes. That reached Shurahbil ibn Hasana, and he said: Amr lied; I accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while Amr is more astray than his family's donkey; but it is your Lord's mercy, your Prophet's supplication, and the death of the righteous before you."
"7236","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, and narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Sulayman ibn Harb, both saying: Shuba narrated to us from Yazid ibn Khumayr from Shurahbil ibn Shafaa, who said: Plague fell in Syria; and Amr stood and said: This filth has fallen, so disperse from it. Ibn Hasana said: I accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while he is more astray than his family's donkey; it is but the mercy of your Lord, the supplication of your Prophet, and the death of the righteous before you; so gather for it, and do not disperse from it. That reached Amr, and he said: He spoke the truth."
"7237","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ruzayq al-Misri and Abdan ibn Ahmad, both saying: Abu al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh narrated to us from Rishdin ibn Sad from Musa ibn Ayyub al-Ghafiqi from Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman from Shurahbil ibn Hasana: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood in the two rakas of the prayer and did not sit until he finished his prayer, then he prostrated two prostrations, then he gave the salutation of peace."
"7238","Shurahbil ibn Aws al-Kindi. Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Abu al-Mughira and Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi, both saying: Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us from Abu al-Hasan Nimran ibn Mikhmar from Shurahbil ibn Aws al-Kindi, and he was of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When he drinks wine, flog him; if he drinks it a second time, flog him; if he drinks it a third time, flog him; and if he drinks it a fourth time, kill him."
"7239","Shurahbil al-Jufi. Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Abu Awn al-Ziyadi from Hammad ibn Yazid al-Minqari from Makhlad ibn Uqba ibn Shurahbil from his grandfather Shurahbil, who said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when there came to him a tall white bedouin, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, an old man with a raging fever driving him to the graves. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An old man with a raging fever; it is for him an expiation and a purification. He repeated it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeated it to him; he repeated it three times or four. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Since you refuse, it is as you say, and whatever Allah decrees will come to pass. He said: He did not reach the evening of the morrow except dead."
"7240","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Abu Awn al-Ziyadi from Hammad ibn Yazid al-Minqari from Makhlad ibn Uqba ibn Shurahbil from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever finds earning a livelihood difficult, upon him is Oman."
"7241","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from al-Fadl ibn Sahl al-Araj from Yunus ibn Muhammad from Hammad ibn Yazid - Makhlad ibn Uqba ibn Shurahbil narrated to me - from his grandfather Abd al-Rahman, from his father, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a growth on my palm, and I said: O Prophet of Allah, this growth has harmed me; it comes between me and the hilt of the sword so that I cannot grasp it, and between me and the rein of the mount. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come near me. So I drew near him, and he said: Open your hand. So I opened it. Then he said: Close it. So I closed it. He said: Come near me. So I drew near him. He said: Open it. So I opened it, and he breathed into my palm, then he placed his hand upon the growth, and he kept grinding it with his palm until he removed it from it, and I see no trace of it."
"7242","Shurahbil ibn al-Awar Abu Shimr al-Dibabi Dhu al-Jawshan. Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and Muadh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad, and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, and narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Iqal al-Harrani from Abu Jafar al-Nahshali, all saying: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us from his father, from Abu Ishaq, from Dhu al-Jawshan, a man of al-Dibab, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after he had finished with the people of Badr, with a young foal of my mare called al-Qarha, and I said: O Muhammad, I have brought you the son of al-Qarha for you to take. He said: I have no need of it, and if you wish I will settle you for it with the choicest of the coats of mail of Badr, I will do so. I said: I would not exchange it today for other than it. He said: I have no need of it. Then he said: O Dhu al-Jawshan, will you not become Muslim, so that you are among the first of this affair? I said: No. He said: Why? I said: I saw your people weary of you - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba said in his hadith: mock you. He said: How did the news of their deaths at Badr reach you? I said: It has reached me. He said: Is it a covenant with you? I said: Yes, that you will conquer the Kaba and dwell in it. He said: Perhaps if you live you will see that. He said: O Bilal, take the man's bag and provision him from the ajwa dates. When I turned away he said: He is indeed among the best horsemen of Banu Amir. He said: By Allah, I was with my family at al-Fawr when a rider came, and I said: From where? He said: From Makkah. I said: What did the people do? He said: By Allah, Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has conquered it and settled in it. I said: May my mother be bereaved of me; by Allah, had I become Muslim that day, then asked him for al-Hira, he would have granted it to me. Abu Muslim said: Musaddad said: It reached me from Ibn al-Mubarak: His name is Shurahbil, and he was only called Dhu al-Jawshan because he had a protruding chest."
"7243","Those named Sharahil. Sharahil ibn Murra al-Hamdani. Narrated Abu Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Wadii the judge and Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, both saying: Abada ibn Ziyad al-Asadi narrated to us from Qays ibn al-Rabi from Abu Ishaq from Abu al-Bakhtari from Hujayya ibn Adi, who said: I heard Sharahil ibn Murra saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Rejoice, O Ali, your life and your death are with me."
"7244","Those named Sharik. Sharik ibn Tariq ibn Sufyan, one of Banu Thalaba ibn Dhubyān ibn Nughayd ibn Rayth ibn Ghatafan. Narrated Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi from Abd Allah ibn Raja from Israil from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa from Sharik ibn Tariq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you will enter Paradise by a deed. They said: Not even you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Not even I, unless Allah envelops me from Him with mercy and bounty."
"7245","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Asad ibn Musa from Shayban Abu Muawiya from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa from Sharik ibn Tariq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None will enter Paradise by a deed. They said: Not even you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Not even I, unless Allah envelops me from Him with mercy and bounty."
"7246","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Jafar ibn Humayd from al-Walid ibn Abi Thawr from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa from Sharik ibn Tariq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: None of you will enter Paradise by a deed. They said: Not even you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Not even I, unless Allah envelops me with mercy."
"7247","Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qitrani from Kamil ibn Talha al-Jahdari from Abu Awana from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa from Sharik ibn Tariq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is none of you whom his deed will admit into Paradise. They said: Not even you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Not even I, unless Allah envelops me from Him with mercy."
"7248","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Asad ibn Musa from Shayban from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa from Sharik ibn Tariq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is none of you but has a devil. They said: Not even you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Not even I, except that Allah helped me against him, so he submitted."
"7249","Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qitrani from Kamil ibn Talha al-Jahdari from Abu Awana from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa from Sharik ibn Tariq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no one but has a devil. They said: And you? He said: And me, except that Allah helped me against him."
"7250","Sharik, a man of the Companions, not attributed to a tribe. Narrated Muhammad ibn Shuayb al-Asbahani from Hafs ibn Umar al-Mihraqani from Amir ibn Ibrahim from Yaqub al-Qummi from Anbasa from Isa ibn Jariya from Sharik, a man of the Companions, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever commits adultery, faith exits from him; and whoever drinks wine, neither compelled nor constrained, faith exits from him; and whoever plunders a plunder over which people fix their eyes, faith exits from him; and if he repents, Allah turns to him."
"7251","699 - Shakal ibn Humayd al-Absi. 7225 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sad ibn Aws al-Katib narrated, Bilal ibn Yahya al-Absi narrated to me, that Shutayr ibn Shakal informed him, from his father Shakal ibn Humayd, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, teach me a supplication of refuge with which I may seek refuge? He took my hand, then said: Say: I seek refuge in You from the evil of my self, and from the evil of my hearing, and from the evil of my sight, and from the evil of my tongue, and from the evil of my heart, and from my semen. Then he said to me: Memorize it. Sad said: And al-mani means his fluid."
"7252","700 - Shufayy ibn Mati al-Asbahi, and there is disagreement concerning his companionship. 7226 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated, from Thalabah ibn Muslim al-Khathami, from Ayyub ibn Bashir al-Ijli, from Shufayy ibn Mati al-Asbahi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Four persons torment the people of the Fire despite the torment they themselves are in; they rush about between scalding water and Hell, crying out for woe and destruction. Some of the people of the Fire say to others: What is the matter with these who torment us despite the torment we are in? He said: A man locked upon him is a chest of embers, a man dragging his intestines, a man whose mouth flows with pus and blood, and a man eating his own flesh. He said: It is said to the owner of the chest: What is the matter with the outcast who has tormented us despite the torment we are in? He says: The outcast died while around his neck were people's properties for which no settlement or payment is found. Then it is said to the one dragging his intestines: What is the matter with the outcast who has tormented us despite the torment we are in? It is said: The outcast would not care if urine got on him and would not wash it. Then it is said to the one whose mouth flows with pus and blood: What is the matter with the outcast who has tormented us despite the torment we are in? He says: The outcast used to eat people's flesh."
"7253","701 - Shibl ibn Mabad al-Madani. 7227 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, who said: Abu Bakrah, and Nafi, and Shibl ibn Mabad testified against al-Mughirah ibn Shubah that they looked upon him as one looks at the Kohl stick in the Kohl container. Then Ziyad came, and Umar said: A man has come who testifies only by truth. He said: I saw an ugly sight and obscenity. He said: So Umar flogged them the hadd punishment."
"7254","702 - Shayban Abu Yahya al-Ansari, grandfather of Abu Hubayrah. 7228 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated, from Ashath ibn Sawwar, from Yahya ibn Abbad, from his grandfather Shayban, that he went early to the mosque and sat by one of the chambers of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and heard his voice. He said: Abu Yahya. He said: Yes. He said: Enter. So he entered, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was eating breakfast, and said: Come to breakfast. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I intend to fast. He said: And I intend to fast; our muezzin has poor eyesight and called the adhan before dawn."
"7255","Shurayh ibn Abraha. 7229 - Muhammad ibn Nusayr al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Shadhakuni narrated, Abd al-Wahid ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated, Sharqi ibn al-Qutami narrated, from Amr ibn Qays, from Muhill ibn Wadaah, from Shurayh ibn Abraha, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) utter the takbir in the days of Tashriq from the Zuhr prayer on the Day of Sacrifice until he left Mina."
"7256","7230 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Karim al-Zafaran al-Askari narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs al-Shaybani narrated, Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Saadi Abu Dawud narrated, Abd al-Wahid ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated, Sharqi ibn Qutami narrated, from Amr ibn Qays, from Muhill ibn Wadaah, from Shurayh ibn Abraha, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uttering the takbir in the days of Tashriq until he left Mina, uttering takbir after every prayer."
"7257","Those named Shihab. 704 - Shihab, a man from the Companions, who used to reside in Egypt. 7231 - Muhammad ibn Muadh al-Halabi narrated to us, al-Qanabi narrated, Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated, from Salm ibn Abi al-Dhayyal, from Abu Sinan, a man from the people of Madinah, who heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah narrating, from Shihab - a man from the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who used to reside in Egypt - that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever conceals a fault of a believer, it is as if he gave life to a dead person."
"7258","705 - Shihab ibn al-Majnun, grandfather of Asim ibn Kulayb, and there is disagreement concerning his name; some said his name is Shubayb, and some said Shutayr. 7232 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Mualla ibn Asad al-Ammi narrated, Muhammad ibn Imran narrated, Abu Madan narrated, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was placing his hand on his thigh, pointing with the forefinger, and saying: O Turner of hearts, make my heart firm upon Your religion."
"7259","706 - Shubayb ibn Nuaym, not attributed. 7233 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Said narrated, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Rashid ibn Sad, from Shubayb ibn Nuaym, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umm Mildam eats flesh and drinks blood; its cold and its heat are from Hell."
"7260","707 - Shuayb ibn Amr, not attributed. 7234 - Abd Allah ibn al-Saqr al-Sukkari narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated, Salamah ibn Raja narrated, Aidh ibn Shurayh narrated, that he heard Anas ibn Malik, and Shuayb ibn Amr, and Najiyah ibn Amr saying: We saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dyeing his hair."
"7261","708 - Shatab al-Mamdud Abu Tawil. 7235 - Abu Zayd Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Hawti narrated to us, Abu al-Mughirah narrated, Safwan ibn Amr narrated, Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr narrated, from Abu Tawil Shatab al-Mamdud, that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: What do you think of a man who did all sins and left none of them, and in that did not leave any small or great need but committed it; is there repentance for him? He said: Have you embraced Islam? He said: As for me, I testify that there is no god but Allah, alone without partner, and that you are the Messenger of Allah. He said: Yes; do good deeds and leave evil deeds, and Allah will turn them all into good deeds for you. He said: And my treacheries and wicked deeds? He said: Yes. He said: Allah is Greatest, and he kept uttering takbir until he disappeared."
"7262","709 - Shuwayfi, not attributed. 7236 - Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Rasibi narrated to us, Abu Maysarah al-Nahawandi narrated, al-Walid ibn Salamah al-Harrani narrated, Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Shuwayfi narrated, from his father, from his grandfather Shuwayfi, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is not ashamed of what he says, or what is said to him, is of impure origin; his mother carried him in impurity."
"7263","710 - al-Sharid ibn Suwayd al-Thaqafi. What Amr ibn al-Sharid narrated from his father. 7237 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and al-Hasan ibn Sahl al-Mujawwiz al-Basri narrated to us, both saying: Abu Asim narrated, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Taifi, Amr ibn al-Sharid narrated, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated me behind him, and said: Will you not recite something of Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt for me? I said: Yes. He said: Go on. So I recited to him a hundred verses or rhymes; whenever I completed a verse or rhyme he said: Go on."
"7264","7238 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated, Sufyan narrated, Ibrahim ibn Maysarah narrated, Amr ibn al-Sharid informed me, from his father, who said: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Do you have anything of the poetry of Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt? I said: Yes. He said: Go on. So I recited a verse to him, and he kept saying: Go on, and I recited to him, until I had recited a hundred verses to him."
"7265","7239 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muammal ibn Hisham narrated, Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated, Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Do you have any poetry with you? I said: Yes. He said: Recite something to me. So I recited a verse to him, then fell silent. He said: Go on. So I recited a verse to him, then fell silent. He said: Go on. So I recited a verse to him, until I had recited a hundred, then I fell silent, and he fell silent."
"7266","7240 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, or Yaqub ibn Asim - Sufyan doubts - from al-Sharid, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man who had let his lower garment hang down, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Lift up your lower garment. The man said: O Messenger of Allah, I am bow-legged, my knees knock together. He said: Lift up your lower garment, and all of Allah's creation is good. That man was never seen thereafter except with his lower garment to the middle of his shins."
"7267","7241 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man trailing his lower garment, so he hastened to him, or hurried to him, and said: Lift up your lower garment and fear Allah. He said: I am bow-legged in the shins, my knees knock. He said: All of Allah's creation is good. He said: That man was never seen thereafter except with his lower garment to the middle of his shins, or reaching the middle of his shins. Asad ibn Musa did not mention in his hadith the doubt concerning Amr ibn al-Sharid and Yaqub ibn Asim."
"7268","7242 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Isa ibn Yunus narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was sitting thus, reclining on the fleshy part of his hand behind his back, and said: This is the sitting of those upon whom wrath fell."
"7269","7243 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Mindal narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man who had sat and reclined on his left hand, and said: This is the sitting of those upon whom wrath fell."
"7270","7244 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Riqashi narrated, (h) and Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to me, both saying: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, Abd Allah ibn Utbah ibn Urwah ibn Masud al-Thaqafi narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from al-Sharid, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When one of you drinks, then strike him; if he returns, then strike him; then if he returns, then strike him; then if he returns a fourth time, then kill him."
"7271","7245 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, (h) and Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated, both saying: Abu Ubaydah al-Haddad narrated, from Khalaf ibn Mihran Abu al-Rabi, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Salih ibn Dinar, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a sparrow frivolously, it will cry out to Allah on the Day of Resurrection, saying: O Lord, this servant of Yours killed me frivolously and did not kill me for benefit."
"7272","7246 - Ahmad ibn Ali al-Jarudi al-Asbahani narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Sufyan narrated, Khalid ibn Yazid al-Kahili narrated, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated, from Aban ibn Salih, from Ibn Dinar, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"7273","7247 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab al-Jumahi narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, (h) and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, (h) and Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman narrated, all saying: Sharik narrated, from Yala ibn Ata, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, that a leper came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pledge allegiance to him. So I came to him and mentioned it to him, and he said: Go to him and inform him that I have pledged allegiance to him, so let him return."
"7274","7248 - Khayr ibn Arafah al-Misri narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated, Ibn Lahiah narrated, from Imran ibn Rabiah al-Sadafi, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the two leather socks."
"7275","7249 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated, from Wabr ibn Abi Dulaylah, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The delay of one who can pay makes lawful his honor and his punishment."
"7276","7250 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Wabar ibn Abi Dulaylah, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The delay of one who can pay makes lawful his honor and his punishment. Sufyan said: Makes his honor lawful means that he may complain against him, and his punishment means his imprisonment. And the correct form is Wabr ibn Abi Dulaylah with dammah on the dal, and al-Numan ibn Abd al-Salam narrated it from Sufyan and said Amr: from Wabr ibn Abi Dalilah with fathah on the dal."
"7277","7251 - Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated, al-Numan ibn Abd al-Salam narrated, (h) and al-Miqdam ibn Dawud al-Misri narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated, Ubayd Allah ibn Hurmuz narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi al-Fityan, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking about something concerning camels, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Slaughter its fat one, and mount its lean one, and milk on the day of water, and enter Paradise in peace."
"7278","7252 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated, al-Qasim ibn Rashdin ibn Umayr narrated, Makhramah ibn Bukayr narrated, from his father, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: A woman was stoned at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When we finished with her, I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: We have stoned this wicked woman. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stoning is expiation for what she did."
"7279","7253 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, (h) and Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, both saying: Abu Usamah narrated, from Husayn al-Muallim, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The neighbor has more right to his adjoining property."
"7280","7254 - Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman narrated, from Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak, from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yala, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The neighbor has more right to his adjoining property. I said to Umar: What is al-saqab? He said: Neighborhood."
"7281","7255 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Abdah ibn Abd Allah al-Saffar narrated, Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated, from Yunus ibn al-Harith al-Taifi, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to decree preemption in the well, the house, and the garden before it is divided."
"7282","7256 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, Abu Hatim al-Razi narrated, al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated, from Yaqub ibn Ata, Amr ibn al-Sharid informed me, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The neighbor has more right to his adjoining property as long as he needs it."
"7283","Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman from al-Sharid. 7257 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from al-Sharid ibn Suwayd al-Thaqafi, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother bequeathed that a slave be freed on her behalf, and I have a black maidservant. He said: Call her. So she came, and he said: Who is your Lord? She said: Allah. He said: Who am I? She said: The Messenger of Allah. He said: Free her, for she is a believer."
"7284","Ata ibn Abi Rabah from al-Sharid. 7258 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibrahim ibn Umar al-Makki, who said: I heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah saying: al-Sharid came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of the Conquest and said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed that if Allah grants you the conquest of Makkah, I would pray in Bayt al-Maqdis. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Here, then pray - three times."
"7285","Amr ibn Rafi from al-Sharid. 7259 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Abd al-Warith ibn Abd al-Samad narrated, my father narrated to me, Abu Yunus al-Qushayri narrated to me, Simak ibn Harb narrated, that Amr ibn Rafi narrated to him - and he was a freedman of Abu Sufyan - that al-Sharid, while he was walking between Mina and al-Shib during the Hajj of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which he performed, said: And there was the stepping of a camel behind me, so I turned and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He recognized me and said: Al-Sharid? I said: Yes. He said: Shall I not carry you behind me, O Sharid? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. I said: I have neither fatigue nor weariness, but I seek blessing in my mount with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he said: O Sharid, do you have anything of the poetry of Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt? I said: I am the most retentive of people. He said: Give. So I recited to him; when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent I fell silent, and when he said: Go on, I recited to him, until when that grew long, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said at that point: With Allah is the knowledge of Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt."
"7286","Chapter of Sad. Those named Sakhr. 711 - Sakhr ibn Harb ibn Umayyah ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf Abu Sufyan. Reports of Abu Sufyan and his death. 7260 - Muhammad ibn Ali Fustuqah informed us, Dawud ibn Rashid narrated, from al-Haytham ibn Adi, who said: Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, may Allah have mercy on him, died nine years into the reign of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and the sight of Abu Sufyan ibn Harb had been taken away."
"7287","7261 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated, from al-Waqidi, who said: And in it died Abu Sufyan Sakhr ibn Harb, at eighty-eight years old, meaning the year thirty-one."
"7288","7262 - Bakr ibn Ahmad ibn Muqbil narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Shabib narrated, Yaqub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri narrated, Mujashi ibn Amr al-Asadi narrated, Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with him, from Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, that Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt was at Ghazzah - or he said: at Iliya - and when we returned, Umayyah said to me: O Abu Sufyan, shall we go ahead of the company and talk? I said: Yes. So we did. He said to me: O Abu Sufyan, which of them is on the side of Utbah ibn Rabiah? I said: Which of them is on the side of Utbah ibn Rabiah? He said: Noble on both sides, and avoids wrongs and forbidden things? I said: Yes. He said: And an aged chief? I said: An aged chief. He said: Age and nobility have impaired him. I said to him: You lie; he only increases in nobility as he increases in age. He said: O Abu Sufyan, it is a word I have not heard anyone say to me since I became Christian; do not hasten against me until I inform you. He said: Go on. He said: I used to find in my books a prophet to be sent from this Harrah of ours, and I thought - rather I did not doubt - that I was he. When I studied with the people of knowledge, he was from Banu Abd Manaf. I looked among Banu Abd Manaf and found none fit for this matter except Utbah ibn Rabiah. When you informed me of his age, I knew it was not he, since he had passed forty and no revelation had come to him. Abu Sufyan said: Time passed, and revelation came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I went out with a party of Quraysh intending Yemen for trade, and passed by Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt, and said to him mockingly: O Umayyah, the Prophet you were awaiting has come forth. He said: Indeed it is true, so follow him. I said: What prevents you from following him? He said: Nothing prevents me from following him except shyness before the women of Thaqif, for I used to tell them that I was he, then they would see me a follower of a youth from Banu Abd Manaf! Then Umayyah said: And it is as if, O Abu Sufyan, if you oppose him, you will be tied as a kid is tied until you are brought to him and he judges concerning you as he wishes."
"7289","7263 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated, Ibn Lahiah narrated, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, who said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with twelve thousand from the Muhajirun, the Ansar, Ghifar, Aslam, Muzaynah, Juhaynah, and Banu Sulaym, and they led the horses until they camped at Marr al-Zahran, while Quraysh did not know of them. So they sent Abu Sufyan and Hakim ibn Hizam to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Gain protection for us from him, or inform him of war. So Abu Sufyan ibn Harb and Hakim ibn Hizam went out and met Budayl ibn Warqa, and took him along until, when they were at al-Arak near Makkah - and that was in the evening - they saw the tents and the army and heard the neighing of horses. That alarmed them and they feared it, and said: These are Banu Kab whom war has stirred. Budayl said: These are more than Banu Kab; their arraying has not reached this. Is Hawazin pasturing our land? By Allah, we do not know this either; this is like the throng of pilgrims. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had sent cavalry ahead of him seizing the spies, and Khuzaah were on the road letting none pass. When Abu Sufyan and his companions entered the Muslim camp, the cavalry seized them under the night and brought them fearing killing. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, rose against Abu Sufyan and struck his neck, and the people clung to him, and they brought him out to enter with him upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The guard held him back from reaching the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he feared killing. Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, had been his close friend in the Jahiliyyah, so he called out at the top of his voice: Will you not take me to Abbas? So he came to him and warded off from him, and asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to entrust him to him, and walked in his place among the people. Abbas rode with him under the night and took him through the army of the people until they all saw him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had said to Abu Sufyan when he struck his neck: By Allah, you shall not come near the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until you die. So he sought help from Abbas and said: I am to be killed. So he protected him from the people lest they plunder him. When he saw the multitude of the army and their obedience, he said: I have never seen like tonight a gathering of a people. Abbas rescued him from their hands and said: You will be killed if you do not embrace Islam and testify that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. He kept wanting to say what Abbas commanded him, but his tongue would not utter. He spent the night with Abbas. As for Hakim ibn Hizam and Budayl ibn Warqa, they entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and embraced Islam, and he began asking them about the people of Makkah. When the call for the dawn prayer was made, the people bustled, and Abu Sufyan was alarmed and said: O Abbas, what do you want? He said: They are the Muslims preparing to attend upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abbas took him out, and when Abu Sufyan saw them going to prayer, bowing and prostrating in their prayer when he prostrated, he said: O Abbas, do they do nothing he commands but obey? Abbas said: If he forbade them food and drink they would obey him. He said: O Abbas, speak to him concerning your people: is there pardon from him for them? Abbas took Abu Sufyan until he brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abbas said: O Messenger of Allah, this is Abu Sufyan. Abu Sufyan said: O Muhammad, I sought help from my god and sought help from your god; by Allah, never did I meet you but you overcame me. Had my god been true and your god false, I would have overcome you. So he testified that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. Abbas said: O Messenger of Allah, I wish you would permit me to go to your people, to warn them of what has come and call them to Allah and His Messenger. So he permitted him. Abbas said: How shall I address them, O Messenger of Allah? Clarify for me a guarantee in which they may trust. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say to them: Whoever testifies that there is no god but Allah alone without partner, and testifies that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and withholds his hand, is safe; and whoever sits at the Kaaba and lays down his weapon is safe; and whoever locks his door upon him is safe. Abbas said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is our cousin, and I wish he would return with me, and that you would single him out with an honor. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe. Abu Sufyan kept asking him for understanding, and the house of Abu Sufyan is at the upper part of Makkah. And he said: Whoever enters the house of Hakim ibn Hizam and withholds his hand is safe - and the house of Hakim ibn Hizam is at the lower part of Makkah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mounted Ali upon his white mule which Dihyah al-Kalbi had gifted to him. Abbas set out with Abu Sufyan riding behind him. When Abbas had gone, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent after him, saying: Overtake Abbas and bring him back to me, and informed them of what he feared for him. The messenger overtook him, and Abbas disliked returning, saying: Does the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fear to send back Abu Sufyan desiring few people, so that he disbelieves after his Islam? He said: Detain him. So he detained him. Abu Sufyan said: Is it treachery, O Banu Hashim? Abbas said: We do not betray, but I have some need concerning you. He said: What is it, that I may fulfill it for you? He said: Repeat it when Khalid ibn al-Walid and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam come upon you. Abbas halted at the strait below al-Arak near Mina, and Abu Sufyan had retained his discourse from him. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent the cavalry crossing, some behind others, and divided the cavalry into two halves: he sent al-Zubayr and reinforced him with Khalid with the army from Aslam, Ghifar, and Qudaah. Abu Sufyan said: Is this the Messenger of Allah, O Abbas? He said: No, but Khalid ibn al-Walid. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Sad ibn Ubadah before him in a squadron of the Ansar. He said: Today is the day of battle; today the sanctuary is made lawful. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered with the squadron of faith from the Muhajirun and the Ansar. When Abu Sufyan saw many faces he did not know, he said: O Messenger of Allah, you have multiplied then, or chosen these faces over your people? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You and your people did that; these believed me when you belied me, and aided me when you expelled me. With the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day were al-Aqra ibn Habis, Abbas ibn Mirdas, and Uyaynah ibn Badr al-Fazari. When he saw them around the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Who are these, O Abbas? He said: This is the squadron of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with it is red death; these are the Muhajirun and the Ansar. He said: Go on, O Abbas; I have never seen like today soldiers or a gathering. Al-Zubayr marched with the people until he halted at al-Hajun, and Khalid pressed on until he entered from the lower part of Makkah. The rabble of Banu Bakr met him and fought them, and Allah routed them, and they were killed at al-Hazwarah until they entered the houses. A party of them ascended on horseback to al-Khandamah, and the Muslims followed them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered among the last of the people, and a caller called: Whoever locks his house upon him and withholds his hand is safe. Abu Sufyan called out in Makkah: Embrace Islam and you will be safe. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, restrained them from Abbas. Hind bint Utbah came forward and seized the beard of Abu Sufyan, then called: O Ghalib, kill this foolish old man. He said: Let go my beard; I swear to you, if you do not embrace Islam your neck will be struck. Woe to you, he has come to us with truth; enter your couch - I think he said: and be silent."
"7290","7264 - Abu Shuayb al-Harrani narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Nufayli narrated, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah ibn Masud, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marched on and left Abu Ruhm Kulthum ibn Husayn al-Ghifari in charge of Madinah, and went out on the tenth of Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted, and the people fasted with him, until when he was at al-Kadid between Usfan and Amaj he broke his fast. Then he marched on until he camped at Marr al-Zahran with ten thousand Muslims from Muzaynah and Sulaym, and in every tribe a number, and Islam; the Muhajirun and the Ansar all accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), none of them stayed behind. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) camped at Marr al-Zahran, and news had been blinded from Quraysh - no news of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached them, nor did they know what he would do - on that night Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, Hakim ibn Hizam, and Budayl ibn Warqa went out seeking news and waiting whether they would find news or hear of it. Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib had already come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) partway, and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib and Abd Allah ibn Abi Umayyah ibn al-Mughirah had met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between Makkah and Madinah and sought entry upon him. Umm Salamah spoke to him concerning them, saying: O Messenger of Allah, your cousin, your aunt's son, and your in-law. He said: I have no need of them; as for my cousin, he violated my honor, and as for my aunt's son and in-law, he is the one who said to me at Makkah what he said. When that was conveyed to them - and with Abu Sufyan was a young son of his - he said: By Allah, he shall either permit me or I shall take the hand of this son of mine, then we shall go forth in the land until we die of thirst and hunger. When that reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he softened toward them, then permitted them, and they entered and embraced Islam. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) camped at Marr al-Zahran, al-Abbas said: O morning of Quraysh! By Allah, if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enters Makkah by force before they seek protection from him, it will be the destruction of Quraysh till the end of time. He said: So I sat upon the white mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and went out upon it until I came to al-Arak. I said: Perhaps I shall meet some woodcutter, or a milkman, or one in need coming to Makkah, to inform them of the place of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so they may come out to him and seek protection from him before he enters upon them by force. He said: By Allah, as I was riding upon it seeking what I had gone out for, I heard the speech of Abu Sufyan and Budayl ibn Warqa disputing, Abu Sufyan saying: I have never seen like today fires nor an army. Budayl was saying: By Allah, these are the fires of Khuzaah stirred by war. Abu Sufyan was saying: Khuzaah - by Allah, too lowly and mean for these to be their fires and army. So I recognized his voice and said: O Abu Hanzalah. He recognized my voice and said: Abu al-Fadl? I said: Yes. He said: What is the matter with you - may my father and mother be ransom for you. I said: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, this is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the people; O morning of Quraysh, by Allah. He said: What is the stratagem - may my father and mother be ransom for you? He said: I said: By Allah, if he captures you he will strike your neck, so ride with me upon this mule until I bring you to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and seek protection for you. He said: So he rode behind me and his two companions returned. I moved with him; whenever I passed a fire of the Muslims, they said: Who is this? When they saw the mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they said: The uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon his mule - until I passed the fire of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: Who is this? And rose toward me. When he saw Abu Sufyan on the rump of the mule, he said: Abu Sufyan, enemy of Allah! Praise be to Allah who has empowered over you without pact or covenant. Then he hastened toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I spurred the mule and outpaced him as a slow beast outpaces a slow man. I dismounted from the mule and entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Umar entered and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is Abu Sufyan; Allah has empowered over him without pact or covenant, so let me strike his neck. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have granted him protection. Then I sat by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and took hold of his head, saying: By Allah, no man shall converse with him tonight besides me. When Umar persisted concerning him, I said: Gently, O Umar! By Allah, had he been from the men of Banu Adi ibn Kab you would not have said this, but you knew he is a man from the men of Banu Abd Manaf. He said: Gently, O Abbas! By Allah, your Islam on the day you embraced Islam was dearer to me than the Islam of al-Khattab had he embraced Islam, and that is only because I knew your Islam was dearer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than the Islam of al-Khattab. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take him to your lodging, O Abbas, and when morning comes bring him to me. So I took him to my lodging and he spent the night with me. In the morning I brought him early to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him, he said: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, has it not come time for you to know that there is no god but Allah? He said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, how generous and joining you are! By Allah, I thought that had there been another with Allah, it would have availed me something. He said: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, has it not come time for you to know that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, how forbearing, generous, and joining you are! By Allah, there remained something of this in myself until now. Al-Abbas said: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, embrace Islam and testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah before your neck is struck. He said: So he testified the testimony of truth and embraced Islam. I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a man who loves this pride, so grant him something. He said: Yes; whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe, and whoever locks his door is safe, and whoever enters the mosque is safe. When he went to leave, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abbas, detain him at the strait of the valley by the nose of the mountain until the armies of Allah pass by him and he sees them. He said: So I took him out until I detained him where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to detain him. He said: The tribes passed by him upon their banners; whenever a tribe passed he said: Who are these? I would say: Sulaym. He would say: What have I to do with Sulaym? He said: Then a tribe would pass. He would say: Who are these? I would say: Muzaynah. He would say: What have I to do with Muzaynah? Until the tribes had passed; no tribe passed but he said: Who are these? I would say: Banu so-and-so. He would say: What have I to do with Banu so-and-so. Until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed in the green-clad squadron containing the Muhajirun and the Ansar, of whom nothing could be seen but the eyes. He said: Glory be to Allah! Who are these, O Abbas? I said: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the Muhajirun and the Ansar. He said: None has power or strength against these. By Allah, O Abu al-Fadl, the kingdom of your nephew has become great this morning. I said: O Abu Sufyan, it is prophethood. He said: Then yes indeed. I said: Safety to your people. He said: So he went out until, when he came to them, he cried at the top of his voice: O company of Quraysh, this is Muhammad come to you with what you have no power against. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe. His wife Hind bint Utbah rose to him and seized his mustache, saying: Kill the fat black one; what an evil scout of a people. He said: Woe to you, let not this one deceive you concerning yourselves, for there has come what you have no power against. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe. They said: Woe to you, what use is your house to us? He said: And whoever locks his door is safe, and whoever enters the mosque is safe. So the people dispersed to their houses and to the mosque."
"7291","7265 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Musa al-Ansari narrated, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Muhammad ibn Ishaq."
"7292","7266 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, Thabit informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Rabah, who said: We came as a delegation to Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, and among us was Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. Each would prepare food one day and invite the other. I said: O Abu Hurayrah, today is my day. He came before the food was ready, so I said: O Abu Hurayrah, narrate to us something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until our food arrives. He said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of the Conquest, and he said: O Abu Hurayrah, call the Ansar for me. So I called them and they came hurrying. He said: Do you see the rabble of people? They said: Yes. He said: When you meet them tomorrow, reap them with reaping, then your appointment is al-Safa. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Khalid ibn al-Walid over one flank, and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam over the other flank, may Allah be pleased with him, and appointed Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah over those on foot in the belly of the valley. When the people came we met them, and none advanced but they laid him to rest, and conquest was granted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He came and ascended al-Safa, and the Ansar came and surrounded al-Safa, and Abu Sufyan came and said: O Messenger of Allah, the picked multitude of Quraysh has been made lawful; there is no Quraysh after today. He said: Whoever locks his door is safe, and whoever lays down weapons is safe, and whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe."
"7293","7267 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Shabbabah narrated, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated, Thabit al-Bunani narrated, from Abd Allah ibn Rabah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he entered Makkah on the Day of the Conquest, Abu Sufyan came and said: O Messenger of Allah, Quraysh has been made lawful; there is no Quraysh after today. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever locks his door is safe, and whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe. And he mentioned the like of the hadith of Hammad ibn Salamah."
"7294","7268 - Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan al-Baghdadi narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Bishr al-Bajali narrated, al-Hakam ibn Abd al-Malik narrated, from Qatadah, from Anas, who said: When we were at Sarif, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Abu Sufyan is near you, so beware of him. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Embrace Islam, O Abu Sufyan. He said: O Messenger of Allah, my people, my people. He said: Your people - whoever locks his door is safe. He said: Grant me something. He said: Whoever enters your house is safe."
"7295","What Abu Sufyan Sakhr ibn Harb narrated. 7269 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah informed me, from Ibn Abbas, Abu Sufyan ibn Harb narrated to me, from his mouth to my ear, who said: I went out during the truce that was between us and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: While I was in Syria, a letter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought to Heraclius. Dihyah al-Kalbi had brought it and handed it to the chief of Busra, who handed it to Heraclius. Heraclius said: Is there here a man from the people of this man who claims to be a prophet? They said: Yes. He said: So I was called among a group of Quraysh, and we entered upon Heraclius and were seated before him. He said: Which of you is nearest in kinship to this man who claims to be a prophet? Abu Sufyan said: I said: I am. So they seated me before him and seated my companions behind me. Then he called his interpreter and said: Tell them: I shall ask about this man who claims to be a prophet; if he lies to me, then belie him. Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, had it not been that lying would be attributed to me, I would have lied. Then he said to his interpreter: Ask him: How is his lineage among you? He said: He is among us of lineage. He said: Was any of his fathers a king? I said: No. He said: Did you accuse him of lying before he said what he said? I said: No. He said: Do the nobles of people follow him, or their weak? He said: I said: Rather their weak. He said: Do they increase or decrease? I said: No, rather they increase. He said: Does anyone among them apostatize from his religion after entering it, disliking it? I said: No. He said: Did you fight him? I said: Yes. He said: How was your fighting him? I said: War between us and him is alternate; he gains from us and we gain from him. He said: Does he betray? I said: No, and we are in a truce with him; we do not know what he will do in it. He said: By Allah, he gave me no opening for a word into which I could insert anything except this. He said: Did anyone before him say this saying? I said: No. He said to his interpreter: Tell him: I asked you about his lineage, and you claimed he is among you of lineage, and thus are messengers sent among the lineages of their people. I asked you whether any of his fathers was a king, and you claimed not; I said: Had any of his fathers been a king, I would say: A man seeking the kingdom of his fathers. I asked you about his followers, whether their weak or their nobles; you said: Rather their weak, and they are the followers of messengers. I asked you whether you accused him of lying before he said what he said; you claimed not, and I knew he would not abandon lying upon people then go and lie upon Allah. I asked you whether anyone among them apostatizes from his religion after entering it, disliking it; you claimed not, and thus is faith when it mixes with the cheerfulness of hearts. I asked you whether they increase or decrease; you claimed they increase, and thus is faith until it is complete. I asked you whether you fought him; you claimed you fought him, and war between you and him is alternate, he gains from you and you gain from him, and thus are messengers tried, then theirs is the outcome. I asked you whether he betrays; you claimed he does not betray, and thus are messengers: they do not betray. I asked you whether anyone before him said this saying; you claimed not. I said: Had anyone before him said this saying, I would say: A man following a saying said before him. Then he said: What does he command you? I said: He commands us with prayer, zakat, kinship, and chastity. He said: If what you say concerning him is true, he is a prophet. I knew he would come forth, but I did not think he was from you. Had I known I could reach him, I would love to meet him; had I been with him, I would wash his feet, and his kingdom shall reach what is beneath my feet. Then he called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and read it; in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Heraclius, chief of Rome: Peace upon whoever follows guidance. As for what follows: I call you with the call of Islam. Embrace Islam and you will be safe; embrace Islam and Allah will give you your reward twice. If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin. And: O people of the Book, come to a word equal between us and you - to His saying - that we are Muslims. When he finished reading the letter, voices rose with him and clamor increased, and we were ordered out. I said to my companions when we went out: The affair of the son of Abu Kabshah has become great; indeed the king of Banu al-Asfar fears him. I remained certain of the affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would prevail, until Allah brought Islam upon me. Al-Zuhri said: So Heraclius called the nobles of Rome and gathered them in a house of theirs, saying: O company of Rome, shall you have prosperity and guidance forever, and your kingdom remain for you? He said: They rushed like wild donkeys toward the doors and found them locked. He called them back: I only tested your firmness upon your religion, and I have seen from you what I loved. So they prostrated to him and were pleased with him."
"7296","7270 - Harun ibn Kamil al-Sarraj al-Misri narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated, Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, that Abd Allah ibn Abbas informed him, that Abu Sufyan ibn Harb ibn Umayyah informed him, that Heraclius sent for him among a party of Quraysh, who were merchants in Syria during the truce in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had made a truce with Abu Sufyan and the disbelievers of Quraysh. They came to him at Iliya, and he called them into a مجلس with the wise men of Rome around him. Then he called his interpreter and said: Tell them: Which of them is nearest in kinship to this man who claims to be a prophet? Abu Sufyan said: I am the nearest of them to him in kinship. He said: Come near me, and they brought his companions near, placing them at his back. Then he said to his interpreter: Tell them: I shall ask this one about this man; if he lies to me, then belie him. Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, had it not been for shame that they would attribute lying to me, I would have lied about him. Then the first thing he asked me about, he said: How is his lineage among you? I said: He is among us of lineage. He said: Did anyone among you say this saying before him? I said: No. He said: Was any of his fathers a king? I said: No. He said: Did their nobles follow him or their weak? I said: Rather their weak. He said: Do they increase or decrease? I said: Rather they increase. He said: Is there anyone among them who apostatizes in displeasure with his religion after entering it? I said: No. He said: Did you accuse him of lying? I said: No. He said: Does he betray? I said: No, and we are in a truce with him; we do not know what he will do in it. He said: He gave me no opening for a word into which I could insert anything except this word. He said: Did you fight him? He said: I said: Yes. He said: How was your fighting him? I said: War between us and him is alternate rounds; he gains from us and we gain from him. He said: What does he command you with? I said: He says: Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and leave what your fathers used to worship, and he commands prayer, charity, chastity, and kinship. He said: Then he said to his interpreter: I asked you about his lineage, and you claimed he is among you of lineage, and thus are messengers sent among the lineage of their people. I asked you whether anyone among you said this saying before him, and you claimed not. I said: Had anyone before him said this saying, I would say: A man following a saying said before him. I asked you whether any of his fathers was a king, and you claimed not. I said: Had there been a king among his fathers, I would say: He seeks the kingdom of his fathers. I asked you whether you accused him of lying before he said what he said, and you claimed not. I know he would not abandon lying upon people and lie upon Allah. I asked you whether the nobles of people followed him or their weak, and you claimed that their weak are those who followed him, and they are the followers of messengers. I asked you whether they increase or decrease, and you claimed they increase, and thus is faith until it is complete. I asked you whether anyone among them apostatizes in displeasure with his religion after entering it, and you claimed not, and thus is faith when it mixes with the cheerfulness of hearts. I asked you whether he betrays, and you claimed not, and thus are messengers: they do not betray. I asked you how your fighting him was, and you claimed war between you is alternate rounds, and thus are messengers tried, then theirs is the outcome. I asked you about what he commands you with, and you claimed he commands you to worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, forbids you the worship of idols, and commands you with prayer, charity, chastity, and kinship. If what you say concerning him is true, he will soon possess the place of these two feet of mine. He is a prophet. I knew he would come forth, but I did not think he was from you. Had I known I could reach him, I would seek to meet him; had I been with him, I would wash his feet. Then he called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which Dihyah al-Kalbi had brought to the chief of Busra, who handed it to Heraclius. He read it; in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad, servant of Allah and His Messenger, to Heraclius, chief of Rome. Peace upon whoever follows guidance. As for what follows: I call you with the call of Islam. Embrace Islam and you will be safe; embrace Islam and Allah will give you your reward twice. If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin - al-Layth said: The Arisiyyun are the tithe-collectors - and: O people of the Book, come to a word equal between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, associate nothing with Him, and take none of us others as lords besides Allah; if they turn away, say: Bear witness that we are Muslims. Abu Sufyan said: When he said what he said and finished reading the letter, there was much clamor with him, voices rose, and we went out. I said to my companions when we went out: The affair of the son of Abu Kabshah has risen; indeed the king of Banu al-Asfar fears him. I remained certain he would prevail until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, brought Islam upon me. Ibn Natur, the governor of Iliya and Heraclius's bishop over the Christians of Syria, used to narrate: That Heraclius, when he came to Iliya, awoke one morning troubled in spirit. Some of his patricians said to him: We have disapproved of your appearance. Heraclius was a diviner who looked at the stars. He said to them when they asked him: I saw tonight, when I looked at the stars, that the king of circumcision has appeared. He said: Who among these nations circumcises? He said: The Jews circumcise. He said: Let not their affair concern you; write to the cities of your kingdom to kill whoever among the Jews is in them. While they were upon that, a man sent by the king of Ghassan came to Heraclius informing him of the emergence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), before the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached him. He said: Go and see whether he is circumcised or not. They looked at him and informed him that he was circumcised. He asked him about the Arabs, and he informed him that they circumcise. Heraclius said: This is the king of this nation. Then Heraclius wrote to a companion of his at Rome, his peer in knowledge. Heraclius marched to Hims, and did not leave Hims until his companion's letter came, agreeing with Heraclius's view concerning the emergence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and that he is a prophet. Heraclius permitted the nobles of Rome into a hall of his at Hims, then ordered the doors locked. Then he looked down upon them and said: O company of Rome, shall you have prosperity and guidance, and your kingdom remain? Follow this prophet. They rushed like wild donkeys toward the doors and found them locked. When Heraclius saw that and despaired of their faith, he said: Bring them back to me. He said: What I said to you just now, I said only to test your firmness upon your religion. I have seen from you what I love. So they prostrated to him and were pleased with him."
"7297","7271 - Abu Shuayb Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Nufayli narrated, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah ibn Masud, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, Abu Sufyan ibn Harb narrated to me, who said: We were a people of merchants, and the war between us and the Messenger of Allah had confined us until our wealth perished. When the truce came between us and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we did not feel safe concerning our wealth, so I went out with a group of Quraysh heading to Syria, wherein was our trade. I arrived when Heraclius had overcome those Persians who had opposed him, expelled them from it, and recovered his great cross which they had taken from him. When that reached him from them, and it reached him that his cross had been rescued for him - and Hims was his residence - he went out from it on foot giving thanks to Allah when He restored to him what He restored, to pray in Bayt al-Maqdis. The road was spread for him with carpets, and sweet basil strewn upon it. When he reached Iliya and completed his prayer therein, with his patricians and the bishops of Rome with him, he said: And there came to him the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Dihyah ibn Khalifah al-Kalbi: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad the Messenger of Allah to Heraclius, chief of Rome: Peace upon whoever follows guidance. As for what follows: Embrace Islam and you will be safe; embrace Islam and Allah will give you your reward twice. If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the tillers. Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: Ibn Shihab said: The bishop of the Christians narrated to me, saying: I met him in the time of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, claiming to me that he witnessed that of the affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the affair of Heraclius and understood it. He said: When the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him with Dihyah, he took it and placed it between his thigh and his side. He said: Then he wrote to a man at Rome who read of Hebrew what he read, mentioning to him his affair, describing to him his matter and the news of what he brought. He said: The companion at Rome wrote to him that he is the Prophet we were awaiting, no doubt in it; so follow him and believe him. Heraclius ordered the patricians of Rome, and they were gathered for him in the hall of his kingdom. He ordered it and its doors were barred upon them. Then he looked down upon them from an upper chamber, fearing them for himself, saying: O company of Rome, I have gathered you for news: There has come to me the letter of this man calling me to his religion. By Allah, he is the man we were awaiting and find in our book. Come, let us follow him and believe him, so our world and hereafter remain safe for us. They snorted with one man's snort, then rushed to the doors of the hall to leave, and found them locked against them. He said: Bring them back to me - fearing them for himself - and they were brought back to him. He said: O company of Rome, I said to you this saying I said only to see how firm you are upon your religion concerning this new matter. I have seen from you what pleases me. So they fell prostrating to him, and he ordered the doors of the hall opened for them, and they left."
"7298","7272 - Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uzayz narrated, Salamah ibn Rawh narrated, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah informed me, that Abd Allah ibn Abbas informed him, saying: Abu Sufyan ibn Harb narrated to me that he was in Syria among men of Quraysh who had come as merchants, during the truce that was between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the disbelievers of Quraysh. Abu Sufyan said: The messenger of Caesar came, and took me and my companions until we arrived at Iliya. We entered upon him; he was sitting in his royal مجلس wearing the crown, with the nobles of Rome around him. He said to his interpreter: Ask them: Which of them is nearest in kinship to this man who claims to be a prophet? Abu Sufyan said: I said: I am the nearest of them to him in kinship. He said: What kinship is between you and him? I said: A cousin of mine - Abu Sufyan said: And there was none in the party that day from Banu Abd Manaf besides me. Caesar said: Bring him near me. Then he ordered my companions placed behind my back by my shoulder. Then Caesar said to his interpreter: Tell his companions: I shall ask this one about the man who claims to be a prophet; if he lies, then belie him. Abu Sufyan said: Had it not been for shame that day that my companions would attribute lying to me, I would have lied about him when he asked me, but I was ashamed they would attribute lying to me, so I told him truthfully. Then Caesar said to his interpreter: Tell him: How is the lineage of this man among you? I said: He is among us of lineage. He said: Did anyone among you ever say this saying? I said: No. He said: Did you accuse him of lying before he said what he said? I said: No. He said: Was anyone of his fathers a king? I said: No. He said: Did the nobles of people follow him or their weak? I said: Rather their weak. He said: Do they increase or decrease? I said: Rather they increase. He said: Does anyone among them apostatize in displeasure with his religion after entering it? I said: No. He said: Does he betray? I said: We are in a truce with him now, and we fear that. Abu Sufyan said: He gave me no opening for a word into which I could insert something disparaging him, fearing it would be attributed from me, besides it. He said: Did you fight him, and did he fight you? He said: Yes. He said: How is his war and your war? I said: Fortunes alternate; one time against us, another time for us against him. He said: What does he command you with? I said: He commands us to worship Allah alone and associate nothing with Him, forbids us to worship what our fathers worshipped, and commanded us with prayer, charity, chastity, fulfilling the covenant, and rendering trusts. He said: Then he said to his interpreter, when I said that to him: Tell him: I asked you about his lineage among you, and you claimed he is among you of lineage, and thus are messengers sent among the nobility of their people. I asked you whether anyone of his fathers said this saying; you said: No. I said: Had anyone among you said this saying, I would say: A man following the saying of one before him. I asked you whether you accused him of lying before he said what he said, and you claimed not. I knew he would not abandon lying upon people and lie upon Allah. I asked you whether there was a king among his fathers before, and you claimed not. I said: Had there been a king among his fathers, I would say: A man seeking the kingdom of his fathers. I asked you whether the nobles of people follow him or their weak, and you claimed their weak are his followers, and thus are the followers of messengers. I asked you whether they increase or decrease, and you claimed they increase, and thus is faith. I asked you whether anyone among them apostatizes in displeasure with his religion after entering it, and you claimed not, and thus is faith when it mixes with the cheerfulness of hearts: none dislikes it. I asked you whether he betrays, and you claimed not, and thus are messengers: they do not betray. I asked you whether you fought him and he fought you, and you claimed he did that, and that your war and his war are alternate fortunes - one time against you, another time for you against him - and thus are messengers, then theirs is the outcome. I asked you with what he commands you, and you claimed he commands you to worship Allah alone, associate nothing with Him, forbids what your fathers used to worship, and commands you with prayer, charity, chastity, and rendering trusts. He is a prophet. I knew he would come forth, but I did not think he was from you. If what you said is true, he will soon possess the place of these two feet of mine. By Allah, had I hope to reach him, I would undertake meeting him and wash his feet. Abu Sufyan said: So he called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and ordered it read; in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad, servant of Allah and His Messenger, to Heraclius, chief of Rome. Peace upon whoever follows guidance. As for what follows: I call you with the call of Islam. Embrace Islam and you will be safe; embrace Islam and Allah will give you your reward twice. If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin. And: O people of the Book, come to a word equal between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, associate nothing with Him, and take none of us others as lords besides Allah; if they turn away, say: Bear witness that we are Muslims. Abu Sufyan said: When he finished his speech, the voices of the nobles of Rome around him rose and their clamor increased; I do not know what they were saying. He said: We were ordered out. When I went out with my companions and got away, I said: The affair of the son of Abu Kabshah has risen; this king of Banu al-Asfar fears him. Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, I remained abased, certain his affair would prevail, until Allah brought Islam upon me while I was reluctant."
"7299","7273 - Abu Zurah narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman narrated, Shuayb narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, from Abu Sufyan, the like of it."
"7300","7274 - Masadah ibn Sad al-Attar narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated, Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated, from Husayn, from Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, who said: Abu Sufyan ibn Harb said: The first day I inclined toward Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the day Caesar said in his kingdom, authority, and presence what he said - meaning his saying: Had I known it was he, I would walk to him until I kiss his head and wash his feet. Abu Sufyan said: And I attended him with his forehead dripping sweat from the distress of the epistle which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had written to him. Abu Sufyan said: I remained inclined toward Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until I embraced Islam. And in his message: O people of the Book, come to a word equal between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, associate nothing with Him, and take none of us others as lords besides Allah; if they turn away, say: Bear witness that we are Muslims. He it is who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth to make it prevail over all religion, though the polytheists dislike. Fight those who believe not in Allah nor the Last Day, nor forbid what Allah and His Messenger forbade, nor follow the religion of truth, from those given the Book, until they pay the jizyah from hand while humbled."
"7301","712 - Sakhr al-Ghamidi. 7275 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym and Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, (h). And Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated, all saying: Shubah narrated, from Yala ibn Ata, from Umarah ibn Hadid, from Sakhr al-Ghamidi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bless my nation in its early morning. When he sent an expedition, he sent it at the beginning of the day. Sakhr was a merchant man, and would send his lads at the beginning of the day, so his wealth increased."
"7302","7276 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, (h). And Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Khuzai narrated, (h). And Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Aishah narrated, all saying: Hushaym narrated, from Yala ibn Ata, from Umarah ibn Hadid, from Sakhr al-Ghamidi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7303","7277 - Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated, from al-Numan ibn Thabit, from Yala ibn Ata, from Umarah ibn Hadid, from Sakhr al-Ghamidi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bless my nation in its early morning."
"7304","7278 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Yala ibn Ata, from Umarah ibn Hadid, from Sakhr, who had met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not revile the dead, lest you harm the living."
"7305","713 - Sakhr ibn al-Aylah al-Bajali. 7279 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Aban ibn Abd Allah al-Bajali narrated, Ibn Abi Hazim narrated, from Sakhr ibn al-Aylah, who said: I took the paternal aunt of al-Mughirah ibn Shubah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and al-Mughirah came. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked his aunt, saying: O Sakhr, when people embrace Islam, they safeguard their wealth and blood, so hand her over to them. So I handed her over to him. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had given me wealth of Banu Sulaym, then they embraced Islam and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking for the wealth. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called me and said: O Sakhr, when people embrace Islam, they safeguard their wealth and blood, so hand it over to them. So I handed it over to them."
"7306","7280 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi narrated, Aban ibn Abd Allah narrated, from Uthman ibn Abi Hazim and Kathir ibn Abi Hazim, from Sakhr ibn al-Aylah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Thaqif, and he mentioned the like of it."
"7307","714 - Sakhr Abu Hazim al-Ahmasi, father of Qays ibn Abi Hazim. 7281 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated, Shubah narrated, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw his father standing in the sun while he was delivering a sermon, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to move, or ordered concerning him and he was moved."
"7308","7282 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Sukkari al-Jundaysaburi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uqbah al-Sadusi narrated, Yahya ibn Abi Burdah ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Burdah ibn Abi Musa al-Ashari narrated, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from his father, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in worn appearance, and he said: Do you have wealth? He said: Yes, of every wealth Allah has given me: camels, cattle, and sheep. He said: Whoever has wealth, let it be seen upon him."
"7309","715 - Sakhr ibn Qudamah. 7283 - Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari and Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan narrated to us, both saying: Khalid ibn Khidash narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Ayyub, from al-Hasan, from Sakhr ibn Qudamah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No child shall be born after the hundredth year in whom Allah has need."
"7310","717 - Sakhr ibn al-Qaqa al-Bahili. 7284 - Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated, Qazah ibn Suwayd al-Bahili narrated, my father Suwayd ibn Hujayr narrated to me, my maternal uncle narrated to me, saying: I met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between Arafah and Muzdalifah, and took hold of his camel's halter, saying: O Messenger of Allah, what brings me near Paradise and distances me from the Fire? He said: By Allah, though you shortened the question, you have asked greatly and at length. Establish the prescribed prayer, pay the obligatory zakat, perform Hajj of the House, what you love people to do to you, do to them, and what you dislike people to do to you, leave people from it. Let go the camel's halter. Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj said: And Sakhr ibn al-Qaqa al-Bahili said it."
"7311","718 - al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, a Mukhadram; his name is Sakhr ibn Qays ibn Muawiyah ibn Husayn ibn Ubadah ibn Nazzal ibn Murrah ibn Ubayd ibn al-Harith ibn Amr ibn Sad ibn Zayd Manat ibn Tamim ibn Murrah. 7285 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, that al-Ahnaf ibn Qays said: While I was circumambulating the House in the time of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, a man from Banu Layth took my hand and said: Shall I not give you glad tidings? I said: Yes. He said: Do you remember when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to your people Banu Sad, and I began presenting Islam to them and calling them to it, and you said: He calls to good and commands it; indeed he calls to good and commands it. I conveyed that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: O Allah, bless al-Ahnaf. Al-Ahnaf used to say: There is nothing of my deeds I hope more from."
"7312","Mention of the death of Suhayb and some of his reports. 7286 - Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated, saying: Suhayb ibn Sinan, kunya Abu Yahya, died at Madinah in Shawwal of the year thirty-eight. He was from the captives of Mosul; the Romans had taken him captive."
"7313","7287 - Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated to me, from Ubayd Allah, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who said: When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was stabbed, he commanded Suhayb, freedman of Banu Judan, to lead people in prayer."
"7314","7288 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated, Umarah ibn Zad han narrated, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The forerunners are four: I am the forerunner of the Arabs, Suhayb is the forerunner of Rome, Salman is the forerunner of Persia, and Bilal is the forerunner of Abyssinia."
"7315","7289 - Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Mubarak narrated, Muhammad ibn Thawr narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, concerning His saying: And among people is one who sells himself - it was revealed concerning Suhayb ibn Sinan and Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him - and that the one who caught up with Suhayb on the road to Madinah was Qunfudh ibn Umayr ibn Judan."
"7316","7290 - Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Mubarak narrated, Muhammad ibn Thawr narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, claimed that Suhayb ransomed himself from his family with his wealth, then went out emigrating; they caught up with him on the road, and he gave them of what remained of his wealth."
"7317","What Suhayb narrated. Abd Allah ibn Umar from Suhayb. 7291 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Zayd ibn Aslam, who said: Ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque of Banu Amr ibn Awf to pray therein, and Suhayb entered with him. Men from the Ansar came to him greeting him. Abd Allah said: So I asked Suhayb how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would do when greeted in prayer. He said: He would gesture with his hand."
"7318","7292 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated, (h). And Ahmad ibn Ali al-Abbar narrated to us, Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated, both saying: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated, Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the mosque of Quba and was praying, and people began entering and greeting him. When Suhayb came out, I asked him how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would respond to them. He said: With his hand like this - and he gestured with it."
"7319","7293 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated, (h). And Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, Layth ibn Sad narrated, Bukayr ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, from Nabil, keeper of the cloak, from Ibn Umar, from Suhayb, who said: I passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, and greeted him, and he responded with a gesture."
"7320","Abu Layla from Suhayb. 7294 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Saminah narrated, (h). And Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bashshar Bundar narrated, both saying: Uthman ibn Umar narrated, al-Nahhas ibn Qahm narrated, al-Qasim ibn Awf al-Shaybani narrated, from Ibn Abi Layla, from his father, from Suhayb, that Muadh ibn Jabal, when he came to Syria, saw the Jews prostrating to their rabbis and scholars, and saw the Christians prostrating to their bishops. When he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he prostrated to him. He said: What is this, O Muadh? He said: I came to Syria and saw the Jews prostrating to their scholars and rabbis, and saw the Christians prostrating to their priests and monks. I said: What is this? They said: The greeting of prophets. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They lied upon their prophets as they distorted their book. Had I commanded anyone to prostrate to anyone, I would command the wife to prostrate to her husband."
"7321","Said ibn al-Musayyib from Suhayb. 7295 - Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Missisi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sinan al-Ruhawi narrated, saying: I heard my father saying: I heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah saying: I heard Mujahid saying: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyib saying: I heard Suhayb saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: He has not believed in the Quran who deems its prohibitions lawful."
"7322","7296 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Muin al-Asbahani narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Harish narrated, Yaqub ibn Muhammad narrated, Husayn ibn Hudhayfah narrated, my father and my uncles informed me, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Suhayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was shown your place of emigration: a salt-flat between two lava tracts; it may be Hajar, or it may be Yathrib. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Madinah, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, went out with him. I had intended to go out with him, but youths of Quraysh held me back. I spent that night standing, not sitting. They said: Allah has distracted him from you with his belly - though I was not ill. So they slept, and I went out. Some of them caught up with me after I had traveled, wanting to turn me back. I said to them: Shall you have ounces of gold and journeys of mine at Makkah, and you let me go free, and give me assurance? They did so. I followed them to Makkah and said: Dig beneath the threshold of the door, for beneath it are the ounces. Go to so-and-so woman with such-and-such sign, and take the two pieces of jewelry. I went out until I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Quba before he moved from it. When he saw me, he said: O Abu Yahya, the transaction has profited - three times. I said: O Messenger of Allah, none preceded me to you, and none informed you but Jibril, peace be upon him."
"7323","Aslam, freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, from Suhayb. 7297 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Musab ibn Abd Allah al-Zubayri narrated to me, my father narrated to me, from Rabiah ibn Uthman, from Zayd ibn Salm, from his father, who said: I went out with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, until I entered with him upon Suhayb at a garden in al-Aliyah. When Suhayb saw him, he said: O people, O people. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: What is with him - may he have no father - calling people against me? He said: He was only calling a lad of his called Yuhannas. He said: O Suhayb, there is nothing in you I criticize except three traits; were it not for them, I would prefer none over you. He said: What are they, for you are censorious? He said: Will he inform me about them? He said: You will not ask me about anything but I shall inform you of it. He said: And you will not inform me of anything but I shall believe you. He said: I see you squander your wealth, take the kunya of a prophet's name - Abu Yahya - and claim Arab lineage while your tongue is non-Arab. He said: As for squandering my wealth, I spend it only in its right. As for my kunya, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me the kunya Abu Yahya; shall I abandon it for your saying? As for my claiming lineage among the Arabs, the Romans took me captive when I was small, and I do not remember my family; even if I were split from dung, I would claim lineage to it."
"7324","Kab al-Ahbar from Suhayb. 7298 - Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated, Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, from Kab al-Ahbar, who said: We find in the Torah that Dawud, prophet of Allah, peace be upon him, when he finished his prayer would say: O Allah, set right for me my religion which You made the safeguard of my affair, and set right for me my world wherein You placed my livelihood. O Allah, I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath, and seek refuge in Your pardon from Your retribution, and seek refuge in You from You. None withholds what You give, none gives what You withhold, and the fortune of the fortunate avails him nothing against You. Kab al-Ahbar said: And Suhayb informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would finish with this supplication from his prayer."
"7325","7299 - Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Umari al-Qadi narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated, (h). And al-Qasim ibn Abbad al-Khattabi al-Basri narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Said narrated, both saying: Hafs ibn Maysarah narrated, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, that Kab swore by Allah who split the sea for Musa, that Suhayb narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never saw a town he intended to enter but said when he saw it: O Allah, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shade, Lord of the seven earths and what they carry, Lord of the devils and what they mislead, Lord of the winds and what they scatter: We ask You the good of this town and the good of its people, and seek refuge in You from its evil and the evil of its people."
"7326","7300 - Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Husayn al-Qili narrated, al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated, Musa ibn Uqbah narrated, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mughith, from Kab, Suhayb narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would supplicate, saying: O Allah, You are not a god we newly took, nor a lord we innovated, nor did we have before You a god to resort to and abandon You, nor did anyone aid You in creating us that we should associate him with You. Blessed and exalted are You. Kab said: And thus would Dawud, prophet of Allah, peace be upon him, supplicate."
"7327","Sayfi ibn Suhayb from Suhayb. 7301 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Fasawi narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman narrated, from Yusuf ibn Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sayfi ibn Suhayb, from his father Muhammad ibn Yazid and his uncle Abd al-Hamid ibn Yazid ibn Sayfi, from Sayfi ibn Suhayb, from Suhayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever gives a woman a dower, intending not to pay it to her, shall meet Allah, Mighty and Majestic, as an adulterer. And whoever takes a loan, intending not to repay it, shall meet Allah as a thief."
"7328","7302 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar narrated, Amr ibn Dinar, agent of al-Zubayr ibn Shuayb al-Basri, narrated, that the sons of Suhayb said to Suhayb: O our father, the sons of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrate from their fathers. He said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire. And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Any man who marries a woman, intending to give her nothing of her dower, dies the day he dies as an adulterer; and any man who buys a purchase from a man, intending to give him nothing of its price, dies the day he dies as a traitor, and the traitor is in the Fire."
"7329","7303 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated, Yusuf ibn Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sayfi narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, from his great-grandfather's father, from Suhayb, who said: I accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before revelation came to him."
"7330","7304 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Amr ibn Awf al-Wasiti narrated, Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated, Abd al-Hamid ibn Sayfi - a man from the descendants of Suhayb - narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, that Suhayb said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while before him were dates and bread. He said: Come near and eat. So I began eating of the dates. He said: Do you eat dates while you have ophthalmia? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I suck them from the other side. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled."
"7331","7305 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated, Jabir ibn Ghanim narrated, from Ibn Suhayb, from his father, from his grandfather Suhayb, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man's prayer in congregation equals his prayer alone by twenty-five."
"7332","7306 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musa al-Harashi narrated, Isa ibn Shuayb narrated, al-Daffa Abu Rawh al-Qaysi narrated, Abd al-Hamid ibn Sayfi narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is cupping at the hollow of the back of the neck, for it is a remedy for seventy-two ailments, and five ailments: madness, leprosy, vitiligo, and molar pain."
"7333","7307 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Shabib al-Asbahani narrated to us, Harun ibn Abd Allah al-Hammal narrated, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Zabalah al-Makhzumi narrated, Ali ibn Abd al-Hamid ibn Ziyad ibn Sayfi ibn Suhayb narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, from Suhayb, that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by a captive of his to seek protection for him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - while Suhayb was sitting in the mosque - and said to Abu Bakr: Who is this with you? He said: A captive of mine from the polytheists; I seek protection for him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Suhayb said: There was in the neck of this one a place for the sword. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, became angry. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him and said: Why do I see you angry? He said: I passed with this captive of mine by Suhayb, and he said: There was in the neck of this one a place for the sword. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps you harmed him. He said: No, by Allah. He said: Had you harmed him, you would have harmed Allah and His Messenger."
"7334","7308 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Shabib al-Asbahani narrated to us, Harun ibn Abd Allah al-Hammal narrated, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Zabalah al-Makhzumi narrated, Ali ibn Abd al-Hamid ibn Ziyad ibn Sayfi ibn Suhayb narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, from Suhayb, that when the polytheists surrounded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - they advanced upon the cave and turned back - he said: O Suhayb, and I have no Suhayb. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to go out, he sent Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, twice or thrice to Suhayb, and found him praying. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I found him praying, and disliked to cut his prayer. He said: You did right. They went out that night. In the morning he went out until he came to Umm Ruman, wife of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. She said: Do I not see you here, while your two brothers have gone out and left something of their provision for you? Suhayb said: So I went out until I entered upon my wife Umm Umar, and took my sword, quiver, and bow, until I arrived at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Madinah, and found him and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sitting. When Abu Bakr saw me, he rose to me and gave me glad tidings of the verse revealed concerning me, took my hand - I blamed him somewhat, and he apologized - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) congratulated me on profit, saying: The transaction has profited, O Abu Yahya."
"7335","7309 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Nasr ibn Shabib al-Asbahani narrated to us, Harun ibn Abd Allah al-Hammal narrated, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Makhzumi narrated, Ali ibn Abd al-Hamid ibn Ziyad ibn Sayfi ibn Suhayb narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, from Suhayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never witnessed a scene but I was present, never pledged a pledge but I was present, never sent an expedition but I was present in it, never raided a raid - early or late - but I was in it at his right or his left. They never feared what was before them but I was before them, nor what was behind them but I was behind them. I never placed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between me and the enemy until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away."
"7336","Hamzah ibn Suhayb from his father. 7310 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated, (h). And Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Nufayli narrated, both saying: Abd Allah ibn Amr narrated, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Hamzah ibn Suhayb, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: O Suhayb, you took a kunya though you have no child, and claimed lineage to the Arabs while you are a man of Rome. He said: O Commander of the Faithful, as for your saying you took a kunya though you have no child, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me the kunya Abu Yahya. As for your saying you claimed lineage to the Arabs while you are a man of Rome, I am a man from al-Namir ibn Qasit, taken captive from Mosul after I was a lad who knew his family and lineage."
"7337","Uthman ibn Suhayb from his father. 7311 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated, (h). And al-Qasim ibn Abbad al-Khattabi narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Said narrated, both saying: Rishdin ibn Sad narrated, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Had, from Uthman ibn Suhayb, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said one day to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Who is the most wretched of the former? He said: The one who hamstrung the camel, O Messenger of Allah. He said: You spoke truthfully; who is the most wretched of the latter? He said: I have no knowledge, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The one who strikes you upon this - and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointed with his hand to his crown. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say to the people of Iraq: By Allah, I wish he had sent forth your most wretched, so he dyed this - meaning his beard - from this - placing his hand upon the front of his head. The wording is of the hadith of Suwayd ibn Said, and al-Hadrami said in his hadith: And he pointed with his hand to his crown."
"7338","7312 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah narrated, from Ibn Suhayb, from his father Suhayb, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When the donkey brays, seek refuge in Allah from Satan, the outcast."
"7339","Ubayd ibn Umayr al-Laythi from Suhayb. 7313 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated, Ibn Lahiah narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Jafar ibn Abd Allah informed him that he heard Ubayd ibn Umayr al-Laythi narrating, from Suhayb ibn Sinan, who said: When water, then wine, then milk were presented to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he took the milk. Jibril said to him: You did right; you took the fitrah, and upon it every beast was tormented. Had you taken the wine, you would have strayed, your nation would have strayed, and you would have been of the people of this - pointing to the valley called the valley of Hell. I looked into it, and it was blazing."
"7340","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Suhayb. 7314 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, (h). And Abu Ayyub Ahmad ibn Bishr al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated, both saying: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the people of Paradise enter Paradise and the people of the Fire enter the Fire, a caller will call: O people of Paradise, you have with Allah an appointment He wishes to fulfill for you. They will say: What is it? Did He not make our scales heavy, whiten our faces, admit us to Paradise, and take us out of the Fire? He said: Then the veil shall be lifted, and they shall look at Him. By Him in whose hand is my soul, He has given them nothing dearer to them than looking at Him."
"7341","7315 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Khuzai narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: For those who do good is the best and increase. He said: When the people of Paradise enter Paradise - and he mentioned the like of it."
"7342","7316 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah and Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated, both from Thabit al-Bunani, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I marvel at Allah's decree for the Muslim: all of it is good. If ease befalls him and he is grateful, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, rewards him; if hardship befalls him and he is patient, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, rewards him. Hammad added in it: And every decree Allah, Mighty and Majestic, decrees for the Muslim is good."
"7343","7317 - Muhammad ibn Aban al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ammar ibn Khalid al-Wasiti narrated, Abd al-Hakim ibn Mansur narrated, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, who said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one of the two evening prayers. When he finished, he turned to us with his face laughing, saying: Will you not ask me what I laughed from? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: I marvel at Allah's decree for the Muslim servant: everything Allah decrees for him is good, and none for whom every decree of Allah is good but the Muslim servant."
"7344","7318 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Darir narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, that Thabit al-Bunani informed them, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, that Suhayb said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would move his lips with something in the days of Hunayn when he prayed the dawn prayer. We said: O Messenger of Allah, you keep moving your lips with something after the dawn prayer, which you did not do. He said: A prophet before us was pleased by the multitude of his nation, saying: These shall not perish - I think he said something - so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed to him: Give your nation choice among one of three: either I unleash upon them death, the enemy, or hunger. He presented that to them. They said: As for hunger, we have no strength for it, nor strength for the enemy, but death. So seventy thousand of them died in three days. Today I say: O Allah, through You I strive, through You I contend, and through You I fight."
"7345","7319 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Asr, he would whisper - whispering, in the saying of some, is moving his lips as if speaking something - and it was said to him: O Prophet of Allah, when you pray Asr you whisper? He said: A prophet among the prophets was pleased with his nation, saying: Who can stand against these? So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed to him to give them choice between My taking vengeance upon them, and My unleashing their enemy upon them. They chose vengeance, so He unleashed death upon them, and seventy thousand of them died in a day. He said: Whenever he narrated this hadith, he would narrate this other hadith. He said: There was a king among kings, and that king had a soothsayer who soothsaid for him. That soothsayer said: Look for me a boy, clever - or he said: quick to learn - to teach him this knowledge of mine, for I fear I shall die and this knowledge will cease among you, with none among you knowing it. They looked for a boy for him as he described, and ordered him to attend that soothsayer and visit him regularly. He said: On the boy's path was a monk in a hermitage - Mamar said: I think the people of hermitages that day were Muslims - so the boy began asking that monk whenever he passed him about his religion until he informed him. He said: I only worship Allah. The boy began staying with the monk and arriving late to the soothsayer. The soothsayer sent to the boy's family that he hardly attends me. The boy informed the monk of that, and the monk said to him: When the soothsayer says to you: Where were you? Say: With my family. When your family says to you: Where were you? Say: With the soothsayer. While the boy was upon that, he passed by a large company of people held back by a beast - some said that beast was the lion - so the boy took a stone and said: O Allah, if what the monk says is true, I ask You to kill this beast; if what the soothsayer says is true, that I not kill it. Then he threw it and killed the beast. The people said: Who killed it? They said: The boy. The people hastened to him, saying: This boy has learned knowledge none has learned. A blind man heard of him and came to him. The blind man said to him: If you restore my sight, you shall have such-and-such. The boy said to him: I want none of this from you, but tell me: if your sight returns to you, will you believe in Him who restored it to you? He said: Yes. So he supplicated Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and He restored his sight, and the blind man believed. The king learned of their affair and sent for them, and they were brought. He said: I shall kill each of you with a killing unlike his companion's. He ordered the monk and the man who had been blind: the saw was placed upon the parting of one of them, and he was killed, and he killed the other with another killing. Then he ordered the boy, saying: Take him to such-and-such mountain and cast him from its top. When they took him to that mountain and reached the place they intended, they began falling from the mountain and tumbling from it until none remained but the boy. Then the boy returned. The king ordered concerning him, saying: Take him to the sea and cast him into it. They took him to the sea, and Allah drowned those with him, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, saved him. The boy said: You shall never kill me until you crucify me, then shoot me, saying when you shoot me: In the name of Allah, Lord of this boy - or he said: In the name of Allah, Lord of the boy. He ordered him crucified, then said: In the name of Allah, Lord of the boy. The boy placed his hand upon his temple, then died. The people said: This boy has learned knowledge none has learned; we believe in the Lord of this boy. It was said to the king: Were you distressed that three opposed you? All these people have opposed you. So he dug a trench, cast firewood and fire into it, gathered the people, and said: Whoever returns to his religion we leave him; whoever does not return we cast into this fire. He began casting them into that trench. That is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Cursed were the companions of the trench - of fire full of fuel - until He reached: The Mighty, the Praiseworthy. It is mentioned that the boy was exhumed in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, placing his hand upon his temple as he placed it when killed."
"7346","7320 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri narrated to us, Ali ibn Uthman al-Lahiqi narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, Thabit al-Bunani informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There was among those before you a king with a sorcerer. When the sorcerer grew old, he said to the king: I have grown old, so hand me a boy to teach him sorcery. He handed him a boy, and he would teach him sorcery. Between the king and the sorcerer was a monk. The boy heard some of his speech and was pleased by his manner and speech. When he came to the sorcerer, he would sit with the monk; when he came to the sorcerer, he would beat him, saying: What detained you? When he returned to his family, he would sit with the monk; when he went to his family, they would say: What detains you? And beat him. He complained of that to the monk, saying: When the sorcerer wants to beat you, say: My family detained me. When your family wants to beat you, say: The sorcerer detained me. He said: While he was thus, one day he came upon a great beast holding people back from passing it. He said: Today I shall know: is the affair of the sorcerer dearer to Allah, or the affair of the monk? He took a stone and said: In the name of Allah; O Allah, if the affair of the monk is dearer to You and better, kill this beast. He threw it and killed it, and the people went. It reached the monk, who said: O my son, you are better than I, and you shall be tried; if tried, do not point to me. The boy would heal the blind and the leper and these ailments. The king had a companion who became blind. He heard of him and came to him with many gifts, saying: Heal me and yours is all this. He said: I heal none; only Allah, Mighty and Majestic, heals. If you believe in Allah, He shall heal you. He believed in Him, so he supplicated Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and he recovered. The blind man was seized and kept torturing him until he pointed him to the boy. The king said to him: Do you have a lord besides me? He said: Yes, my Lord and your Lord is Allah. He kept at him until he pointed him to the monk. He seized him with torment, saying: Return from your religion. He refused, so he ordered the saw placed upon the parting of his head and they split him. He said to the blind man: Return from your religion. He refused, so he placed the saw in the parting of his head until its two halves fell upon the ground. It was said to the boy: Return from your religion. He refused, so he sent him with a group to a mountain, saying: Take him up such-and-such mountain; when he reaches its peak, if he returns from his religion, well; otherwise hurl him. He said: He was taken to the mountain; when he reached its peak, he said: O Allah, suffice me against them as You will. The mountain shook with them, and they all went, and the boy came until he entered upon the king. He said: What happened to your companions? He said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sufficed me against them. He sent them with a group in a boat, saying: Take him to the sea; when you are in its midst with him, if he returns, well; otherwise drown him. They took him; when they ventured with him, he said: O Allah, suffice me against them as You will. They all drowned, and the boy came. He said: What happened to your companions? He said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sufficed me against them. He said to the king: You shall never kill me until you do what I command you; if you do it, you shall kill me. He said: What is it? He said: Gather people in one plain, then take an arrow from your quiver, saying: In the name of Allah, Lord of the boy. Then shoot me with it; if you do that, you shall kill me. He did so: placed the arrow, saying: In the name of Allah, Lord of the boy. The arrow fell upon his temple. The boy placed his hand upon the arrow's place and died. The people said: We believe in the Lord of the boy - thrice. It was said to him: Do you see what you feared? By Allah, it has befallen you: all people have believed. He ordered the mouths of the roads dug with trenches therein, saying: Whoever returns from his religion, well; otherwise cast him into it."
"7347","Abu al-Salil from Suhayb. 7321 - Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Mukram narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Kurdi narrated, (h). And Sahl ibn Musa Shiran narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated, both saying: Salim ibn Nuh narrated, from al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Salil, from Suhayb, who said: I prepared food for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and came to him while he was sitting among a group. I stood facing him and gestured to him. He gestured to me: And these? I said: No. I fell silent and stood in my place. When he looked at me, I gestured to him. He said: And these? I said: No. He did that twice - or thrice - then I said: Yes, and these. It was only a little thing I had prepared for him. He came, and they came with him, and ate - I think he said - and some remained of it."
"7348","720 - Suhayb ibn al-Numan. 7322 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari narrated to us, Ayyub ibn Muhammad al-Warraq narrated, Muhammad ibn Musab al-Qirqisai narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Suhayb ibn al-Numan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of a man's prayer in his house over his prayer where people see him is like the excellence of the obligatory over the voluntary."
"7349","Those named Safwan. 721 - Safwan ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf al-Jumahi, kunya Abu Wahb. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of the Conquest of Makkah, and he gave him four months' respite. He witnessed Hunayn while a polytheist, then embraced Islam after that. He died at the killing of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. 7323 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud al-Misri narrated to us, Khalid ibn Nizar narrated, Umar ibn Qays narrated, that Abd Allah ibn Safwan, while he was burying his father, a rider came to him and said: The Commander of the Faithful Uthman ibn Affan has been killed. He said: By Allah, I do not know which of the two calamities is greater: my father's death, or Uthman's killing?"
"7350","7324 - Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated, Muhammad ibn Talhah al-Taymi narrated to me, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Harithah ibn al-Numan narrated to me, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Harithah, who said: When Safwan ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf al-Jumahi came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: With whom have you lodged, O Abu Wahb? He said: I lodged with Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib. He said: You lodged with the most loving of Quraysh toward Quraysh."
"7351","7325 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, from Safwan ibn Abd Allah ibn Safwan, from his grandfather, who said: It was said to Safwan: Whoever does not emigrate has perished. So he called for his mount, rode it, and came to Madinah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What brought you, O Abu Wahb? He said: It reached me that there is no religion for one with no emigration. He said: Return to the valleys of Makkah. So he returned, entered the mosque, and pillowed his cloak. A man came and stole, and he was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: O Messenger of Allah, this one stole my cloak. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered concerning him and his hand was cut. He said: O Messenger of Allah, my cloak did not reach what a man's hand is cut for; I have made it charity upon him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why not before you brought him to me?"
"7352","7326 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, that Safwan ibn Umayyah came to Madinah, slept in the mosque, and placed a striped cloak of his beneath his head. A thief came and stole it. He was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered him cut. He said: O Messenger of Allah, it is his. He said: Why not before you brought him to me?"
"7353","7327 - Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Talhah al-Yarbui narrated, Ali ibn Mushir narrated, from Ashath ibn Sawwar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Safwan ibn Umayyah was sleeping in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a striped cloak of his beneath his head. A man came and pulled it from beneath his head while he slept. He caught up with him and brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioning his affair to him. He ordered him cut. Safwan said to him: This affair of my cloak did not reach that he be cut for it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why was it not before you brought him to me?"
"7354","What Safwan ibn Umayyah narrated. 7328 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Yazid ibn Harun narrated, Sulayman informed us, from Abu Uthman, from Amir ibn Malik, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Plague is martyrdom, abdominal illness is martyrdom, and the woman dying in childbirth is martyrdom."
"7355","7329 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Yahya ibn Said narrated, from al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Amir ibn Malik, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Plague, the woman dying in childbirth, and drowning are martyrdom."
"7356","7330 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Amir ibn Malik, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: He said: Plague, drowning, burning, and the woman dying in childbirth are martyrdom for my nation."
"7357","7331 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, both saying: Yusuf ibn Hammad al-Mana narrated, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, from Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas, who said: There was a wedding feast among us, and Safwan ibn Umayyah entered upon us. Food was brought, and he said: Tear meat with the front teeth, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Tear meat with the front teeth, for it is more appetizing, more wholesome, and more agreeable."
"7358","7332 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated, Sufyan narrated, Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah narrated, from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, who said: My father gave me a wedding in the reign of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and invited people to a feast of ours. Among those who came to us was Safwan ibn Umayyah. He said: Tear meat with the teeth, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: It is more appetizing, more agreeable, more wholesome, and more agreeable."
"7359","7333 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated, Khalid narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muawiyah, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, who said: Safwan ibn Umayyah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me cutting meat from the bone, and said: O Safwan. I said: At your service. He said: Bring meat near your mouth, for it is more wholesome and more agreeable."
"7360","7334 - Ubayd al-Ijli narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated, Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, that a thief came to him while he slept, pulled his lower garment from beneath him, awoke, seized him, and brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He ordered concerning him and he was cut. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I have permitted it to him. He said: Why not before you brought him to me? The imam, when a hadd among the hudud reaches him, establishes it."
"7361","7335 - al-Abbas ibn Hamdan al-Hanafi narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated, Amr ibn Hammad ibn Talhah al-Qannad narrated, Asbat ibn Nasr narrated, from Simak ibn Harb, from Humayd, nephew of Safwan ibn Umayyah, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, who said: I was sleeping in the mosque with a striped cloak of mine worth thirty dirhams upon me. A man came to me and snatched it from me. The man was seized and brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He ordered him cut. I came to him and said to him: Do you cut him for thirty dirhams? It is his. He said: Why was this not before you brought him to me?"
"7362","7336 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Missisi narrated to us, Abu Tawbah al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Yazid, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, that a thief came to his father while he slept, pulled his lower garment from beneath his head, awoke, seized him, and brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He ordered him cut. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I have permitted it to him. He said: Why not before you brought him to me? The imam, when a hadd among Allah's hudud reaches him, establishes it."
"7363","7337 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated, Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated, from Qatadah, from Ata, from Tariq ibn Muraqqa, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, that a man stole a cloak, and he raised him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He ordered his cutting. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I have pardoned him. He said: Why was this not before you brought him to me, O Abu Wahb? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut him."
"7364","7338 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, Sadan ibn Yahya narrated, Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Abd Allah ibn Safwan ibn Umayyah, from his father, who said: I slept in the mosque, and my lower garment was pulled from beneath me. I caught up with him and brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He ordered his hand cut. I said: O Messenger of Allah, it is his. He said: Why not before you brought him to me?"
"7365","7339 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Sharik narrated, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Umayyah ibn Safwan, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed iron coats of mail from me on the Day of Hunayn. I said: Guaranteed, O Muhammad? He said: Guaranteed. He said: Some of them were lost, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If you wish, we shall compensate you for it? He said: No, I desire Islam more than that."
"7366","7340 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Yahya ibn Adam narrated, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me on the Day of Hunayn, while he was the most hateful of people to me; he kept giving me until he was the most beloved of people to me."
"7367","7341 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, Sadan ibn Yahya narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah, from al-Zuhri, from Abd Allah ibn Safwan ibn Umayyah, from his father, who said: When it was said to me: perished is he who did not emigrate, I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. He said: Return, O Abu Wahb, to the valleys of Makkah."
"7368","7342 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi al-Jurjani narrated, Abd al-Razzaq narrated, Yahya ibn al-Ala informed us, Bashir ibn Numayr narrated, that he heard Makhul saying: Yazid ibn Abd Allah narrated, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Amr ibn Qurrah came to him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, wretchedness has been written upon me, and I see myself earning only from my tambourine with my palm; will you permit me singing without indecency? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not permit you, nor honor. You lied, O enemy of Allah; Allah provided you lawful, wholesome provision, and you chose what Allah forbade of His provision in place of what Allah permitted of His lawful. Had I warned you before, I would have done with you. Arise from me and repent to Allah. If you take anything after the warning, I shall strike you a painful striking, shave your head in mutilation, banish you from your family, and make your spoil plunder for the youths of Madinah. So Amr rose with evil and shame known only to Allah. When he turned, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These are the disobedient; whoever of them dies without repentance, Allah shall gather him on the Day of Resurrection as he was: effeminate, naked, not screened from people by a fringe, whenever he stands he falls. Then Urfutah ibn Nuhayk al-Tamimi rose and said: O Messenger of Allah, I and my household earn from this hunting, and we have in it portion and blessing, though it distracts from the remembrance of Allah and from prayer in congregation, and we need it; do you permit it or forbid it? He said: I permit it, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has permitted it. A good deed, and Allah is more deserving of excuse. Before me there were messengers of Allah, all of whom hunted or sought hunting. Sufficient for you for congregational prayer, when absent from it seeking provision, is your love of the congregation and its people, your love of the remembrance of Allah and its people. Seek lawful for yourself and your dependents, for that is striving in the path of Allah. Know that Allah's aid is in righteous trade."
"7369","722 - Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami. What Safwan ibn al-Muattal narrated. 7343 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated, my father narrated, Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated, from Abd Allah ibn al-Mufaddal, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Safwan ibn al-Muattal, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of his journeys. He prayed Isha, then slept until about midnight, then rose and recited these verses from the end of Surah Al Imran until he completed the Surah. Then he performed ablution and used the toothstick, then prayed two rakahs - I do not know whether his standing, bowing, or prostration was longer. Then he said: Then he awoke and recited the verses also, then used the toothstick and performed ablution, then did the like of that, then prayed what he prayed like the first. Then he kept praying two by two, sleeping then waking as he did the first time, until he had prayed eleven rakahs."
"7370","7344 - Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated, Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated, from al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, from Said al-Maqburi, from Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the sun rises, Satan accompanies it; when it spreads, he leaves it. When it nears the zenith, he accompanies it; when it passes, he leaves it. When it nears setting, he accompanies it; when it sets, he leaves it. So he forbade prayer in those hours."
"7371","7345 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali narrated, Salm ibn Qutaybah narrated, Umar ibn Nabhan narrated, Abu Isa Sallam narrated to me, Safwan ibn al-Muattal narrated: We went out as pilgrims. When we were at al-Arj, there was a snake writhing, and soon it died. A man among us took out a rag from a cloak of his, wrapped it, and dug for it in the ground. Then we came to Makkah; while we were in the Sacred Mosque, a person stood over us and said: Which of you is the companion of Amr ibn Jabir? We said: We do not know him. He said: Which of you is the companion of the jinn? They said: This one. He said: He was the last of the nine believers who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) listening to the Quran."
"7372","7346 - Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Marwazi narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman narrated, from Ismail ibn Ayyash, Abu Wahb narrated, from Makhul, from Safwan ibn al-Muattal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me announcing: Do not make nabidh in jars."
"7373","723 - Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi. Abd Allah ibn Masud from Safwan ibn Assal. 7347 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated, al-Saq ibn Hazn narrated, Ali ibn al-Hakam al-Bunani narrated, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi narrated, saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was reclining in the mosque upon a cloak of his. I said to him: O Messenger of Allah, I have come seeking knowledge. He said: Welcome to the seeker of knowledge; the seeker of knowledge is indeed surrounded by angels and shaded by their wings, then they ride one upon another until they reach the lowest heaven, out of their love for what he seeks. What have you come seeking? He said: Safwan said: O Messenger of Allah, we keep traveling between Makkah and Madinah, so give us a fatwa about wiping over the leather socks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Three days for the traveler, and a day and night for the resident."
"7374","Zirr ibn Hubaysh al-Asadi from Safwan. Zubayd al-Yami from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan. 7348 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj, and Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, saying: Abu Musa al-Harawi narrated, Ashath ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zubayd al-Yami narrated, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, who said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was traveling, a man came and said: O Muhammad. They said: Lower your voice. He said: O Messenger of Allah, a man loves people but did not see them? He said: A man is with whom he loves. Then he asked him about wiping over the leather socks. He said: Three days and their nights for the traveler, and a day and night for the resident; he does not remove it for urine, sleep, or stool, except from janabah. Then he asked him about repentance. He said: Repentance has a gate in the west, a distance of seventy years - or forty years - remaining thus until some of your Lord's signs come: the rising of the sun from its west."
"7375","Talhah ibn Musarrif from Zirr. 7349 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Tustari al-Daqiqi narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Affan narrated, Yahya ibn Fudayl narrated, al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated, Abu Janab narrated, Talhah ibn Musarrif narrated to me, that Zirr ibn Hubaysh came to Safwan ibn Assal and said: What brought you early to me this morning? He said: Seeking knowledge brought me early. He said: None does what you did but the angels lay their wings for him, pleased with what he does. I said: I came early to ask you about wiping over the leather socks. He said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I wipe over the leather socks, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes; three for the traveler, not removing them for stool, urine, or sleep, and a day for the resident."
"7376","Habib ibn Abi Thabit from Zirr from Safwan. 7350 - Ubayd al-Ijli narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated, Ali ibn Abd Allah al-Amiri al-Razi narrated, saying: I read to Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, and it was read to him, and we said: Shall we narrate from you? He said: Yes. He said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed me, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh al-Asadi, that he came to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi - who was among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked - and said: Your need? They said: We went out today seeking knowledge. He said: Whoever leaves his house seeking knowledge, the angels lay their wings for the learner and the scholar. I asked him about wiping. He said: Yes; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked, and he made for the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and night, except from janabah, but from stool, urine, and sleep."
"7377","Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr. 7351 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Asim, from Zirr, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal asking him about wiping over the leather socks. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us in travel not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights, except from janabah, but from sleep, stool, and urine."
"7378","7352 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi. He said: What brought you? I said: I have come seeking knowledge. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No one leaves his house in pursuit of knowledge but the angels lay their wings for him, pleased with what he does. I said: I have come to ask you about wiping over the leather socks? He said: Yes; I was in the army which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent, and he commanded us to wipe over the leather socks when we had put them on in purity: three when we traveled, and a night when we resided, not removing them for stool, urine, or sleep, removing them only from janabah. He said: And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: In the west is an open gate for repentance, a distance of seventy years, not closed until the sun rises from its west."
"7379","7353 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal. He said: What brought you? I said: Seeking knowledge. He said: The angels lay their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he seeks. He said: I said: Doubt about wiping over the leather socks after stool and urine has entered my chest, and you were a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I have come asking you about that: did you hear anything from him concerning it? He said: Yes; he used to command us, when we were traveling or were travelers, not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights except from janabah, but from stool, urine, and sleep. I said: Did you hear him mention love? He said: Yes; while we were with him on a march, a Bedouin called him with a loud voice - or he said resonant - Ibn Uyaynah doubted - saying: O Muhammad. He answered him with similar speech, saying: Mah. He said: What do you think of a man who loves people but did not join them? He said: He is on the Day of Resurrection with whom he loves. He said: He kept narrating to us until he said: From the west is a gate, its width a distance of seventy years, which Allah opened for repentance the day He created the heavens and the earth, and He does not close it until the sun rises from it."
"7380","7354 - Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Khashshab al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Jafar al-Raqqi narrated, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Asim, from Zirr, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal. He said: When we traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he commanded us not to remove our leather socks for three nights and their days, except from janabah; but from stool or urine, no."
"7381","7355 - Abu Zurah and Jafar al-Qalanisi narrated to us, both saying: Adam narrated, (h). And Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, both saying: Shubah narrated, from Asim, from Zirr, from Safwan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"7382","7356 - Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated, Sharik narrated, from Asim, from Zirr, from Safwan, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set, for wiping over the leather socks, three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"7383","7357 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated, Zaidah narrated, Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud narrated, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to enjoin us, when we traveled or were on a journey, not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights except from janabah, but from urine, sleep, and stool."
"7384","7358 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated, Zuhayr narrated, Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud narrated, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal, while something concerning wiping over the leather socks troubled my chest, so I said: Something concerning wiping over the leather socks has troubled my chest. He said: Yes; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us, when we were on a journey or traveling, not to remove or take off our leather socks for three days and their nights, for stool, urine, or sleep, except janabah. And a man of the desert folk came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) calling out to him in a loud voice: O Muhammad, O Muhammad. So we said to him: Woe to you, lower - or restrain - your voice, for you have been forbidden this. He said: No, by Allah, until he hears me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Here I am. He said: What do you think of a man who loved a people but did not join them? He said: He is with whom he loved; a man is with whom he loved."
"7385","7359 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I went in the morning to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, intending to ask him about wiping over the leather socks, and he said: What brought you? I said: Seeking knowledge. He said: Shall I not give you glad tidings? I said: Yes indeed. Then he raised the hadith, saying: The angels lower their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he seeks. Then he asked him about wiping over the leather socks, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The traveler wipes over the leather socks three days and their nights, for urine, stool, or sleep, not for janabah. Then he began narrating to us, saying: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, a bedouin called out to him in a loud voice: O Muhammad, O Muhammad, O Muhammad. I said: Lower your voice, for you have been forbidden to raise your voice. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him in like manner to what he heard from him, saying: Here I am. Then he asked him about love: about a man loving a people he has not yet joined. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man is with whom he loved. Then he began narrating to us, saying: The door of repentance is open from the direction of the west, its width a journey of seventy years; it will not be closed until the sun rises from its direction. Then he recited this verse: Do they await but that the angels should come to them - to the end of the verse."
"7386","7360 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, and he said: What brought you, O bald one? I said: Seeking knowledge. He said: It has reached me that the angels lower their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he does. I said: Something concerning wiping over the leather socks has stirred - or occurred - in my mind; have you retained anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it? He said: Yes; when we were on a journey or traveling, he commanded us not to take off our leather socks for three days except from janabah, but from stool, urine, and sleep. I said: Have you retained anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning love? He said: Yes; we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on such-and-such a journey, when a man at the rear of the people called out to him in a loud voice, a rude, coarse bedouin, saying: O Muhammad, O Muhammad. The people said to him: Quiet, for you have been forbidden this. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him in like manner to his voice, saying: Here I am. He said: A man loves a people but has not joined them. He said: A man is with whom he loved. He kept narrating to me until he narrated to me: Allah, mighty and majestic, has placed in the west a door, the width of its passage seventy years, for repentance; it will not be closed until the sun rises from its direction - and that is the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: The day that some of the signs of your Lord come, no soul will benefit from its faith if it had not believed before or earned good in its faith."
"7387","7361 - Uthman ibn Amr al-Dabbi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated, Hammam narrated, Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came as a delegate during the caliphate of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him - and what carried me to come as a delegate was meeting the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So I met Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, and I said: Did you meet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, and I campaigned with him in twelve campaigns. I said: Narrate to me about wiping over the leather socks. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us, when we were on a journey or traveling, to wipe over our leather socks for three nights and their days, for urine, stool, or sleep; but from janabah, no. He said: And while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was up one night, he mentioned a door from the direction of the setting of the sun, the breadth of its width a seventy-year journey, open for repentance; it will not be closed until the sun rises from its direction. He said: And a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) calling out to him in a loud voice, saying: O Muhammad, what do you think of a man who loves a people but has not yet joined them? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him in like manner to his voice: A man is with whom he loved."
"7388","7362 - Said ibn Sayyar al-Wasiti narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn narrated, Abu al-Ahwas narrated, from Asim, from Zirr, from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: When we were on a journey, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us not to take off our leather socks for three days except from janabah, but from stool, urine, or sleep."
"7389","7363 - al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Abd al-Malik ibn al-Walid ibn Madan narrated, from Asim, from Zirr, from Safwan ibn Assal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said concerning wiping over the leather socks: For the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"7390","7364 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq narrated, Ikrimah ibn Ibrahim narrated, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr, from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: For the traveler, when he has put his feet in while both are pure, to wipe over them for three days and their nights, and not to remove them for stool or urine, except from janabah."
"7391","7365 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, Khalid ibn Yusuf al-Samti narrated, Abu Awanah narrated, from Asim, from Zirr, from Safwan ibn Assal. Zirr said: I came to him and he said: What brought you? I said: Seeking knowledge. He said: There is no Muslim man who seeks knowledge but the angels lower their wings for him, pleased with what he does. I said to him: You are a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and something concerning wiping over the leather socks after stool and urine has troubled my chest; so inform me of something, if you heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us, when we were on a journey or traveling, to wipe over our leather socks for three nights and their days, and not to take them off except from janabah, but from stool, urine, or sleep. I said to him: Did you hear him saying anything about love? He said: Yes; we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a campaign - or a lesser pilgrimage - when a bedouin came forward on his mount until, when he was at the rear of the people, he began calling out in a loud voice: O Muhammad, O Muhammad. It was said: Woe to you, lower your voice, for you have been commanded with that. He said: By Allah, I will not do so until he hears me - and he was a coarse, rude bedouin. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard his voice, he said: Here I am. He said: What do you think of a man who loved a people but has not yet joined them? He said: He is with whom he loved. He kept narrating to us until he narrated to me that toward the west there is a door open for repentance, the breadth of its width seventy years; it will remain open until the sun rises from its direction, and when it rises from its direction - that is when: No soul will benefit from its faith if it had not believed before or earned good in its faith."
"7392","7366 - Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated, Ali ibn Mushir narrated, from Misar ibn Kidam, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal, and he said to me: What brought you? He said: I said: I seek knowledge. He said: No man leaves his house seeking knowledge but the angels lower their wings for him, pleased with what he does. I said to him: You were a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but the matter of wiping over the leather socks has occurred in my mind. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us, when we were on a journey or traveling, not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights except from janabah, but from sleep, stool, or urine. And he informed me that a bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and called out to him from the rear of the people in a loud voice, saying: O Muhammad. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him in like manner to his speech: Here I am. He said: What do you think of a man who loves a people but has not yet joined them? He said: A man is with whom he loved."
"7393","7367 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Saydalani al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Sayf Abu Dawud al-Harrani narrated, Said ibn Bazigh narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq - Misar ibn Kidam narrated to me - from Asim, from Zirr, from Safwan ibn Assal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - in like manner."
"7394","7368 - Yahya ibn Muhammad al-Hinnai narrated to us, Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, and he said: What brought you? I said: I came seeking knowledge. He said: We used to converse that the angels lower their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he seeks. Then I asked him about wiping over the leather socks. He said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and we used to wipe for three days and their nights, and the resident a day and a night."
"7395","7369 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi narrated, al-Hakam ibn Bashir narrated, Amr ibn Qays al-Mulai narrated, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, and I stood at his door, and he came out to me, saying: What brought you? I said: You are a man who accompanied the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the matter of wiping over the leather socks after stool and urine has troubled my chest; have you heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning anything about that? He said: Yes; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us, when we were traveling, not to remove our leather socks for three days, but from stool, urine, or sleep."
"7396","Narrated Muhammad ibn Jaban al-Jundayshaburi from Muhammad ibn Humayd from al-Hakam ibn Bashir from Amr ibn Qays al-Mula'i and Khallad al-Saffar from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7397","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Sa'id ibn Muhammad ibn Tawwab al-Husri from al-Hurr ibn Malik al-Anbari from Mubarak ibn Fadala from Asim ibn Bahdala from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I went in the morning to one of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Safwan ibn Assal, and he said: What has brought you? I said: I have come seeking knowledge. He said: Shall I not tell you that the angels lower their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he does. And I saw us while we were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, when a Bedouin called out to him in a loud voice, saying: O Muhammad. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Here I am. He said: A man loves a people but has not done any of their deeds. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man is with whom he loves."
"7398","Narrated Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti from Muhammad ibn Hassan al-Burjulani from Muhammad ibn Yazid from Abu al-Ashhab Ja'far ibn al-Harith from Asim from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to enjoin us, when we travelled or were on a journey, not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights except from major impurity, but only because of sleep, urine, and stool."
"7399","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Ubaydullah ibn Amr al-Qawariri, and narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati from Yahya ibn Khalaf Abu Salama al-Jawbari, both saying: Abd al-A'la narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Khalid ibn Kathir al-Hamdani narrated to me from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him about wiping over the leather socks, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have come asking you about knowledge. He said: The angels lower their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he seeks. And what knowledge are you asking about? I said: O Prophet of Allah, about wiping over the leather socks. He said: Yes, a day and a night for the resident, and three for the traveller, because of stool or urine; then renew ablution."
"7400","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Nu'aym ibn Hammad from Ibn al-Mubarak, Malik ibn Mighwal informed us from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us, when we were on a journey, not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights because of urine, nor sleep, nor stool, except from major impurity."
"7401","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqa al-Baghdadi from Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Himsi from his father from Salama ibn Abd al-Malik al-Awsi from Abu al-Hasan al-Hamdani Ali ibn Salih from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us, when we travelled, not to remove the leather socks for three days and their nights except from major impurity; he commanded us to wipe over them because of stool, urine, and sleep."
"7402","Narrated Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi from Ali ibn Muslim al-Tusi from Isma'il ibn Ulayya from Rawh ibn al-Qasim from Asim ibn Bahdala from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I asked Safwan ibn Assal about wiping. He said: When we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we would wipe over them three times while travelling, except from major impurity, but only because of stool, urine, and sleep."
"7403","Narrated Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid al-Bayruti from Safwan ibn Salih from al-Walid ibn Muslim, Shayban informed me from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, Zirr ibn Hubaysh narrated to me, who said: Doubt about wiping over the leather socks after urine and stool entered my chest. So I came to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi and mentioned that to him. I said: Inform me of something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: He used to command us, when we were travelling or travellers, not to take off our leather socks for three nights and their days except from major impurity, but only because of urine, stool, or sleep."
"7404","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Ishaq ibn Shahin from Khalid from Sa'id al-Jurayri from Asim from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about wiping over the leather socks: Three days for the traveller, and a day and a night for the resident."
"7405","Narrated Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti from Abu al-Sha'tha Ali ibn al-Hasan from Abda ibn Sulayman from Salih ibn Salih from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I came to Safwan ibn Assal and he said: What has brought you? I said: I seek knowledge. He said: The angels spread their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he seeks. And I asked him about wiping over the leather socks, and he said: We used to wipe at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for three days and their nights for the traveller, and a day and a night for the resident."
"7406","Narrated Abdullah ibn Najiya from Abdullah Umar ibn Aban from Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman from Habib ibn Hassan from Asim from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about wiping over the leather socks: For the traveller three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"7407","Narrated Abdullah ibn Najiya from Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban from Abu Khalid al-Ahmar, Hajjaj narrated to us from Asim from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about wiping: For the traveller over the leather socks three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"7408","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani, Abu Kurayb narrated to us from al-Muharibi from Abu Janab al-Kalbi from Asim ibn Bahdala from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I went in the morning to Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, asking him about wiping over the leather socks, and he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us to wipe for three days and their nights while travelling, and a day and a night for the resident. And I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever goes out in search of knowledge, the angels lower their wings for him."
"7409","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Habib al-Tara'ifi al-Raqqi from Ayyub ibn Muhammad al-Wazzan from Urwa ibn Marwan al-Raqqi from al-Rabi' ibn Badr from Asim from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and began narrating to us: For repentance there is a gate, the width between its two leaves is like what is between the east and the west; it will not be closed until the sun rises from its west. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: The Day when some of the signs of your Lord will come, no soul will benefit from its faith if it had not believed before or earned good through its faith."
"7410","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Muharibi, my father narrated to us from Yahya ibn Salama ibn Kuhayl from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I said to Safwan ibn Assal: Something about wiping over the leather socks has entered my chest. Have you heard anything about it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, he used to command us, when we were travelling, not to remove the leather socks for three days except from major impurity, but only because of sleep, stool, and urine."
"7411","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Muharibi, Salih ibn Musa al-Talhi narrated to us from Asim from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7412","Narrated Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Naysaburi from Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani from Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman, al-Mas'udi narrated to us from Salama ibn Kuhayl from Asim from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: When we were travelling or travellers, we would not remove our leather socks for three days."
"7413","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Missisi from Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli from Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us from Asim from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixed wiping over the leather socks for the traveller as three days and their nights, and the resident a day and a night."
"7414","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani from Ali ibn al-Madini, and narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna Abu Musa, and narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qatrani from al-Hasan ibn Khalid al-Harrani, all saying: Ziyad ibn al-Rabi' al-Yahmuri narrated to us, Asim ibn Bahdala narrated to us from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I was among the strictest of people about wiping over the leather socks, so I met Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi and asked him. He said: What brought you in the morning? I said: Seeking knowledge. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever goes out in the morning seeking knowledge is in the path of Allah until he returns, and the angels lower their wings for the seeker of knowledge. He said: And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah, Mighty and Exalted, has opened a gate from the direction of the west for repentance, between its two leaves is seventy years; it will not be closed until the sun rises from its direction. He said: I said: Add more for me, may Allah have mercy on you. He said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, when a Bedouin with a loud voice called out to him: O Muhammad, stop so we may ask you three things. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him with something like his saying: Here I am. The Bedouin came and said: A man loves a people but has not yet done any of their deeds. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man is with whom he loves. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never rejoiced at a hadith as they rejoiced at it. I said: Narrate to me about wiping over the leather socks. He said: When we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, we would wipe for three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night. And the wording is that of Ali ibn al-Madini."
"7415","Narrated Abdullah Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Salih ibn Malik al-Khuwarizmi from Abd al-A'la ibn Abi al-Musawir, Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud narrated to us from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: We came to Safwan ibn Assal and he said: Are you visiting? We said: Yes. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever visits his believing brother wades through the gardens of Paradise until he returns, and whoever visits his sick believing brother wades through the gardens of Paradise until he returns."
"7416","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from al-Musayyab ibn Wadih, Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us from Ibn Ajlan from Asim ibn Bahdala from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon a Jewish boy while he was sick, and said: Do you bear witness that there is no god but Allah? He said: Yes. He said: Do you bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger? He said: Yes. Then he died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Muslims took charge of him; they washed him and buried him."
"7417","Narrated Bakr ibn Ahmad ibn Muqbil al-Basri from Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Adami from Ja'far ibn Salama, Hammad ibn Yazid Abu Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, Asim ibn Bahdala narrated to us from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: He said: Allah has a rooster whose head is beneath the Throne, its wing is in the air, and its talons are in the earth. When it is in the last hours of the night and at the ends of the prayers, it flaps its wing and claps with glorification, so the roosters crow answering it with glorification."
"7418","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Umar ibn Salih ibn Jubayra al-Wasiti, Mushma'ill ibn Milhan narrated to us from Ata ibn Ajlan from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he heard a man calling the adhan, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon the fitra. He said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. He said: He has testified to the truth. He said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. He said: He has come out of the Fire."
"7419","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj from Muhammad ibn Bakkar, Yahya ibn Uqba ibn Abi al-Ayzar narrated to us from Idris al-Awdi and Ibn Abi Layla, both from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan ibn Assal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in: When the sky is split apart."
"7420","Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Zirr from Safwan. Narrated Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid al-Bayruti from Safwan ibn Salih from al-Walid ibn Muslim, Marwan ibn Janah informed me from Ishaq ibn Abdullah from Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Zirr from Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about wiping over the leather socks: For the traveller three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"7421","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Yusuf, Yahya ibn Hamza narrated to us from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwa from Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Zirr ibn Hubaysh from Safwan ibn Assal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Exalted, has opened a gate from the west: its extent is seventy autumns for repentance; He has not closed it until the sun rises from its west. And no man goes out seeking knowledge but the angels spread their wings for him, pleased with what he does. The Arabs said at that: O Prophet of Allah, has Allah not given a servant a single trait that is better? He said: Good character. Then they said to him: Should we take medicine? He said: Do you not know that the One who sent down the disease sent down the cure, or no disease has come down except that a cure has been sent down for it, except one disease. They said: O Prophet of Allah, what is it? He said: Old age. Then he said: For the traveller three days wiping over his leather socks, and for the resident a day and a night."
"7422","Abdullah ibn Salama from Safwan. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and Abu Khalifa and Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Sawra al-Baghdadi, all saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shu'ba narrated to us from Amr ibn Murra from Abdullah ibn Salima from Safwan ibn Assal, that two Jews, one of them said to his companion: Let us go to this Prophet. He said: Do not say to him Prophet, for if he hears you he will have four eyes. So they went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about the saying of Allah, Mighty and Exalted: And We gave Musa nine clear signs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not associate anything with Allah, and do not kill the soul which Allah has forbidden except by right, and do not commit adultery, and do not steal, and do not walk with an innocent one to a ruler to kill him, and do not consume usury, and do not slander a chaste woman, and do not flee from the battle. And upon you especially, O Jews, not to transgress on the Sabbath. They kissed his hand and said: We bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah. He said: What prevents you from following me? They said: Dawud prayed that there would always be a prophet among his offspring, and we fear that if we follow you the Jews will kill us."
"7423","Abu Gharif Ubaydullah ibn Khalifa from Safwan. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, Abu Rawq Atiyya ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Abu al-Gharif Ubaydullah ibn Khalifa narrated to us from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me in a detachment and said: Fight in the name of Allah in the path of Allah; do not misappropriate spoils, and do not betray, and do not kill a child. And for the traveller three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night wiping over the leather socks."
"7424","Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman from Safwan. Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and Abdan ibn Ahmad, both saying: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Maslama ibn Ali narrated to us from al-Awza'i from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salama from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) encouraged seeking knowledge before it goes away. A man said: How will it go when we have learned it and taught it to our children? He became angry and said: Are not the Torah and the Gospel in the hands of the People of the Book, yet do they avail them anything?"
"7425","Safwan Abu al-Qasim al-Zuhri. Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi, and narrated Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi from Abu Nu'aym, both saying: Bishr ibn Sulayman narrated to us from al-Qasim ibn Safwan al-Zuhri from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Cool down the Zuhr prayer, for the severity of heat is from the blast of Hell."
"7426","Safwan ibn Qudama al-Mura'i. Narrated Musa ibn Harun and Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Anbar al-Misri, both saying: Musa ibn Maymun ibn Musa al-Mura'i narrated to us, saying: My father Maymun ibn Musa narrated to me from his father from his grandfather Abd al-Rahman ibn Safwan ibn Qudama, who said: My father Safwan migrated to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in Madina, and pledged allegiance to him upon Islam. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) extended his hand to him, and he wiped over it. Safwan said to him: I love you, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: A man is with whom he loves."
"7427","Safwan ibn Muhammad, and it is said Muhammad ibn Safwan ibn Muhammad. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us from Dawud ibn Abi Hind from al-Sha'bi from Safwan ibn Muhammad, that he came to his sheep and caught two hares, and slaughtered them with a sharp stone. He brought them to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and hung them up, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I slaughtered them with a sharp stone. He said: Eat them both."
"7428","Safwan or Ibn Safwan. Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qatrani from Sulayman ibn Harb, Shu'ba narrated to us from Simak ibn Harb, who said: I heard Safwan, or Ibn Safwan, say: I sold to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the leg of trousers, and he weighed out for me and gave me extra."
"7429","Those named Suhar. Suhar ibn Abbas, and it is said Ibn Abis al-Abdi. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Muslim ibn Ibrahim, and narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Abu Umar al-Hawdi, al-Dahhak ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir narrated to us from Abd al-Rahman ibn Suhar from Suhar, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a sickly man, so permit me to make nabidh in a small jar like this one, meaning a small one. So he permitted him."
"7430","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad, Isma'il ibn Ibrahim, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Wahb ibn Baqiyya, Khalid informed us, both from Sa'id al-Jurayri from Abu al-Ala from Abd al-Rahman ibn Suhar from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Hour will not come until tribes are swallowed up by the earth, until it is said: Who remains of the sons of so-and-so. So I knew that he meant the Arabs, because the non-Arabs have no tribes."
"7431","Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin al-Misri, my father narrated to me from his father from his grandfather Rishdin ibn Sa'd from Amr ibn al-Harith from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Mansur ibn Abi Mansur, who narrated to him that Suhar ibn Sakhr al-Abdi narrated to him, that he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): We are in a land with abundant breads and greens, and we drink nabidh with that. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Drink of it what does not take away reason and wealth."
"7432","Narrated Ahmad ibn Rishdin from Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi, Hafs ibn Sulayman narrated to us from Mis'ar from Mus'ab ibn al-Muthanna from Suhar ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: O Suhar ibn Abbas, make your drink wholesome, and give drink to your neighbour."
"7433","Those named Sila. Sila ibn al-Harith al-Ghifari. Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri from Haywa ibn Shurayh, al-Harith ibn Shaddad al-San'ani narrated to me that Abu Salih Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ghifari informed him that Sulaym ibn Itr al-Tujibi used to preach to the people while standing. Sila ibn al-Harith al-Ghifari, who was among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said to him: By Allah, we did not abandon the covenant of our Prophet, nor sever our kinship, until you and your companions stood up among us."
"7434","Those named Sirma. Sirma al-Udhri. Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti, Abd al-Hamid ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: I heard Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman narrating from Sirma al-Udhri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Banu al-Mustaliq, and we took noble Arab women captive. We strongly desired enjoyment, and bachelorhood had become hard upon us, so we wanted to enjoy ourselves and practise withdrawal. Some of us said to others: It is not right for us to do this while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is among us, until we ask him. So we asked him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Practise withdrawal or do not practise it; whatever soul Allah has written to come into being until the Day of Resurrection, it will come into being."
"7435","Those named Salih. Salih Shuqran, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). What Shuqran, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated. Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Zayd ibn Akhzam, Uthman al-Ghatafani narrated to us, saying: I heard Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrating from his father, Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi' informed me, saying: I heard Shuqran, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: By Allah, I placed the blanket under the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7436","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Harithi narrated to me, Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Abi Khalaf al-Baghdadi, al-Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father from Shuqran, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I saw him, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), facing towards Khaybar upon a donkey, praying, gesturing with gestures."
"7437","Sa'sa'a ibn Mu'awiya ibn Husayn ibn Ubada ibn Nazzal ibn Murra ibn Ubayd ibn al-Harith ibn Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Zayd Manat ibn Tamim ibn Murra, paternal uncle of al-Ahnaf ibn Qays; he used to reside in Basra. Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Hudba ibn Khalid narrated to me, Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us from al-Hasan from Sa'sa'a ibn Mu'awiya, uncle of al-Ahnaf, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him reciting this verse: So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it. I said: By Allah, I do not care if I hear nothing else; it suffices me, it suffices me."
"7438","Sa'sa'a ibn Najiya ibn Iqal ibn Muhammad ibn Sufyan ibn Mujashi' ibn Darim, grandfather of al-Farazdaq the poet; he used to reside in Basra. Narrated Muhammad ibn Zakariyya al-Ghallabi from al-Ala ibn al-Fadl, and Abu Umayya Salm ibn Isam al-Asbahani al-Thaqafi narrated to me from al-Abbas ibn al-Faraj al-Riyashi, al-Ala ibn al-Fadl ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, Abbad ibn Kusayb Abu al-Hisab al-Anbari narrated to us, Tufayl ibn Amr al-Raba'i, Rabi'a ibn Malik ibn Hanzala, brothers of Ujayf, narrated to us from Sa'sa'a ibn Najiya al-Mujashi'i, who was the grandfather of al-Farazdaq ibn Ghalib ibn Sa'sa'a, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he presented Islam to me, so I became Muslim, and he taught me verses of the Qur'an. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I did deeds in the pre-Islamic period; is there reward for me in them? He said: And what did you do? I said: Two pregnant she-camels of mine strayed, so I went out seeking them upon a camel of mine. Two tents in an open land were raised before me, so I headed towards them. I found in one of them an old man, and I said: Have you seen two pregnant she-camels? He said: What are their brands? I said: The brand of Ibn Darim. He said: We have found your she-camels, and made them give birth and multiplied them, and Allah has benefited thereby a household of your people from the Arabs of Mudar. While he was speaking to me, a woman from the other tent called out that she had given birth. He said: And what did she give birth to? If it is a boy, he has shared with us in our people, and if it is a girl, we will bury her alive. She said: A girl. I said: And what is this buried-alive girl? He said: A daughter of mine. I said: I will buy her from you. He said: O brother of Banu Tamim, do you say you will buy your daughter, though I have told you that I am a man of the Arabs of Mudar? I said: I am not buying her person from you; I am only buying her soul so that you do not kill her. He said: For what will you buy her? I said: These two she-camels of mine and their offspring. He said: And will you add this camel of yours? I said: Yes, on condition that you send a messenger with me, and when I reach my family I will return the camel to you. So he did. When I reached my family I returned the camel to him. One night I reflected to myself that this is a noble deed, none of the Arabs preceded me to it. Islam appeared, and I had saved alive three hundred and sixty buried-alive girls, buying each of them for two pregnant she-camels and a camel. Is there reward for me in that? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have its reward since Allah blessed you with Islam."
"7439","Narrated Bakr ibn Muqbil al-Basri, Muhammad ibn Marzuq informed us, Abdullah ibn Harb narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn As'ad, nicknamed Ibn Daha, narrated to me, Iqal ibn Shabba ibn Iqal ibn Sa'sa'a ibn Najiya al-Mujashi'i narrated to me, my father narrated to me from my grandfather from his father Sa'sa'a ibn Najiya al-Mujashi'i, who said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, sometimes a surplus remains for me which I store for calamity and the wayfarer. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your mother and your father, your sister and your brother, the nearest, the nearest."
"7440","Those named Sunabih. Sunabih ibn al-A'sar al-Bajali, then al-Ahmasi; he used to reside in Kufa. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Nu'man, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Mujalid ibn Sa'id narrated to us from Qays ibn Abi Hazim from al-Sunabih, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am your forerunner at the Pond, and I will vie with the nations through you in numbers; so do not turn back after me as disbelievers, striking the necks of one another."
"7441","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated to me from al-Sunabih, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am your forerunner at the Pond, and I will vie with the nations through you in numbers, so do not fight one another after me."
"7442","Narrated Abdullah ibn Sa'id ibn Yahya al-Raqqi from Abu Farwa Yazid ibn Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sinan al-Rahawi, my father narrated to me from his father from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid from Qays ibn Abi Hazim from al-Sunabih al-Ahmasi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am your forerunner at the Pond, vying with the nations through you in numbers; do not fight after me."
"7443","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Ibn al-Asbahani, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us from Mujalid ibn Sa'id from Qays ibn Abi Hazim from al-Sunabih, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a fine she-camel among the charity camels and said: May Allah destroy the owner of this she-camel. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I took it back in exchange for a camel from the edge of the herd. He said: Then yes."
"7444","Narrated Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh, Waki' narrated to us from al-Salt ibn Bahram from al-Harith ibn Wahb from al-Sunabih, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My nation will remain in a hold of its religion as long as they do not wait for the Maghrib until the stars interlace, in imitation of the Jews, and as long as they do not delay the Fajr in imitation of the Christians."
"7445","Chapter. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger."
"7446","Narrated Harun ibn Kamil al-Misri from Abdullah ibn Salih, al-Layth narrated to me, Yunus narrated to me from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger."
"7447","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to me, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, freedman of Muzayna, narrated to us from Safwan ibn Sulaym from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger."
"7448","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Muhriz ibn Salama, al-Mughira ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us from his father from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger."
"7449","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Wahb ibn Baqiyya, Khalid informed us from Muhammad ibn Amr from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger."
"7450","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Amr ibn Uthman al-Himsi, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, al-Zubaydi narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger."
"7451","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Ali ibn Shu'ayb al-Sammar, Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us from Ishaq ibn Rashid from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the sanctuary, and he said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and His Messenger."
"7452","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ja'far al-Qattat al-Kufi from Minjab ibn al-Harith, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, Jarir narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and for His Messenger."
"7453","Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli, Salama ibn Rawh narrated to us from Uqayl from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and for His Messenger."
"7454","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Yahya ibn Durust, Muhammad ibn Thabit narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sanctuary except for Allah and for His Messenger."
"7455","Chapter. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was at al-Abwa, and I gifted him a wild donkey; he returned it to me. When he saw dislike in my face, he said: It is not our rejecting you, but we are in ihram."
"7456","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qa'nabi from Malik, and narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abdullah ibn Yusuf, Malik informed us from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that he gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while he was at al-Abwa or at Waddan, a wild donkey. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw what was in my face, he said: We have not returned it except that we are in ihram."
"7457","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih, al-Layth narrated to me, Ibn Shihab narrated to me from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas, that al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him at al-Abwa or at Waddan, so I gifted him a wild donkey, and he returned it to me. When he saw dislike in my face, he said: It is not our rejecting you, but we are in ihram."
"7458","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi from Abdullah ibn Salih, al-Layth narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Mas'ud from Abdullah ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama ibn Qays al-Laythi, that a wild donkey was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at al-Abwa, and he returned it to its owner, then said: There is no rejecting you from us, but we are in ihram."
"7459","Narrated Amr ibn Hafs al-Sadusi from Asim ibn Ali, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that he gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey, and he returned it to him. When he saw what was in my face from returning his gift, he said: Indeed there is no rejecting you from us, but we are in ihram."
"7460","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey which I hunted at Waddan, and I gifted it to him at al-Abwa. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned it to me, and saw what was in my face from how hard that was upon me, and said: We have not returned it to you out of dislike for it, but we are in ihram."
"7461","Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzaz, and narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Yahya ibn Durust, both saying: Muhammad ibn Thabit al-Abdi narrated to us from Amr ibn Dinar from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that a man gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wild donkey meat, and he returned it to him, and said: We are in ihram."
"7462","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Wahb ibn Baqiyya, Khalid informed us from Muhammad ibn Amr from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that he gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey, and he returned it. He saw that in his face, and said: We have not returned it to you except that we are in ihram."
"7463","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani from Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali, Abu Asim narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, Ibn Abi Labid informed me from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I gifted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey, and he returned it to me. When he saw what was in my face, he said: It is not our rejecting you, but we are in ihram."
"7464","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Ali ibn Shu'ayb al-Simsar, Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us from Ishaq ibn Rashid from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey while he was in ihram, and he returned it to me. When he saw what was in my face, he said: There is no rejecting you from us, but we are in ihram."
"7465","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Ubaydullah ibn Sa'd, my uncle narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah, nephew of al-Zuhri, narrated to us from his uncle, Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba informed me that Abdullah ibn Abbas used to say: I heard al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama say: I gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey at al-Abwa, and he returned it to me. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recognized dislike of his returning it in my face, he said: It is not our rejecting you, but we are in ihram."
"7466","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Ubaydullah ibn Sa'd, my uncle narrated to us, my father narrated to us from Salih ibn Kaysan from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, that Abdullah ibn Abbas informed him that al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama ibn Qays informed him: That he gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey while he was at Waddan, and he returned it."
"7467","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Amr ibn Uthman al-Himsi, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, al-Zubaydi narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who informed him that he gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey while he was at al-Abwa or at Waddan. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned it to me. When he recognized his returning it in my face, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not our rejecting you, but we are in ihram."
"7468","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti and Abu Zur'a Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi, both saying: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, Jarir narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a leg of a wild donkey while he was at Waddan, and he returned it to me. When he saw what was in my face, he said: We have not returned it to you except that we are in ihram."
"7469","Narrated Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Mahshi from Ubaydullah ibn Sa'id ibn Ufayr, my father narrated to me, my maternal uncle al-Mughira ibn al-Hasan ibn Rashid al-Hashimi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Salim narrated to me from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab narrated to me from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama ibn Qays, who narrated to him that he gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey while he was at Waddan or at al-Abwa, and he returned it to him. He recognized in my face grief at his returning it to me, and said: There is no rejecting you from us, but we are in ihram."
"7470","Narrated Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tustari from Ali ibn Harb al-Mawsili, Harun ibn Imran narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us from Ata from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by while I was at al-Abwa, and I gifted him a wild donkey, and he returned it to me. When he saw dislike in my face, he said: It is not our rejecting you, but we are in ihram."
"7471","Chapter. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we hit among the offspring of the polytheists in night raids. He said: They are of them."
"7472","Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam, Sufyan ibn Uyayna informed us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, we approach a monastery at night of the polytheists, with them are their women and children, and we kill them. He said: They are of them."
"7473","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Ubayd, Hajjaj narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Ibn Shihab informed him from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was told: Horses raided at night and hit among the children of the polytheists. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: They are of their fathers."
"7474","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Yahya ibn Durust, Muhammad ibn Thabit narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about horses trampling the children of the polytheists at night. He said: They are of their fathers."
"7475","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal and Arim Abu al-Nu'man, both saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar informed us from Ibn Abbas, who said: They said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), our horses trampled children of the polytheists. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are of their fathers. And he did not mention al-Zuhri, nor Ubaydullah, nor al-Sa'b."
"7476","Narrated Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari from Ali ibn Shu'ayb al-Simsar, Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us from Ishaq ibn Rashid from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a detachment hitting offspring in the confusion of a raid. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Their children are of them. Then he forbade killing offspring on the day of Khaybar."
"7477","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj from Ali ibn al-Ja'd, al-Zanji ibn Khalid narrated to us, saying: I heard Ibn Shihab narrating from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that he said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Messenger of Allah, we approach a house of the polytheists at night, with them are their children and women, and we kill them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are with their fathers."
"7478","Narrated Isma'il ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf from Ahmad ibn Salih, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd informed me from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba from Ibn Abbas, that al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama said: O Messenger of Allah, horses in the confusion of a raid hit among the children of the polytheists. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are of them, or with the fathers."
"7479","Narrated Sahl ibn Musa Shiran al-Ramahurmuzi from Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd narrated to us from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the enemy attacked at night, when among their women and children are hit in the raid. He said: They are of them. And he forbade killing women and children."
"7480","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti and Abu Zur'a al-Dimashqi, both saying: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ja'far al-Kufi from Minjab ibn al-Harith, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, Jarir narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about offspring in the houses of the polytheists, which we approach at night; what of those under the raid among the children? He said: They are of them."
"7481","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Mattuyah al-Asbahani from Yahya ibn Uthman al-Himsi, Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us from Sulayman from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amin from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, we hit the children of the polytheists in the night raid. He said: Do not do that again, and there is no harm; for their children are of them."
"7482","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Dawud ibn Amr al-Dabbi narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas, that al-Sa'b ibn Jaththama said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, a house of the houses of the polytheists which we attack at dawn with a raid, and we hit children under the bellies of horses without knowing? He said: They are of them."
"7483","Sudayy ibn al-Ajlan Abu Umama al-Bahili. He settled in Sham and died there. And of his reports. Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqa from Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Maqabiri, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, Shurahbil ibn Muslim narrated to me, saying: I heard Abu Umama al-Bahili Sudayy ibn Ajlan ibn Amr ibn Wahb."
"7484","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Jawhari from Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Minqari, al-Asma'i narrated to us, saying: Abu Umama al-Bahili al-Sudayy ibn Ajlan is of a clan called Banu Sahm ibn Amr, a branch of Bani Qutayba."
"7485","Narrated Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri from Yahya ibn Bukayr, saying: Abu Umama al-Bahili, may Allah have mercy on him, whose name is Sudayy ibn Ajlan, died in the year eighty-six; his age was ninety-one."
"7486","What Abu Umama narrated. Abdullah ibn Busr al-Yahsibi from Abu Umama. Narrated Abu Yazid Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratisli from Nu'aym ibn Hammad, and narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Mu'adh ibn Asad, both saying: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Safwan ibn Amr narrated to us from Abdullah ibn Busr from Abu Umama, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the saying of Allah, Mighty and Exalted: And he will be given to drink of festering water which he will sip. He said: It will be brought near to him and he will hate it. When it is brought near to him, it will scorch his face and the skin of his head will fall off. When he drinks it, it will cut his intestines until it comes out from his back. Allah, Mighty and Exalted, says: And they will be given to drink boiling water which will cut their intestines. Allah, Mighty and Exalted, says: And if they cry for help, they will be helped with water like molten brass which will scorch the faces; evil the drink."
"7487","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from his father, and narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Abd al-Wahhab ibn al-Dahhak, both saying: Baqiyya narrated to us from Safwan ibn Amr from Abdullah ibn Busr from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Make Allah beloved to His servants, and Allah will love you."
"7488","Narrated Muhammad ibn Harun ibn Muhammad ibn Bakkar al-Dimashqi from al-Walid ibn Utba, Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us from Safwan ibn Amr, Abdullah ibn Busr narrated to me from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Make Allah beloved to His servants, and Allah will love you."
"7489","Raja ibn Haywa from Abu Umama. Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, and narrated Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Kaysan al-Missisi from Hibban ibn Hilal, both saying: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Ya'qub narrated to us, Raja ibn Haywa narrated to us from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prepared an expedition, so I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah for me for martyrdom. He said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils. So we fought and were safe. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prepared another expedition, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah for me for martyrdom. He said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils. So we fought and were safe and gained spoils. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prepared a third expedition, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I came to you twice asking you to supplicate for me for martyrdom, and you said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils, so we fought and were safe and gained spoils. Then I said: O Messenger of Allah, command me with a deed which I may take from you. He said: Upon you is fasting, for there is nothing like it. So Abu Umama and his wife and his servant were found only fasting; when they saw fire or smoke in their house, they knew that a guest had come to them. Then I came to him after that and said: O Messenger of Allah, you commanded me with a matter which I hope Allah will benefit me through; so command me with another matter through which Allah will benefit me. He said: Know that you will not prostrate to Allah a single prostration but Allah will raise you a degree thereby and remove a sin from you thereby."
"7490","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Abd al-Razzaq from Hisham ibn Hassan from Muhammad ibn Abi Ya'qub from Raja ibn Haywa from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition, and I went out among them. I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to grant me martyrdom. He said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils. So we were safe and gained spoils. Then he sent another army, and I went out among them, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to grant me martyrdom. He said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils. Then the third time like that, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I came to you three times asking you to supplicate for me for martyrdom, and you said: O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils, so we were safe and gained spoils. O Messenger of Allah, command me with a deed. He said: Upon you is fasting, for there is nothing like it and no equal to it. Abu Umama said: He was granted much good from that."
"7491","Narrated Ishaq ibn Jamil al-Asbahani from Ahmad ibn Mani', Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us from Wasil from Ibn Abi Ya'qub from Raja ibn Haywa from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prepared an expedition, and he mentioned it."
"7492","Abu Idris al-Khawlani from Abu Umama. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Salih, Mu'awiya ibn Salih narrated to us from Rabi'a ibn Yazid from Abu Idris al-Khawlani from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Upon you is night prayer, for it is the practice of the righteous before you, and it is nearness for you to your Lord, and expiation for sins, and prevention from sin."
"7493","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from al-Hakam ibn Musa, Yahya ibn Hamza narrated to us, and narrated al-Hasan ibn Jarir al-Suri from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi, Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, both from Ishaq ibn Abi Farwa from Makhul from Hafs ibn Sa'id ibn Jabir from Abu Idris from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever introduces satire against Islam in Islam, cut off his tongue. Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal said: Meaning: he satirizes Islam."
"7494","Abu Salih al-Ash'ari from Abu Umama. Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam, and narrated Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Razi from Ali ibn al-Ja'd, both saying: Abu Uthman Muhammad ibn Mutarrif narrated to us, Abu al-Husayn al-Shami narrated to us from Abu Salih al-Ash'ari from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Fever is a blast from Hell; whatever befalls the believer of it is his share of the Fire."
"7495","Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated al-Hasan ibn Sahl al-Mujawwiz al-Basri from Abu Asim from Thawr ibn Yazid from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the supper was lifted from before him, would say: Praise be to Allah, much, good, blessed therein, not sufficed, nor bidden farewell, nor dispensed with, our Lord."
"7496","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nu'aym, Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us from Thawr ibn Yazid from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his table, he would say: Praise be to Allah, much, good, blessed therein, not sufficed, nor bidden farewell, nor dispensed with, our Lord."
"7497","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati and Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, both saying: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, Mu'awiya ibn Salih narrated to me from Amir ibn Jashib from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say at the finishing of food: Praise be to Allah, much praise, good, blessed therein, not sufficed, nor bidden farewell, nor dispensed with."
"7498","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from his father, Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Bishr ibn Nu'aym al-Khaylani narrated to me, Amir ibn Jashib narrated to me from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to say at the finishing of his food: Praise be to Allah, much praise, good, blessed therein, not sufficed, nor bidden farewell, nor dispensed with, our Lord."
"7499","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Hisham ibn Ammar, Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever goes to the mosque intending only to learn good or teach it, he will have like the reward of a pilgrim whose pilgrimage is complete."
"7500","Narrated Muhammad ibn Harun ibn Muhammad ibn Bakkar al-Dimashqi, saying: al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Khallal al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Quddus narrated to us, Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Days will not pass until a group of my nation drinks wine, calling it by other than its name."
"7501","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Na'ila al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn al-Mughira, al-Nu'man narrated to us, Abu Sa'id narrated it to us from Sufyan al-Thawri from Thawr ibn Yazid from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah, Mighty and Exalted, blames incapacity; so exert yourself with effort, and if you are overcome, say: I have relied upon Allah, or: Allah suffices me, and He is the best Disposer."
"7502","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi from Hisham ibn Ammar, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Bahir ibn Sa'd from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whatever a man spends upon his house, family, children, and servants is charity for him."
"7503","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi from Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri, Sadaqa ibn Abdullah narrated to us from Thawr ibn Yazid from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Exalted, loves gentleness and is pleased with it, and helps through it in what He does not help through harshness."
"7504","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi, both saying: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Khalid ibn Yazid ibn Abi Malik narrated to us from his father from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no servant who enters Paradise but women of the wide-eyed maidens sit at his head and at his feet, singing to him with the most beautiful voice heard by jinn and men; it is not with the flutes of Satan, but with glorifying Allah and declaring Him holy."
"7505","Narrated Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani from Suwayd ibn Sa'id, Khalid ibn Yazid ibn Abi Malik narrated to us from his father from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Do the people of Paradise have intercourse? He said: Vigorously, but there is no semen and no death."
"7506","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Mattuyah al-Asbahani and Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi, Muhammad ibn Hafs al-Awsabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us, Safwan ibn Amr narrated to us from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies guarding the frontier in the path of Allah, Allah will secure him from the trial of the grave."
"7507","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sarraj al-Askari from Isma'il ibn Ibrahim al-Tarjuman, Muhammad ibn Mihsan al-Ukkashi narrated to us, Safwan ibn Amr narrated to us from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, Abu Umama narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Modesty and lack of eloquence are of faith, and they bring near to Paradise and distance from the Fire. Obscenity and foul speech are of Satan, and they bring near to the Fire and distance from Paradise. A Bedouin said to Abu Umama: We say in poetry that lack of eloquence is of foolishness. He said: Do you see me saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, while you beautify with your foul poetry."
"7508","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi, Jami' ibn Thawb narrated to us from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no man whose face becomes dusty in the path of Allah but Allah will secure him from the smoke of the Fire on the Day of Resurrection, and there is no man whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah but Allah will secure his feet from the Fire on the Day of Resurrection."
"7509","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda from Yahya ibn Salih, Jami' ibn Thawb narrated to us from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: What a good man I am for the wicked of my nation. A man of his companions said to him: How are you, O Messenger of Allah, for the best of them? He said: As for the wicked of my nation, Allah will admit them to Paradise through my intercession, and as for the best of them, Allah will admit them to Paradise through their deeds."
"7510","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Mattuyah and Ibrahim ibn Irq al-Himsi, both saying: Muhammad ibn Hafs al-Awsabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us from Hariz ibn Uthman from Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays Friday prayer, and fasts his day, and visits a sick person, and attends a funeral, and attends a marriage, Paradise becomes due for him."
"7511","Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi, judge of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Umama Sudayy ibn Ajlan. Narrated Abu Zur'a Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Abu Mushir, and narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Yusuf, both saying: Khalid ibn Yazid ibn Subayh narrated to us from Salim ibn Abdullah al-Muharibi from Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no servant struck down by a bout of illness but Allah raises him from it pure."
"7512","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Ahmad ibn Hanbal, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Isma'il ibn Abdullah narrated to me that Sulayman ibn Habib narrated to them from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The bonds of Islam will be undone bond by bond; every time a bond is undone, people will cling to the one next to it. The first of them to be undone is rule, and the last of them is prayer."
"7513","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Abi Hassan al-Anmati, both saying: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Hammad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Khalid ibn al-Zibriqan narrated to us from Sulayman ibn Habib from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The townspeople sitting in the path of Allah are a support for the Muslims and their frontier; so do not overcharge them, and do not hoard, and a townsman must not sell for a Bedouin, and a man must not outbid his brother, and must not propose over his proposal, and a woman must not overturn her sister's vessel; for each one's sustenance is upon Allah, Mighty and Exalted."
"7514","Narrated Abu Zur'a Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Abu al-Jumahir, Abu Ka'b al-Sa'di narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi narrated to us from Abu Umama, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I guarantee a house in the outskirts of Paradise for whoever abandons arguing, even if he is right; and a house in the middle of Paradise for whoever abandons lying, even if joking; and a house in the highest part of Paradise for whoever has good character."
"7515","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and Abdan ibn Ahmad, both saying: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Hammad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Khalid ibn al-Zibriqan narrated to us from Sulayman ibn Habib from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Four are cursed by Allah above His Throne, and His angels say amen upon them: He who fortifies himself against women, neither marrying nor taking a concubine so that no child is born to him; and a man who imitates women though Allah created him male; and a woman who imitates men though Allah, Mighty and Exalted, created her female; and one who misleads the poor. Khalid ibn al-Zibriqan said: Meaning he who mocks them, saying to the poor man: Come, I will give you, then when the man comes to him he says: I have nothing with me; and saying to the blind man: Beware of the well, beware of the beast, though there is nothing before him; and a man asking about the house of a people, and he directs him to another."
"7516","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Jarir al-Suri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Ghaffar al-Bayruti, Rawaha bint Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Awza'i narrated to me, my father narrated to me, saying: I heard Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi say: Abu Umama narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: O Allah, I ask You for a soul tranquil through You, believing in meeting You, pleased with Your decree, content with Your giving."
"7517","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Amr ibn Hashim al-Bayruti from al-Awza'i, Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi narrated to me from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three, whoever has one of them is guaranteed by Allah, Mighty and Exalted: Whoever goes out in the path of Allah is guaranteed by Allah; if He takes his soul, He will admit him to Paradise, and if He returns him to his family, then with what he obtained of reward or spoils. And a man who is in the mosque is guaranteed by Allah; if He takes his soul, He will admit him to Paradise, and if He returns him to his family, then with what he obtained of reward or spoils. And a man who enters his house with peace is guaranteed by Allah, Mighty and Exalted."
"7518","Narrated Abu Zur'a al-Dimashqi from Abu Mushir, Isma'il ibn Abdullah ibn Sama'a narrated to us from al-Awza'i from Sulayman ibn Habib from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7519","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abdullah ibn Yusuf, Kulthum ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi, who said: I went out fighting, and when I passed by Hims, I went out to the market to buy what a traveller cannot do without. When I looked at the door of the mosque, I said: If only I entered and prayed two rak'as. When I entered, I saw Thabit ibn Ma'bad, and Ibn Abi Zakariyya, and Makhul among a group of the people of Damascus. When I saw them, I came to them and sat with them. They spoke something, then said: We intend Abu Umama al-Bahili. They stood and I stood with them, and we entered upon him. He was an old man grown thin and aged, yet his intellect and speech were better than what we saw of his appearance. The first thing he narrated to us was that he said: This sitting of yours is of Allah's conveyance to you and His proof upon you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conveyed what he was sent with, and his Companions conveyed what they heard; so convey what you hear: Three, all of them guaranteed by Allah, Mighty and Exalted: A man who goes out in the path of Allah is guaranteed by Allah until He admits him to Paradise, or with what he obtained of reward or spoils. And a man who enters his house with peace. Then he said: In Hell there is a bridge with seven arches; upon the middle of them is judgment. The servant will be brought until, when he reaches the middle arch, he will be told: What debt is upon you? And he recited this verse: And they will not conceal any statement from Allah. He said: He will say: O Lord, upon me is so-and-so. He will be told: Pay your debt. He will say: I have nothing, and I do not know what to pay? It will be said: Take from his good deeds. He will continue to be taken from his good deeds until none of his good deeds remain. When his good deeds are exhausted, it will be said: His good deeds are exhausted. It will be said: Take from the sins of those who claim against him, and heap them upon him. It has reached me that men will come with good deeds like mountains, yet it will continue to be taken for those who claim against them until none of their good deeds remain."
"7520","Narrated Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi from Ali ibn al-Husayn al-Mawsili, Anbasa ibn Abi Saghir narrated to us from al-Awza'i from Sulayman ibn Habib and al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymir, both saying: We heard Abu Umama al-Bahili say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Banners will come out from the east for the sons of Abbas; the first of them is destroyed and the last of them is destroyed. Do not help them; may Allah not help them. Whoever walks under one of their banners, Allah will admit him to Hell on the Day of Resurrection. Indeed they are the worst of Allah's creation, and their followers are the worst of Allah's creation. They claim that they are of me; indeed I am free of them, and they are free of me. Their sign: they lengthen hair and wear black; so do not sit with them in gatherings, and do not trade with them in markets, and do not guide them to the road, and do not give them water to drink. The people of heaven are harmed by their takbir."
"7521","Narrated Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi from Ali ibn al-Husayn, Anbasa ibn Abi Saghir narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us from Sulayman ibn Habib, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be four truces between you and the Romans; the fourth will stand at the hand of a man of the people of Heraclius, lasting seven years. A man of Abd al-Qays called al-Mustawrid ibn Khilan said to him: O Messenger of Allah, who is the leader of people on that day? He said: Of forty years' offspring, as if his face is a brilliant star, on his right cheek a black mole, upon him two Qatwani cloaks, as if he is of the men of the sons of Isra'il; he will rule twenty years, extracting treasures and conquering the cities of polytheism."
"7522","Narrated Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Musarrih al-Harrani from Mu'allal ibn Nufayl al-Harrani, Muhammad ibn Mihsan al-Ukkashi narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, saying: I heard Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi say: I heard Abu Umama al-Bahili say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When prayer is established, the gates of heaven are opened and supplication is answered. When one who leaves prayer finishes and does not say: O Allah, protect me from the Fire, admit me to Paradise, and marry me to the wide-eyed maidens, the Fire says: Woe to him, was he unable to seek refuge with Allah from Hell? And Paradise says: Woe to him, was he unable to ask Allah for Paradise? And the wide-eyed maidens say: Woe to him, was he unable to ask Allah to marry him to the wide-eyed maidens?"
"7523","Rashid ibn Sa'd al-Maqra'i from Abu Umama. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Salih, Mu'awiya ibn Salih narrated to me from Rashid ibn Sa'd from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Fear the insight of the believer, for he sees with the light of Allah."
"7524","Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Yusuf ibn Adi, Bishr ibn Ammar narrated to us from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim from Rashid ibn Sa'd from Abu Umama and Abu al-Darda, both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The slaughter of the foetus is the slaughter of its mother."
"7525","Narrated Abu Yazid Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratisli from al-Mu'alla ibn al-Walid al-Qa'qa'i, Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani narrated to us from Rashid ibn Sa'd, who said: Abu Umama met me and took my hand, then said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me and took my hand, then said: O Abu Umama, among the believers are those for whom my heart softens."
"7526","Narrated Ibrahim Duhaym al-Dimashqi, my father narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Sarh narrated to us, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani, Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, both from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam from Rashid ibn Sa'd and Damra ibn Habib, that they heard Abu Umama say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me what to say upon finishing food. He said: O Allah, You have fed us, given us drink, and quenched us; so praise be to You, not sufficed, nor bidden farewell, nor dispensed with."
"7527","Narrated Musa ibn Isa ibn al-Mundhir al-Himsi from his father, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam from Rashid ibn Sa'd from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7528","Narrated Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Sa'id Abu Umar al-Darir al-Kufi from Ahmad ibn Yunus, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from al-Hasan ibn Dinar from al-Khasib ibn Jahdar from Rashid ibn Sa'd from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no god worshipped under the shade of heaven besides Allah greater in enormity with Allah than followed desire."
"7529","Narrated Muhammad ibn Harun ibn Muhammad ibn Bakkar al-Dimashqi from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Khallal al-Dimashqi, Marwan ibn Muhammad al-Tahiri narrated to us, Rishdin ibn Sa'd narrated to us from Mu'awiya ibn Salih from Rashid ibn Sa'd from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Water is not made impure by anything except what overcomes its smell or taste."
"7530","Damra ibn Habib from Abu Umama. Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiya al-Utbi al-Misri from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi, Abdullah ibn Raja al-Shaybani narrated to us from al-Safr ibn Nusayr from Damra ibn Habib from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Your Lord, Exalted, said: When I take the precious thing of My servant, while he is attached to it, and he praises Me for that, I am not pleased with a reward for him less than Paradise."
"7531","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Missisi from Muhammad ibn Awf al-Himsi, Abu al-Mughira narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja al-Shaybani narrated to us from al-Safr ibn Nusayr from Damra ibn Habib ibn Suhayb from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever testifies that I am the Messenger of Allah, let him not attend prayer withholding urine until he relieves himself. And whoever testifies that I am the Messenger of Allah, when he leads a people, let him not single himself out with supplication. And whoever testifies that I am the Messenger of Allah, let him not enter a household until he seeks permission and greets; and when he looks into the bottom of the house, he has entered."
"7532","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Khaythama from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yunus al-Raqqi, Uqba ibn Alqama narrated to us from Artat ibn al-Mundhir from Damra ibn Habib from Abu Umama, who said: A man died at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and no shroud was found for him. They came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Look into the inside of his waist-wrapper. A dinar or two dinars were found. He said: Two brands; pray over your companion. A man said: Its payment is upon me, O Messenger of Allah. So he prayed over him."
"7533","Yazid ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami from Abu Umama. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati and Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb al-Azdi, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, Mu'awiya ibn Salih narrated to me from al-Safr ibn Nusayr from Yazid ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should come to prayer while withholding urine until he relieves himself. And whoever puts his eyes into a house without its people's permission has destroyed. And whoever prays with a people and singles himself out with a supplication to their exclusion has betrayed them."
"7534","Abu Utba al-Kindi from Abu Umama. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Salih, Mu'awiya ibn Salih narrated to me from Abu Utba al-Kindi, that he heard Abu Umama say: A man died at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), leaving two dinars as debt upon him, with no means to pay. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused to pray over him, and said: Pray over your companion. Abu Qatada stood up to him and said: I will pay it off. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed over him. And he also mentioned that a man died at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), leaving two dinars. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two brands."
"7535","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani from Abd al-Rahman ibn Umar Rustah, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Mu'awiya ibn Salih narrated to us from Abu Utba al-Kindi from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, will you recognize your nation on the Day of Resurrection? He said: Yes. I said: Those you have seen, and those you have not seen? He said: Those I have seen, and those I have not seen? I said: By what? He said: Radiant, with light in limbs from the traces of ablution."
"7536","Habib ibn Ubayd al-Rahabi from Abu Umama. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abdullah ibn Salih, Mu'awiya ibn Salih narrated to me from Habib ibn Ubayd al-Rahabi from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He who hopes for the days of Allah does not enjoy silk."
"7537","Narrated Wa'ila ibn al-Hasan al-Irqi from Kathir ibn Ubayd, Baqiyya narrated to us from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam from Habib ibn Ubayd from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: He who hopes for the days of Allah does not enjoy silk."
"7538","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti and Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi, both saying: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Jami' ibn Thawb al-Rahabi narrated to us from Habib ibn Ubayd from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be men of my nation who eat varieties of food, drink varieties of drink, wear varieties of clothing, and speak with affectation; they are the worst of my nation."
"7539","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Hafs al-Awsabi, Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us from Habib ibn Ubayd from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be men of my nation who eat varieties of food, drink varieties of drink, wear varieties of clothing, and speak with affectation; they are the worst of my nation."
"7540","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father, Amr ibn al-Sarh narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us from Rashid ibn Sa'd and Habib ibn Ubayd, that they heard Abu Umama say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me what to say upon my finishing food. He said: Say: O Allah, You have fed us and quenched us; so praise be to You, not denied, nor dispensed with, our Lord."
"7541","Shurayh ibn Ubayd from Abu Umama. Narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Ayyash, who told me from his father from Damdam ibn Zur'a from Shurayh ibn Ubayd, who was informed by Jubayr ibn Nufayr, Kathir ibn Murra, Amr ibn al-Aswad, al-Miqdam ibn Ma'dikarib, and Abu Umama that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, is this matter only among your people? He said: Yes. He said: Then enjoin them to treat us well. So he said to Quraysh: I warn you by Allah lest you be harsh upon my umma after me. Then he said to the people: There will be rulers after me, so render them their obedience, for the ruler is like a shield to be protected by; if they are righteous and God-fearing and command you to good, it is for you and for them, and if they do evil and command you, it is upon them and you are free of them; and when the ruler seeks out suspicion among the people, he corrupts them."
"7542","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi from Hisham ibn Ammar from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Damdam ibn Zur'a from Shurayh ibn Ubayd from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, Kathir ibn Murra, Amr ibn al-Aswad, al-Miqdad ibn Ma'diykarib, and Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When the imam seeks out suspicion among the people, he corrupts them."
"7543","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti, who told me from his father from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Damdam ibn Zur'a from Shurayh ibn Ubayd from al-Harith ibn al-Harith, Kathir ibn Murra, Amr ibn al-Aswad, and Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The best of the imams of Quraysh are the best of the imams of the people."
"7544","Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani from Abu Umama. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Amr ibn Hashim al-Bayruti from Idris ibn Ziyad al-Alhani from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani from Abu Umama al-Bahili, that he used to greet everyone he met. He said: I never knew anyone to greet him before he greeted, except a Jew once who hid behind a pillar, then came out and greeted him. Abu Umama said to him: Woe to you, O Jew, what made you do what you did? He said: I saw you to be a man who greets much, so I knew it was a virtue and I wanted to take hold of it. Abu Umama said: Woe to you, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has made the greeting a salutation for our umma and a security for our covenanted people."
"7545","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abdullah ibn Yusuf from Abdullah ibn Salim from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw the furrow of cultivation and said: It does not enter upon a people except that Allah humbles them."
"7546","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani, and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, and narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, both saying: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, and narrated Sulayman ibn al-Hasan al-Attar from Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani, all saying: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, who informed me from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: I heard the Messenger of Allah say: My Lord, Almighty and Exalted, has promised me that He will admit to Paradise of my umma seventy thousand with no reckoning upon them and no punishment, and three handfuls from the handfuls of my Lord, Almighty and Exalted."
"7547","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Sari ibn Mihran al-Daqqaq from al-Hakam ibn Musa, and narrated Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari from Abu Khaythama Mus'ab ibn Said, both saying: Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7548","Narrated Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Mubarak al-Antaki from Abu Taqi Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik al-Himsi from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid, who told me from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah intends good for a servant, He sweetens him with honey. It was said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what is His sweetening him? He said: He opens for him a righteous deed, then takes his soul while upon it."
"7549","Narrated Ishaq ibn Khalawayh al-Wasiti from Ali ibn Bahr from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid, who told me from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while upon his camel al-Jad'a during the Farewell Pilgrimage, say: I enjoin you regarding the neighbor, until he said much, so I said: He will surely make him an heir."
"7550","Narrated Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid, who told me from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to spread the greeting of peace."
"7551","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab from his father, and narrated Ubayd from Ibn Abi Shayba, and narrated Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Khalid ibn Hayyan al-Raqqi from Sufyan ibn Bishr al-Kufi, all saying: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to spread the greeting of peace."
"7552","Narrated Ayyub ibn Abi Sulayman al-Suri, who told me from Atiyya ibn Baqiyya ibn al-Walid, who told me from his father from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I am the forerunner of the Arabs to Paradise, and Suhayb is the forerunner of the Romans to Paradise, and Bilal is the forerunner of the Abyssinians to Paradise, and Salman is the forerunner of the Persians to Paradise."
"7553","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari, both saying: al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Himyar from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has servants whom Allah will seat on the Day of Resurrection upon pulpits of light, and light will cover their faces until He finishes the reckoning of creation."
"7554","Narrated Abu Aqil Anas ibn Salm al-Khawlani from Ubayd ibn Razin al-Ladhiqi, who said: I heard Ismail ibn Ayyash say: Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani told me from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever teaches a servant a verse from the Book of Allah is his master; he should not forsake him nor prefer himself over him."
"7555","Narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ala ibn Zibriq al-Himsi, who told me from my grandfather Ibrahim ibn al-Ala from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rebuke horses gently, for they respond to rebuke."
"7556","Narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ala ibn Zibriq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn Imran al-Kindi from Baqiyya, and narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Ma'mari from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Maymun al-Raqqi from Sulayman ibn Ubaydullah al-Hattab from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Shall I not tell you about al-Khidr? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: While he was one day walking in a market of the Children of Israel, a mukatab man saw him and said: Give charity to me, may Allah bless you. Al-Khidr said: I believe in Allah; whatever Allah wills of a matter shall be; I have nothing with me to give you. The poor man said: I ask you by the Face of Allah to give charity to me, for I saw a mark upon your face and I hoped for blessing with you. Al-Khidr said: I believe in Allah; I have nothing with me to give you except that you take me and sell me. The poor man said: Can this be right? He said: Yes, I speak the truth; you have asked me by a tremendous matter, and I shall not disappoint you by the Face of my Lord; sell me. So he brought him to the market and sold him for four hundred dirhams. He stayed with the buyer a while, who did not employ him in anything. He said to him: You only bought me seeking some good with me, so enjoin me with work. He said: I dislike to burden you, for you are an old, weak shaykh. He said: It is no burden upon me. He said: Then stand and carry these stones, though it would take no fewer than six men a day to carry them. The man went out for some need of his, then returned having carried the stones in an hour. He said: You have done well and beautifully, and you bore what I did not see you able to bear. Then a journey came upon the man, and he said: I reckon you trustworthy, so take my place among my family with a good deputyship. He said: Then enjoin me with work. He said: I dislike to burden you. He said: It is no burden upon me. He said: Then make bricks for my house until I come to you. The man departed on his journey, then returned, and he had built up his building. He said: I ask you by the Face of Allah, what is your way and what is your affair? He said: You asked me by the Face of Allah, and the Face of Allah brought me into bondage. Al-Khidr said: I shall tell you who I am; I am al-Khidr of whom you heard; a poor man asked me for charity and I had nothing with me to give him, so he asked me by the Face of Allah, and I gave him power over my neck, so he sold me; and I tell you that whoever is asked by the Face of Allah and turns away his asker while able, shall stand on the Day of Resurrection with his skin and no flesh upon him and no bone, rattling. The man said: I believe in Allah; I burdened you, O Prophet of Allah, while not knowing. He said: No harm; you did well and preserved. The man said: By my father and mother, O Prophet of Allah, judge regarding my family and wealth with what Allah shows you, or I shall give you choice and free you. He said: I love that you free me so I may worship my Lord. So he freed him. Al-Khidr said: Praise be to Allah who brought me into bondage, then delivered me from it."
"7557","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani from al-Musayyab ibn Wadih from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say during the Farewell Pilgrimage: O people, Allah has given every possessor of a right his right; there is no bequest for an heir."
"7558","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Kaysan al-Missisi from al-Husayn ibn Bishr al-Tarsusi, and narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn al-Ala ibn Zibriq al-Himsi from my uncle Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, and narrated Musa ibn Harun from Harun ibn Dawud al-Najjar al-Tarsusi, all saying: Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us, who told me from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites Ayat al-Kursi after every prescribed prayer, nothing prevents him from entering Paradise except death. Muhammad ibn Ibrahim added in his hadith: and Say: He is Allah, the One."
"7559","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani and Abdullah ibn Sulayman ibn al-Ash'ath, both saying: Muhammad ibn Awf al-Himsi narrated to us from Sulaym ibn Uthman from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says: There is no god but Allah, alone with no partner to Him; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He is over all things Powerful, no deed precedes it and no sin remains with it."
"7560","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani and Abdullah ibn Sulayman ibn al-Ash'ath, both saying: Muhammad ibn Awf al-Himsi narrated to us from Sulaym ibn Uthman from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says: Glory be to Allah and with His praise, it is like a hundred necks freed when he says it a hundred times. And whoever says: Praise be to Allah a hundred times, it equals a hundred horses saddled and bridled in the path of Allah. And whoever says: Allah is Most Great a hundred times, it equals a hundred sacrificial camels slaughtered at Makka."
"7561","Narrated Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Amr ibn Uthman al-Himsi from Ismail ibn Ayyash, who told us from Shurahbil ibn Muslim and Muhammad ibn Ziyad, that they both heard Abu Umama al-Bahili say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O people, there is no prophet after me and no umma after you; worship your Lord, pray your five, fast your month, and give the charity of your wealth with your souls content with it, and obey those in authority over you, you shall enter the Paradise of your Lord."
"7562","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Sadaqa al-Jubalani from al-Yaman ibn Adi from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever strips bare the back of a Muslim man without right shall meet Allah while He is angry with him."
"7563","Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi from Nuh ibn Umar ibn Huwayy al-Saksaki al-Himsi from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, who said: Jibril, peace be upon him, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Tabuk and said: O Muhammad, attend the funeral of Mu'awiya ibn Mu'awiya al-Muzani. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and Jibril, peace be upon him, descended among seventy thousand angels; he placed his right wing upon the mountains and they lowered, and placed his left wing upon the earth and it lowered, until he looked toward Makka and Madina. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over him, as did Jibril and the angels. When he finished he said: O Jibril, what brought Mu'awiya ibn Mu'awiya al-Muzani to this rank? He said: By his reciting: Say: He is Allah, the One, standing and sitting and riding and walking."
"7564","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Musaffa from Baqiyya from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, who said: I heard Abu Umama, while being asked about the ornamentation of swords, whether it is of treasures; he said: Yes, it is of treasures. A man said: This is a foolish old man whose mind has gone. Abu Umama said: I only told you what I heard."
"7565","Abu Sallam al-Aswad from Abu Umama. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ma'mar from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Zayd ibn Sallam from Abu Sallam from Abu Umama, who said: A man said: What is sin, O Messenger of Allah? He said: What pricks in your chest, so leave it. He said: Then what is faith? He said: Whoever is displeased by his evil deed and pleased by his good deed is a believer."
"7566","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Musaffa from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Abu Thawban from Abu Said al-Shami from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Sallam ibn Abi Sallam al-Habashi from Abu Umama, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, what is faith? He said: When your good deed pleases you and your evil deed displeases you, then you are a believer."
"7567","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Abd al-Wahhab ibn al-Dahhak from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Said ibn Yusuf from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Sallam from Abu Umama, who said: A man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do the people of Paradise marry? He said: Yes, and they eat and drink."
"7568","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Muslim ibn Ibrahim from Ali ibn al-Mubarak and Aban ibn Yazid, both saying: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us from Zayd ibn Sallam from Abu Sallam from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite the Qur'an, for it shall come as an intercessor for its companions on the Day of Resurrection; and hold to the two radiant ones: al-Baqara and Al Imran, for they shall come on the Day of Resurrection as though two clouds, or as though two canopies, or as though two flocks of birds in ranks, pleading on behalf of their companion on the Day of Resurrection. Recite Surat al-Baqara, for taking it is blessing and leaving it is regret, and the sorcerers cannot bear it."
"7569","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qazzaz from Musa ibn Ismail from Aban ibn Yazid from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Zayd ibn Sallam from Abu Sallam from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7570","Narrated Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi from Abu Tawba al-Rabi ibn Nafi from Mu'awiya ibn Sallam from Zayd ibn Sallam, that he heard Abu Sallam say: I heard Abu Umama say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Recite the Qur'an, for it shall come on the Day of Resurrection defending its companions. Recite the two radiant ones: al-Baqara and the sura of Al Imran, for they shall come on the Day of Resurrection as though two clouds, or two canopies, or as though two flocks of birds in ranks, pleading on behalf of their companions. Recite Surat al-Baqara, for taking it is blessing and leaving it is regret, and the sorcerers cannot bear it."
"7571","Narrated Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi from Abu Tawba al-Rabi ibn Nafi from Mu'awiya ibn Sallam from Zayd ibn Sallam, that he heard Abu Sallam, who told me from Abu Umama, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, was Adam a prophet? He said: Yes. He said: How much was between him and Nuh, and Ibrahim? He said: Ten generations. He said: How much was between Nuh and Ibrahim? He said: Ten generations. He said: O Messenger of Allah, how many were the messengers? He said: Three hundred and thirteen."
"7572","Narrated Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from al-Hasan ibn Sahl al-Khayyat from Mus'ab ibn Sallam from Abdullah ibn al-Ala ibn Zayd from Abu Sallam al-Aswad from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: My Basin is as between Aden and Amman; in it are cups numbering the stars of the sky; whoever drinks from it shall never thirst after it; and among those of my umma who shall come to it are those with disheveled heads and soiled clothes, who do not marry the pampered women and do not attend the gates, meaning the doors of the rulers, those who render all that is upon them and are not given all that is theirs."
"7573","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Duhaym from Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb from Umar ibn Yazid from Abu Sallam al-Aswad from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three from whom shall not be accepted on the Day of Resurrection any obligatory ransom nor any voluntary equivalent: one disobedient to parents, one who reminds of favors, and one who denies the decree."
"7574","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Abu Mu'awiya from Abu Qays from Yahya ibn Abi Salih from Abu Sallam al-Habashi from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not perform ablution because of what fire had touched."
"7575","Narrated Abdullah ibn al-Sabbah al-Asbahani from al-Walid ibn Shuja from Abu Mu'awiya from Abu Qays from Yahya ibn Abi Salih from Abu Sallam al-Habashi from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not perform ablution because of a trodden place."
"7576","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abi Yahya al-Hadrami from Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Yunus from Umar ibn Yunus from Sulayman ibn Abi Sulayman from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Zayd ibn Sallam from Abu Umama and Thawban, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the two leather socks after he had urinated."
"7577","Abu Zabya from Abu Umama. Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Ubayd ibn Ghannam, both saying: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us from Sharik from Muhammad ibn Sa'd al-Ansari from Abu Zabya from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Love is from Allah, and renown is in heaven; when Allah loves a servant He says: O Jibril, your Lord loves so-and-so, so love him. He said: Then Jibril calls out in heaven: Your Lord loves so-and-so, so love him. He said: Then He sends down love for him upon the people of the earth."
"7578","Al-Haytham ibn Yazid from Abu Umama. Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Abdullah ibn Yusuf from Yahya ibn Hamza from al-Walid ibn Sulayman ibn Abi al-Sa'ib, that al-Haytham ibn Yazid told him from Abu Umama, who told him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: None wears silk in this world except one who has no share in the Hereafter."
"7579","Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama. Narrated Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Anbar al-Basri and Muhammad ibn al-Rabi ibn Shahin, both saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us from Abu Azza al-Dabbagh, who told me from Shahr ibn Hawshab, who said: I was at Damascus, and heads were brought and placed upon the steps of the mosque of Damascus, and I saw Abu Umama weeping. I said to him: What makes you weep, O Abu Umama? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There shall be among my umma people who recite the Qur'an not passing their collarbones, scattering it as inferior dates are scattered; they shall shoot out of the religion as the arrow shoots out of the prey, then they shall not return to it until the arrow returns to its notch; the worst slain under the sky; blessed is he who kills them and whom they kill."
"7580","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Nu'man, and narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Abu Umar al-Darir, and narrated Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Shu'ayb al-Simsar from Khalid ibn Khidash, and narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Sawti from Uthman ibn Muslim, all saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us from Sinan ibn Rabi'a from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution, washing his hands three times three times, purifying his face three times three times, and his forearms three times three times, wiping his head and his ears, and saying: The two ears are of the head, and washing what is in them; and the wording is of the hadith of Abu Umar."
"7581","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Yahya ibn al-Ala from Layth from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people of Quba: What is this purification with which you have been singled out in this verse: Therein are men who love to purify themselves, and Allah loves the purified? They said: O Messenger of Allah, none of us comes out from relieving himself except that he washes his seat."
"7582","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ja'far ibn Sulayman from Aban from Shahr ibn Hawshab, who informed me from Abu Umama, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever grows a white hair in the path of Allah, it shall be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it misses or hits, shall have for it the like of freeing a slave from the children of Ismail."
"7583","Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi from al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani from Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid from Salm ibn Zurayr from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam al-Saluli from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever narrates from me a false hadith deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"7584","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, both saying: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us from Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith from Marwan Abu Salama from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wipe over the two leather socks and the turban for three days on travel, and a day and a night when resident."
"7585","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Sahl ibn Uthman, and narrated Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari from Ahmad ibn al-Nu'man al-Mu'izz al-Missisi, all saying: Marwan ibn Mu'awiya narrated to us from Abd al-Hakam al-Sadusi from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among the worst of people in station with Allah on the Day of Resurrection is a servant who ruined his Hereafter by the worldly life of another."
"7586","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani, both saying: Waki narrated to us from al-A'mash from Shimr ibn Atiyya from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the Muslim man performs ablution, his sins exit from his hearing and sight and hands and feet; and if he sits, he sits forgiven."
"7587","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Waki from al-A'mash from Shimr ibn Atiyya from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: The supererogatory prayer was only for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7588","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Qays ibn al-Rabi from Shimr ibn Atiyya from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution and completes the ablution, his sins exit from his hearing and sight and hands and feet."
"7589","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Yahya ibn Dawud al-Wasiti from Ibrahim ibn Yazid ibn Mardanaba from Raqaba ibn Masqala from Shimr ibn Atiyya from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution and perfects the ablution, then stands for prayer, his sins exit from his hearing and sight and hands and feet."
"7590","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nu'aym from Fitr ibn Khalifa from Shimr ibn Atiyya, who said: I heard Shahr ibn Hawshab say: I entered upon Abu Umama in the mosque and found him eating thin bread. I said: O Abu Umama, narrate to me from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Yes; had I not heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except once or twice or three or four or five times, I would not narrate it to you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When the Muslim man performs ablution, then sets out to prayer, his sins exit from his hearing and sight and hands and feet. Abu Zabya said: Amr ibn Abasa narrated to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of this hadith, adding therein: When a man retires to his bed pure, then wakes in the night, he does not ask Allah Almighty for anything of the good of this world and the Hereafter except He gives it to him."
"7591","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Mu'awiya ibn Amr from Za'ida from Asim from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: Had I not heard it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except seven times, I would not have narrated it. He said: When a man performs ablution as commanded, sin departs from his hearing and sight and hands and feet."
"7592","Narrated Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti from Muhammad ibn Hassan al-Burjulani from Muhammad ibn Yazid from Ja'far ibn al-Harith from Asim from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: Had I not heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except seven times, I would not have narrated it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man performs ablution as commanded, sin departs from his hearing and sight and hands and feet."
"7593","Narrated Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Yahya al-Raqqi from Abu Farwa Yazid ibn Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sinan al-Rahawi from his father from his grandfather from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa and Abdullah ibn Ali from Amr ibn Murra from Shimr ibn Atiyya from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution and perfects the ablution, his sins exit from his hearing organs and sight and hands and feet."
"7594","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Dawud ibn Rashid, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani, both saying: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Ibn Abi Husayn from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever retires to his bed pure, remembering Allah until slumber overtakes him, he does not turn in an hour of the night asking Allah for anything of the good of this world and the Hereafter except Allah gives it to him."
"7595","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Ahmad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Waqid al-Harrani from Salama ibn Abi Muti from Qatada from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Purification expiates, and prayer is supererogatory."
"7596","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad from Ibn Abi Adi, and narrated Yusuf the judge from Muhammad ibn al-Minhal from Yazid ibn Zuray' , and narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Muhammad ibn Bishr, all of them from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ablution expiates what came before it, then prayer becomes supererogatory."
"7597","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Shayban ibn Farrukh from Abu Umayya al-Hinti from Qatada from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ablution expiates what came before it."
"7598","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, who told me from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami from Mu'adh ibn Hisham, who told me from his father from Qatada from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Ablution expiates what came before it."
"7599","Narrated Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna from Musaddad, and narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid, both saying: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us from Said from Qatada from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: A man of the people of the Bench died, and a dinar was found in his waist-wrapper. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One branding. Then another died, and two dinars were found in his waist-wrapper. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two brandings."
"7600","Narrated Ishaq ibn Jamil al-Asbahani from Ahmad ibn Mani' from Husayn ibn Muhammad from Shayban from Qatada from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abu Umama, who said: A man of the people of the Bench died, and a dinar was found in his waist-wrapper. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One branding. Then another died, and two dinars were found in his waist-wrapper. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two brandings."
"7601","Makhul al-Shami from Abu Umama. Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Haywa ibn Shurayh from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Sadaqa from Abu Wahb from Makhul from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: People are a fruit-bearing tree, and you shall soon return to a thorny tree; if you dispute with them they dispute with you, and if you leave them they do not leave you, and if you flee from them they seek you. He said: Then how is the way out of that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: You lend them of your honor for the day of your need."
"7602","Narrated Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi from Abu Tawba from Musa ibn Umayr from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Cheating one who trusts you is forbidden."
"7603","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Jarir from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman from Bishr ibn Awn from Bakkar ibn Tamim from Makhul from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) established brotherhood among the people, and established brotherhood between himself and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"7604","Narrated Ishaq ibn Khalawayh al-Wasiti from Ali ibn Bahr from al-Walid ibn Muslim from Hafs ibn Ghaylan from Makhul from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever walks to a prescribed prayer in congregation, it is like a pilgrimage; and whoever walks to a voluntary prayer, it is like a complete umra."
"7605","Narrated Uthman ibn Khalid ibn Amr al-Himsi from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Jabbar, who told me from Said ibn Ammar, who told me from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, who told me from Makhul, that they entered upon Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever goes out in the path of Allah is guaranteed by Allah: if Allah takes his soul He admits him to Paradise, and if He returns him He returns him with what he obtained of reward and spoil; the hadith."
"7606","Narrated Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih from Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq from Yahya ibn Ayyub from Ishaq ibn Asid from Abu Hafs al-Dimashqi from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Render the trust to whoever entrusted you, and do not betray whoever betrayed you."
"7607","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Ja'd al-Washa from Muhammad ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn al-Fadl from Salim al-Aftas from Makhul, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite poetry to one another and laugh, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat smiling with them."
"7608","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari from Abu Khaythama Zuhayr ibn Harb from al-Walid ibn Muslim, who told me from Hafs ibn Ghaylan from Makhul from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: A prayer upon the trace of a prayer, with no idle talk between them, is a record in Illiyyin."
"7609","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Uqba al-Sadusi from Hakim ibn Khidham from al-Ala ibn Kathir from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform witr upon his camel."
"7610","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Uqba from Hakim ibn Khidham from al-Ala ibn Kathir from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The ablution of sleep is that you touch water, then wipe with that touching your face and hands and feet like the wiping of tayammum."
"7611","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Uqba from Hakim ibn Khidham from al-Ala ibn Kathir from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever pardons when able, Allah pardons him on the day of hardship."
"7612","Narrated Ahmad ibn Bashir al-Tayalisi from al-Fadl ibn Ghanim from Hassan ibn Ibrahim from Abd al-Malik from al-Ala ibn al-Harith from Makhul from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The least of menstruation is three days and the most is ten."
"7613","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq from Suwayd ibn Said from Musa ibn Umayr from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer in a cloak, crossing its two ends."
"7614","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Muharibi from Musa ibn Umayr from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever learns a verse from the Book of Allah, Almighty and Exalted, it shall meet him on the Day of Resurrection laughing in his face."
"7615","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Ja'far ibn Sulayman from Abu Sinan al-Shami from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any youth who grows up upon the worship of Allah until he dies, Allah shall give him the reward of ninety-nine truthful ones."
"7616","Narrated Abdullah ibn Wahb al-Ghazzi from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani from Yusuf ibn Atiyya from Marzuq Abu Abdullah al-Himsi from Makhul from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any youth who grows up seeking knowledge and worship until he grows old, Allah shall give him on the Day of Resurrection the reward of seventy-two truthful ones."
"7617","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq from Yahya al-Himmani from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed women who scratch faces and tear open garments."
"7618","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell fruit until its soundness appears."
"7619","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade on the day of Khaybar that pregnant women be approached until they give birth."
"7620","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that arrows be sold until divided."
"7621","Narrated Ubayd from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the woman who joins hair and the one joined to, and the woman who tattoos and the one tattooed; and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the meat of domestic donkeys."
"7622","Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Sabuni al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Aqil from Hafs ibn Umar ibn Maymun al-Ubulli from Thawr ibn Yazid from Makhul, and Rashid ibn Sa'd from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was struck by Abdullah ibn Qami'a with a stone on the day of Uhud, wounding his face and breaking his front tooth, saying: Take it, and I am Ibn Qami'a. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him while wiping blood from his face: What is with you; may Allah disfigure you. So Allah set upon him a mountain goat, not a mere goat, which kept butting him until it cut him piece by piece."
"7623","Narrated Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Sawwaf from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Aqil from Hafs ibn Umar from Thawr ibn Yazid from Rashid ibn Sa'd, and Makhul from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when Ibn Qami'a struck him on the day of Uhud, I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he performed ablution, loosen his bandage and wipe over it with the ablution."
"7624","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Ja'd al-Washa from Muhammad ibn Bakkar from Hassan ibn Ibrahim from Abd al-Karim from al-Ala ibn al-Harith from Makhul from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked yawning in prayer."
"7625","Narrated al-Qasim the broker from Asid ibn Zayd from Muhammad ibn al-Fadl from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat between the eyes of Hell; this weighed upon his Companions, so they said: O Messenger of Allah, we narrate from you adding and omitting. He said: That is not what I mean for you; I only mean the one who lies upon me in speech, seeking thereby the disgrace of Islam. They said: O Messenger of Allah, you said: between the eyes of Hell, and does Hell have an eye? He said: Yes; have you not heard Him say: When it sees them from a distant place they shall hear for it fury and roaring; do you think they see except with two eyes?"
"7626","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Said from Abu Nu'aym al-Nakha'i from al-Ala ibn Kathir from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the strap of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broke. He said: We belong to Allah and to Him we return. A man said to him: This is but a strap. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is a calamity."
"7627","Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Ahmad ibn Makhlad ibn Rahawayh from Abu Nu'aym al-Nakha'i from al-Ala ibn Kathir from Makhul from Abu al-Darda, Abu Umama, and Wathila, who said: We heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Keep your mosques clear of your children and your insane, and your disputes and your voices, and the drawing of your swords, and the execution of your penalties; and perfume them on Friday, and take places of purification at the doors of your mosques."
"7628","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh from Bishr ibn Isa from Ibn Abi Fudayk from Umar ibn Hafs from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from sudden death, and it pleased him to fall ill before dying."
"7629","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Hajjaj al-Azraq from Mubarak ibn Said from Umar ibn Musa from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from sudden death, and it pleased him to fall ill before dying."
"7630","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Hajjaj al-Azraq from Mubarak ibn Said from Umar ibn Musa from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) riding, and we passed by a herd of camels. He said: Whose are these? They said: Of Banu al-Anbar. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Those are our people."
"7631","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Yusuf from al-Haytham ibn Humayd from a man from Makhul from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Beware of urine, for it is the first thing for which the servant is reckoned in the grave."
"7632","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sarraj al-Askari from Ismail Ibrahim al-Tarjuman from Ayyub ibn Mudrik from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Full guarding of the frontier is forty days; whoever guards the frontier forty days without selling or buying nor committing an offense exits his sins as on the day his mother bore him."
"7633","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sarraj from Ismail ibn Ibrahim from Ayyub from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Beware of urine, for it is the first thing for which the servant is reckoned in the grave."
"7634","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Sarraj from Ismail ibn Ibrahim from Ayyub from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting is a shield, and it is a fortress among the fortresses of the believer; and every deed is for its doer, and fasting is Mine, and I reward for it."
"7635","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Mus'ab al-Ushnani at Kufa from Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Muharibi from Musa ibn Umayr from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not revile the rulers, and supplicate Allah for them, for their righteousness is righteousness for you."
"7636","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Muhammad from Muhammad ibn Ubayd from Musa ibn Umayr from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether short or reaching, there is written for him the freeing of a slave."
"7637","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Muhammad from Muhammad ibn Ubayd from Musa ibn Umayr from Makhul from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sends blessings upon me, Allah sends blessings upon him tenfold; an angel is entrusted with it until he conveys it to me."
"7638","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Qasim al-Ta'i from Abd al-Malik ibn Abd Rabbihi al-Ta'i from Musa ibn Umayr from Makhul from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dust in the nostrils of a servant in the path of Allah and the smoke of Hell shall never gather."
"7639","Narrated Maslama ibn Jabir al-Lakhmi al-Dimashqi from Munabbih ibn Uthman, who told me from Sadaqa ibn Abdullah, who told me from al-Nu'man, meaning Ibn al-Mundhir, from Makhul, and Yahya ibn al-Harith, meaning al-Dhimari, from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves for Allah and hates for Allah and gives for Allah and withholds for Allah has completed faith."
"7640","Narrated Abu Zur'a Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak al-Suri from al-Haytham ibn Humayd from Hafs ibn Ghaylan from Makhul, who said: I entered, I and Ibn Abi Zakariyya and Sulayman ibn Habib, upon Abu Umama at Hims, and we greeted him. He said: This sitting of yours is of Allah's conveying to you and His proof against you; the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, conveyed, so convey."
"7641","Shurahbil ibn Muslim al-Khawlani from Abu Umama. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Ismail ibn Ayyash, and narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratis from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam, and narrated Muhammad ibn al-Rabi ibn Shahin from Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, and narrated Abu Shu'ayb al-Harrani from Abdullah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi, and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, all saying: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Shurahbil ibn Muslim al-Khawlani from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage: Allah, Almighty and Exalted, has given every possessor of a right his right, and there is no bequest for an heir. The child is for the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone, and their reckoning is upon Allah, Almighty and Exalted. Whoever claims kinship to other than his father or attaches to other than his masters, upon him is the far-reaching curse of Allah until the Day of Resurrection. And no woman shall spend anything from her house except by her husband's permission. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, not even food? He said: That is the best of our wealth. Then he said: A loaned item is to be returned, a granted milch animal is to be given back, a debt is to be settled, and the guarantor is liable; and the wording is of the hadith of Abd al-Razzaq."
"7642","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Haywa ibn Shurayh al-Himsi from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Shurahbil ibn Muslim from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There shall be in the end of time police who go out in the anger of Allah and return in the wrath of Allah; beware of being among their entourage."
"7643","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father, and narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Mu'addib from Ibrahim ibn Shammas, both saying: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Shurahbil ibn Muslim from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in his sermon in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage: O people, there is no prophet after me and no umma after you; worship your Lord, pray your five, fast your month, and obey those in authority over you, you shall enter the Paradise of your Lord."
"7644","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Abd al-Wahhab ibn al-Dahhak from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Shurahbil ibn Muslim from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The devils go out with their banners to the markets, entering with the first to enter and leaving with the last to leave."
"7645","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani from Muhammad ibn Awf al-Himsi from Hashim ibn Amr from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Shurahbil ibn Muslim from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Do not return after me as disbelievers, striking the necks of one another."
"7646","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani from Muhammad ibn Awf al-Himsi from Said ibn Rawh from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Shurahbil ibn Muslim from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for his two leather socks to wear them; he wore one of them, then a crow came and carried off the other and threw it, and a snake came out of it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not wear his two leather socks until he shakes them."
"7647","Safwan al-Asamm al-Ta'i from Abu Umama. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Musaffa from Abu al-Mughira from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Shurahbil ibn Muslim, and Safwan al-Asamm al-Ta'i from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A loaned item is to be returned, a granted milch animal is to be given back, the guarantor is liable, the child is for the bed and for the adulterer is the stone, and there is no bequest for an heir."
"7648","Asad ibn Wada'a from Abu Umama Sudayy ibn Ajlan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Narrated al-Husayn ibn Taqi ibn Abi al-Taqi al-Himsi from my grandfather Abu Taqi Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik from al-Mu'afa ibn Imran al-Zahri from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Asad ibn Wada'a, Shurahbil ibn Muslim, and Muhammad ibn Ziyad from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no prophet after me and no umma after you; worship your Lord, fast your month, pray your five, and give the charity of your wealth with your souls content with it, you shall enter the Paradise of your Lord."
"7649","Shaddad Abu Ammar from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Abu al-Mughira, and narrated Abu Shu'ayb al-Harrani from Yahya ibn Abdullah al-Bablutti, both saying: al-Awza'i narrated to us, who told me from Abu Ammar, who told me from Abu Umama, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have incurred a prescribed penalty, so establish it upon me. He turned away from him. Then he said: I have incurred a prescribed penalty, so establish it upon me. He turned away from him. Then he said: O Messenger of Allah, I have incurred a prescribed penalty, so establish it upon me. He turned away from him. Prayer was established, and when he gave salam he said: O Messenger of Allah, I have incurred a prescribed penalty, so establish it upon me. He said: Did you perform ablution? He said: Yes. He said: And did you pray when we prayed? He said: Yes. He said: Go, for Allah has pardoned you."
"7650","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Sawra al-Baghdadi and Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri, both saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, and narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi, both saying: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us from Ikrima ibn Ammar, who told me from Shaddad Abu Ammar, who told me from Abu Umama, who said: While I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man came and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have incurred a prescribed penalty, so establish it upon me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent, and he repeated: I have incurred a prescribed penalty, so establish it upon me. He said: Prayer was established; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and came out. Shaddad said, and Abu Umama told me, he said: I was walking with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the man followed him saying: O Messenger of Allah, I have incurred a prescribed penalty, so establish it upon me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you left your house, did you perform ablution and perfect it? He said: Yes. He said: And did you attend prayer with us? He said: Yes. He said: Then Allah, Almighty and Exalted, has forgiven you your penalty, or he said: your debt."
"7651","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Muhammad ibn Bakkar ibn al-Rayyan from Anbasa ibn Abd al-Wahid, and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Qurad Abu Nuh from Ikrima ibn Ammar from Shaddad Abu Ammar from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: If you spend the surplus it is better for you, and if you withhold it it is worse for you; and there is no blame upon sufficiency; and begin with those you support; and the upper hand is better than the lower hand."
"7652","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abdullah ibn al-Rumi from al-Nadr ibn Muhammad from Ikrima ibn Ammar from Shaddad Abu Ammar from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The upper hand is better than the lower hand."
"7653","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Yusuf, who told me from Yahya ibn Hamza from al-Awza'i, that Shaddad Abu Ammar told him, who told us from Abu Umama, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None wears silk in this world except one who has no share."
"7654","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Iqal al-Harrani from Abu Ja'far al-Nufayli from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tara'ifi from Mu'awiya ibn Sallam from Hud ibn Ata, who said: I heard Shaddad Abu Ammar say: I heard Abu Umama say: A man came to the Messenger of Allah and said: O Messenger of Allah, what of a man who seeks good and renown, what has he? He said: He has nothing; saying that three times; Allah, Almighty and Exalted, accepts of deeds only what was sincere to Him and by which His Face was sought."
"7655","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Misri al-Ubulli from Abu Asim from Jasr ibn Farqad from al-Nahhas ibn Qahm al-Qaysi from Shaddad Abu Ammar from Abu Umama al-Bahili, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When the children of Ma'dd ibn Adnan reached forty men, they stood against the camp of Musa, peace be upon him, and plundered it, so Musa ibn Imran, peace be upon him, supplicated against them. He said: O Lord, these are the children of Ma'dd who raided my camp. Allah revealed to him: O Musa ibn Imran, do not supplicate against them, for among them is the unlettered Prophet, the warner and bearer of glad tidings of My Paradise, and among them is the mercy-receiving umma, the umma of Muhammad, who are content from Allah with little of provision, and Allah is content from them with little of deeds, so Allah admits them to Paradise by the saying: There is no god but Allah, for their Prophet is Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, humble in his bearing, with intellect gathered in his silence, speaking with wisdom and employing forbearance; I brought him out of the best generation of his umma, Quraysh, then brought him out of Hashim, the elite of Quraysh; they are better from better ever turning to better, and his umma shall turn to better."
"7656","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abi Yahya al-Hadrami from Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami from Umar ibn Yunus from Sulayman ibn Abi Sulayman from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Shaddad Abu Ammar from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril kept enjoining me regarding the neighbor until I thought he would make him an heir."
"7657","Yazid al-Qayni from Abu Umama. Narrated al-Husayn ibn al-Sumayda' al-Antaki from Musa ibn Ayyub al-Nasibi from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Numayr ibn Yazid al-Qayni from his father, who said: I heard Abu Umama narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That Maryam asked her Lord for meat with no blood in it, so He fed her locusts; she said: O Allah, give it life without suckling, and make it follow one another without outcry."
"7658","Quhafa ibn Rabi'a from Abu Umama. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Musaffa, and narrated Ibrahim ibn Mattawayh al-Asbahani from Yahya ibn Uthman al-Himsi, both saying: Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us from Numayr ibn Yazid al-Qayni from Quhafa ibn Rabi'a ibn Sa'd from Sudayy ibn Ajlan Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came during the Farewell Pilgrimage upon a camel until he stood in the midst of the people on the day of Arafa, and said: What day is this? They said: The day of Arafa, the sacred day. He said: Then what month? They said: In the sacred month. He said: Then what land is this? They said: The sacred land. He said: Your wealth and your honor and your blood are forbidden to you, as this day of yours in this month of yours in this land of yours. Verily every prophet's supplication has passed except my supplication, for I have stored it with my Lord until the Day of Resurrection. To proceed: the prophets vie in numbers, so do not disgrace me, for I shall sit for you at the Basin."
"7659","Salama al-Qaysi from Abu Umama. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Amr ibn Uthman from Baqiyya from Safwan ibn Amr from Salama al-Qaysi from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Give glad tidings to those who walk in darkness to the mosques of pulpits of light on the Day of Resurrection; the people shall be frightened while they are not frightened."
"7660","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani from Muhammad ibn Musaffa from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Safwan ibn Amr from Salama al-Qaysi from a man of his household from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7661","Abd al-A'la ibn Hilal al-Sulami from Abu Umama. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani, and narrated Musa ibn Sahl Abu Imran al-Jawni from Hisham ibn Ammar, both saying: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Yazid ibn Ayham from Abd al-A'la ibn Hilal al-Sulami, who said: I heard Abu Umama al-Bahili say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate food, he would say: Praise be to Allah, much, good, blessed praise, never denied, nor bidden farewell to, nor dispensed with."
"7662","Narrated Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Amr ibn Uthman al-Himsi from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Utba ibn Abi Hakim, who told me from Umara ibn Rashid from Abd al-A'la ibn Hilal al-Sulami from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No servant dies on the day he dies leaving yellow or white metal except he shall be branded with it."
"7663","Hatim ibn Hurayth al-Ta'i from Abu Umama. Narrated Musa ibn Harun from al-Haytham ibn Kharija, and narrated Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Hisham ibn Ammar, both saying: al-Jarrah ibn Malih al-Bahrani narrated to us from Hatim ibn Hurayth al-Ta'i, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A loaned item is to be returned, a granted milch animal is to be given back; and whoever finds a tied she-camel whose milk was gathered, it is not lawful for him to tie up its udder until he returns it."
"7664","Abd al-Rahman ibn Maysara al-Hadrami from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Abu Zur'a al-Dimashqi Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr from Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi, and narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti and Abu Zayd Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Hawti, both saying: Abu al-Mughira narrated to us, all saying: Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us from Abd al-Rahman ibn Maysara, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There shall enter Paradise by the intercession of one man who is not a prophet the like of the two tribes, or one of the two tribes, Rabi'a and Mudar."
"7665","Abu al-Ghazi al-Ansi from Abu Umama. Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um, who told me from Abu al-Ghazi al-Ansi from Abu Umama al-Bahili, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Among the best of people are the princes, the princes of Himyar, and Sufyan and al-Sakun, and the Ash'aris."
"7666","Za'ida ibn al-Husayn from Abu Umama. Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi from Jami' ibn Thawb al-Rahabi from Za'ida ibn Husayn from Abu Umama, that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a commander, he would say: Shorten the sermon and lessen speech."
"7667","Abu Sufyan al-Ru'ayni from Abu Umama. Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi from Jami' ibn Thawb from Abu Sufyan al-Ru'ayni from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not appoint a governor until he turbaned him, loosening a tail of it from the right side toward the ear."
"7668","Amr ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abu Umama. Narrated Salama ibn Nahid al-Maqdisi from Abdullah ibn Hani from Damra ibn Rabi'a from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani from Amr ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Almighty and Exalted, faced me toward Syria and turned my back to Yemen, and said to me: O Muhammad, I have made what faces you spoil and provision, and what is behind your back reinforcement; and Islam shall ever increase while polytheism and its people decrease, until two women travel fearing no injustice. Then he said: By Him in whose Hand is my soul, the days and nights shall not pass until this religion reaches the reach of this star."
"7669","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani from Abu Umayr Isa ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Nahhas from Damra ibn Rabi'a from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani from Amr ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There shall ever remain a party of my umma upon the truth, dominant over those who raid them, subduing; those who oppose them shall not harm them, until the command of Allah comes to them while they are thus. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, and where are they? He said: At Bayt al-Maqdis."
"7670","Narrated Ahmad ibn Ruziq ibn Jami' al-Misri from Amr ibn Sawwad al-Sarhi from Ibn Wahb, who informed me from Yunus ibn Yazid from Ata al-Khurasani from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani from the hadith of Amr al-Hadrami of the people of Hims from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us one day, and most of his sermon was mention of the Dajjal, warning us of him and narrating to us of him until he finished his sermon. Among what he said to us that day: Allah, Almighty and Exalted, sent no prophet except he warned his umma, and I am the last of the prophets and you are the last of the ummas, and he shall emerge among you inevitably. If he emerges while I am among you, I am the disputant of every Muslim; and if he emerges among you after me, every man is the disputant of himself, and Allah, Almighty and Exalted, is my successor over every Muslim. He shall emerge from a gap between Iraq and Syria, wreaking havoc right and wreaking havoc left: O servants of Allah, stand firm, for he shall begin saying: I am a prophet, and there is no prophet after me; and it is written between his eyes: disbeliever, which every believer shall read. Whoever of you meets him, let him spit in his face and recite the deterring verses of the sura of the Companions of the Cave. He shall be given power over one soul from the children of Adam, killing it then reviving it; he shall not go beyond that, nor be given power over any other soul. Among his trial is that with him are a paradise and a fire; his fire is a paradise and his paradise is a fire. Whoever is tried by his fire, let him close his eyes and seek aid from Allah; it shall be coolness and peace, as the fire was coolness and peace upon Ibrahim. His days are forty days: a day like a year, a day like a month, a day like a Friday, and a day like the days in those days, and the last of his days like a mirage; a man shall be at the gate of Madina in the morning and reach its other gate before evening. He said: Then how shall we pray, O Messenger of Allah, in those short days? He said: Estimate therein as you estimate in the long days."
"7671","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani from Abu Umayr ibn al-Nahhas from Damra from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani from Amr ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned the like of it."
"7672","Husayn ibn al-Aswad al-Hilali from Abu Umama. Narrated Abu Abd al-Malik Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Dimashqi from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sawwar al-Hilali from Husayn ibn al-Aswad al-Hilali from Abu Umama al-Bahili, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say to his Companions: When one of you is upon ablution and eats food, he should perform ablution, except camel's milk; when you drink it, rinse with water."
"7673","Khidash from Abu Umama. Narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Ayyash, who told me from his father from Damdam ibn Zur'a from Shurayh ibn Ubayd, who said: Khidash said from Abu Umama, that he witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the Farewell Pilgrimage; the first thing he uttered was to say: Allah, Almighty and Exalted, enjoins you regarding your mothers. Then he praised Allah, Almighty and Exalted, then said what Allah willed him to say, then said: Verily a loaned item is to be returned, and a grant is to be returned, and the child is for the bed and for the adulterer is the stone."
"7674","Abu Amir al-Hawzani, whose name is Abdullah ibn Luhayy, from Abu Umama. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Sawwaf al-Tustari, both saying: Abdullah ibn al-Sabbah al-Attar narrated to us from Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman, who said: I heard al-Hajjaj ibn Furafisa narrate from Muhammad ibn al-Walid from Abu Amir al-Hawzani from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A loaned item is to be returned. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, what of the covenant of Allah? He said: The covenant of Allah is the most deserving of what is fulfilled."
"7675","Abu Amir al-Alhani from Abu Umama. Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki from Ya'qub ibn Humayd from Marwan ibn Mu'awiya from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, who told us from Abu Amir al-Alhani from Abu Umama and Utba ibn Abd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Whoever prays the dawn prayer in a mosque in congregation, then remains until he performs the glorification of the forenoon, shall have as the reward of a pilgrim and umra-performer, complete for him his pilgrimage and his umra."
"7676","Abd al-Wahid ibn Qays from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Asqalani from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Amr ibn Bakr al-Saksaki from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahid ibn Qays from Abd al-Wahid ibn Qays, who said: I heard Abu Umama al-Bahili say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: For a man is what he intended, and upon him is what he earned. And a man is with whom he loves. And whoever dies upon the fringes of the road is of its people."
"7677","Kuhayl ibn Harmala from Abu Umama. Narrated Abdullah ibn Musa ibn Abi Uthman al-Anmati al-Baghdadi from al-Hasan ibn Musa from Maslama ibn Ali from Khalid ibn Dihqan from Kuhayl ibn Harmala from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The expiation of every quarrel is two rak'as."
"7678","Marih ibn Masruq al-Hawzani from Abu Umama. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Sulayman ibn Salama al-Khaba'iri from Mani' ibn al-Sari ibn al-Dahhak al-Harrani from Abdullah ibn Humayd al-Yazani from Marih ibn Masruq al-Hawzani from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Kindness is only fitting for one of noble lineage, or religion, or forbearance."
"7679","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Adhani al-Missisi from Muhammad ibn Awf al-Himsi from Abu al-Mughira from Abdullah ibn Raja al-Shaybani, who said: I heard a shaykh surnamed Abu Abdullah Marih narrate that he heard Abu Umama narrate that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I do not fear for my umma a hunger that kills them, nor an enemy that uproots them, but I fear for my umma misleading imams; if they obey them they tempt them, and if they disobey them they kill them."
"7680","Ghaylan ibn Ma'shar from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father Mubashshir ibn Ismail, and narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Musaffa from Baqiyya, and narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mu'alla al-Dimashqi and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari, both saying: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us from Mu'awiya ibn Yahya, all saying: Artat ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us from Ghaylan ibn Ma'shar, who said: I heard Abu Umama say: A man died in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they found no shroud for him. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we found no shroud for him. He said: Seek in his waist-wrapper. They found two dinars. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two brandings; pray over your companion."
"7681","Abu Rashid al-Hubrani from Abu Umama. Narrated Ahmad ibn Khulayd al-Halabi from Ubayd ibn Jannad from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani from Abu Rashid al-Hubrani from Abu Umama, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand and said: O Abu Umama, among the believers are those for whom my heart softens."
"7682","Zayd ibn Artat from Abu Umama. Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani from al-Hasan ibn Arafa from Hafs ibn Ghiyath from Isa from Zayd ibn Artat from Abu Umama al-Bahili, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant is given in this world anything better for him than to be permitted two rak'as which he prays."
"7683","Narrated Ishaq ibn Humayd al-Asbahani from Ahmad ibn Mani' from Abu al-Nadr from Bakr ibn Khunays from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym from Zayd ibn Artat from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: No servant is permitted anything better than two rak'as which he prays; goodness is surely scattered upon the head of the servant so long as he is in his prayer, and servants draw near to Allah, Almighty and Exalted, with nothing like what came out of Him. Abu al-Nadr said: Meaning the Qur'an."
"7684","Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Adam from Abu Umama. Narrated al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas al-Qirtimi al-Baghdadi from Ismail ibn Isa al-Attar from Amr ibn Abd al-Jabbar from Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Adam, who told me from Abu al-Darda, Abu Umama, Wathila ibn al-Asqa', and Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Who are those firm in knowledge? He said: He whose oath is dutiful and whose tongue is truthful, and whose belly and private part are chaste; that is the firmly grounded."
"7685","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah al-Jarjara'i from Kathir ibn Marwan al-Filastini from Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Adam al-Dimashqi, who said: Abu al-Darda, Abu Umama, Wathila ibn al-Asqa', and Anas ibn Malik told me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us one day while we were disputing over something of the religion's affair, and he grew angry with a severe anger the like of which he had not shown, then rebuked us and said: Gently, O umma of Muhammad; those before you perished only by this; they took up disputation for its little good. Leave disputation, for the believer does not dispute. Leave disputation, for the disputer's loss has grown. Leave disputation, for it suffices you as sin to remain ever disputing. Leave disputation, for I shall not intercede for the disputer on the Day of Resurrection. Leave disputation, for I am guarantor of three dwellings in Paradise, its outskirts and its middle and its highest, for whoever leaves disputation while truthful. Leave disputation, for the first thing my Lord forbade me after the worship of idols was disputation, and the drinking of wine. Leave disputation, for Satan has despaired of being worshipped, but he is content with you in provocation, and it is disputation. Leave disputation, for the Children of Israel split into seventy-one sects, and the Christians into seventy-two sects, all upon misguidance except the great multitude. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and who is the great multitude? He said: Whoever is upon what I and my Companions are upon; whoever does not dispute in the religion of Allah, and does not declare any of the people of monotheism a disbeliever for a sin forgiven him. Then he said: Islam began strange and shall return strange. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and who are the strangers? He said: Those who set right when people corrupt, and do not dispute in the religion of Allah, and do not declare any of the people of monotheism a disbeliever for a sin."
"7686","Narrated Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Sufyan al-Raqqi from Ali ibn Maymun al-Raqqi from Abdullah ibn Khalid al-Qurqusani Abdun from Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Awdi from Abu al-Darda and Wathila ibn al-Asqa and Abu Umama and Anas ibn Malik, who said: We were in a gathering in which there were some Jews, and we were discussing qadar (divine decree), when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us angry; he frowned, scowled, and rebuked, then said: Enough, enough; fear Allah, O umma of Muhammad; two deep valleys with deep bottoms, dark; do not stir up upon yourselves the blaze of the Fire. Then he ordered the Jews to stand. Then he spoke, extending his right hand and extending his left finger. Then he said: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful; this is a book from Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful, with the names of the people of Paradise, their fathers, their sons, and their tribes; your Lord has finished, your Lord has finished. Then he extended his left hand, then pointed to it with his right finger. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful; this is a book from the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful, with the names of the people of the Fire, and the names of their fathers, their sons, and their tribes; your Lord has finished, your Lord has finished, your Lord has finished."
"7687","Narrated al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas al-Qirtimi from Ismail ibn Isa al-Attar from Amr ibn Abd al-Jabbar from Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Adam; Abu al-Darda and Wathila ibn al-Asqa and Abu Umama and Anas ibn Malik narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah loves that His concessions be accepted just as the servant loves the forgiveness of his Lord."
"7688","Yazid ibn Khumayr from Abu Umama. Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi from Jami ibn Thawb from Yazid ibn Khumayr from Abu Umama, that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dispatched a commander he said: Shorten the sermon and lessen speech, for indeed part of speech is magic."
"7689","Abdullah ibn Ghabir from Abu Umama. Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Sahl ibn Uthman from al-Muharibi from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim from Abdullah ibn Ghabir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays the dawn prayer in a congregational mosque and remains there until he prays the forenoon supererogatory prayer, it will be like the reward of a pilgrim performing Hajj or Umra, complete in his Hajj and his Umra."
"7690","Salim ibn Amir Abu Yahya al-Khabairi from Abu Umama; Muawiya ibn Salih from Sulaym ibn Amir. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Salih; Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to me from Abu Yahya Sulaym ibn Amir that he heard Abu Umama al-Bahili saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) during the Farewell Pilgrimage upon al-Jada, having placed his feet in the stirrups of the saddle, stretching up to make the people hear, saying: Do you not hear? prolonging his voice. Then a speaker from among groups of people said: What do you enjoin upon us? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Worship your Lord, pray your five, fast your month, pay the zakat of your wealth, and obey those in authority over you, you will enter the Paradise of your Lord. Abu Yahya said: So I said: O Abu Umama, the like of whom were you on that day? He said: A man of thirty years, jostling the camels until I pushed forward on foot toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7691","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Salih; Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to me from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Indeed Allah, Almighty and Exalted, will admit into Paradise seventy thousand of my umma without reckoning. Yazid ibn al-Akhnas al-Sulami said: And what is this among your umma except like the blue fly among flies? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seventy thousand of my umma will enter Paradise, with every thousand seventy thousand, and three handfuls. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and what is the expanse of your Hawd? He said: Like what is between Aden and Oman, and it is wider and wider, and he gestured with his hand. In it are two spouts of gold and silver. He said: O Messenger of Allah, then what is its drink? He said: Its drink is whiter than milk, sweeter in taste than honey, and more fragrant in smell than musk."
"7692","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abdullah ibn Salih; Muawiya narrated to me from Sulaym ibn Amir, that Abu Umama al-Bahili narrated to him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us after the dawn prayer and said: I saw a vision, and it is true, so comprehend it. A man came to me and took my hand and led me until he brought me to a rugged, tall mountain, and said to me: Climb it. I said: I cannot. He said: I will make it easy for you. So every time I lifted my foot I placed it upon a step until we stood level on the mountain. Then we set off, and behold, men and women with their cheeks torn. I said: Who are these? He said: These are those who say what they do not know. Then we set off, and behold, men and women with their eyes and ears nailed. I said: Who are these? He said: These are those who make their eyes see what they did not see, and make their ears hear what they did not hear. Then we set off, and behold, women suspended by their ankles with their heads hanging down, snakes biting their breasts. I said: Who are these? He said: These are those who withhold their children from their milk. Then we set off, and behold, men and women suspended by their ankles with their heads hanging down, lapping from a little water and mud. I said: Who are these? He said: These are those who fast and break their fast before the lawful time of their fast. Then we set off, and behold, men and women of the ugliest appearance, ugliest clothing, and foulest odor, as if their odor were that of privies. I said: Who are these? He said: These are the adulterers, male and female. Then we set off, and behold, dead bodies most swollen and foulest in odor. I said: Who are these? He said: These are the dead of the disbelievers. Then we set off, and we saw smoke and heard howling. I said: What is this? He said: This is Hell, so leave it. Then we set off, and behold, sleeping men beneath the shade of trees. I said: Who are these? He said: The dead of the Muslims. Then we set off, and behold, boys and girls playing between two rivers. I said: Who are these? He said: The offspring of the believers. Then we set off, and behold, men of the finest faces, finest clothing, and best fragrance, as if their faces were parchments. I said: Who are these? He said: These are the truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous. Then we set off, and behold, three men drinking wine of theirs and singing. I said: Who are these? He said: That is Zayd ibn Haritha, and Jafar, and Ibn Rawaha. Then I inclined toward them and they said: You have attained, you have attained. He said: Then I raised my head, and behold, three men beneath the Throne. I said: Who are these? He said: That is your father Ibrahim, and Musa, and Isa, and they are awaiting you, peace of Allah be upon them all."
"7693","Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama. Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi from Hisham ibn Ammar from Sadaqa ibn Khalid from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir. And narrated Idris ibn Abd al-Karim al-Haddad al-Muqri from al-Haytham ibn Kharija from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from his father from Abu Yahya Sulaym ibn Amir al-Himsi, who said: I heard Abu Umama narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: While I was sleeping, I was brought and taken to a rugged mountain, and it was said: Climb. I said: I cannot climb. He said: I will make it easy for you. He said: So I climbed until, when I was level on the mountain, I heard voices. I said: What are these voices? It was said: These are the voices of Hell. Then I was taken until I passed by people most swollen, worst in appearance, and foulest in odor, their odor the odor of privies. I said: Who are these? It was said: These are the adulterers, male and female. Then I was taken until I was brought past women suspended by their breasts, snakes biting them. I said: Who are these? They said: These are those who withhold their milk from their children. Then I was taken until I passed by people suspended by their ankles with their cheeks torn, their cheeks flowing with blood. I said: Who are these? He said: These are those who break their fast before the time of their breaking fast. Then I was taken until I overlooked three men drinking wine of theirs. I said: Who are these? He said: This is Zayd, and Jafar, and Ibn Rawaha. Then I was taken until I overlooked boys playing between two rivers. I said: Who are these? He said: The offspring of the believers, whom Ibrahim nurses. Then I was taken until I overlooked three men. I said: Who are these? He said: Ibrahim, and Musa, and Isa, peace of Allah be upon them, awaiting you."
"7694","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father from al-Walid ibn Muslim from Ibn Jabir from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to deliver the sermon on the Day of Sacrifice upon his mount."
"7695","Yazid ibn Sinan Abu Farwa al-Rahawi from Sulaym ibn Amir. Narrated Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Yahya al-Raqqi from Abu Farwa Yazid ibn Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sinan al-Rahawi; my father narrated to me from his father; Abu Yahya al-Kalai narrated to me from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed the last man to enter Paradise is a man turning over upon the Sirat, back to belly, like a boy whom his father beats while he flees from him, his deed too weak to hasten him. He will say: O Lord, bring me to Paradise and save me from the Fire. So Allah, Exalted, will reveal to him: My servant, if I save you from the Fire and admit you into Paradise, will you confess to Me your sins and errors? The servant will say: Yes, O Lord; by Your might and majesty, if You save me from the Fire I will surely confess to You my sins and errors. Then he will cross the bridge, and the servant will say within himself: If I confess to Him my sins and errors He will surely return me to the Fire. So Allah will reveal to him: My servant, confess to Me your sins and errors, I will forgive them for you and admit you into Paradise. The servant will say: No; by Your might, I never committed a sin at all, nor ever erred an error at all. So Allah will reveal to him: My servant, I have against you a proof. The servant will look right and left and see no one, and say: O Lord, show me Your proof. So Allah will make his skin speak of minor deeds, and when the servant sees that, he will say: O Lord, by Your might, I have grave hidden deeds. So Allah, Almighty and Exalted, will reveal to him: My servant, I know them better than you; confess them to Me, I will forgive them for you and admit you into Paradise. So the servant will confess his sins and enter Paradise. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his molars appeared, saying: This is the lowest of the people of Paradise in rank, so how about the one above him?"
"7696","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Hashim ibn al-Qasim from Abu Aqil Abdullah ibn Aqil al-Thaqafi from Yazid ibn Sinan; Abu Yahya al-Kalai informed me, saying: I heard Abu Umama saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I surely know the last man of my umma to cross the Sirat, writhing upon the Sirat like a boy when his father beats him, and he mentioned the like of it."
"7697","Safwan ibn Amr from Sulaym ibn Amir. Narrated Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj from Abu Zayd Ibn Abi al-Ghimr from Muawiya ibn Yahya from Safwan ibn Amr; Salim ibn Amir narrated to me, saying: I heard Abu Umama saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among the people and said: Indeed Allah has prescribed Hajj upon you. Then a Bedouin man stood and said: Every year? The speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cut off and he became angry, and remained silent long, then he spoke and said: Who is this questioner? The Bedouin said: It is I, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Woe to you, what assures you that I would say yes? By Allah, had I said yes, it would have become obligatory, and had it become obligatory you would have abandoned it, and had you abandoned it you would have disbelieved. Verily, those before you were destroyed only by the imams of hardship. By Allah, had I permitted for you all that is in the earth of things, and forbidden for you the like of a camel's hoof, you would have fallen into it. Then Allah, Exalted, revealed: Do not ask about things, to the end of the verse."
"7698","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi from al-Abbas ibn Uthman al-Muallim. And narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Duhaym; both said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us from Safwan ibn Amr from Sulaym ibn Amir and Abu al-Yaman al-Hawzani from Abu Umama from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Indeed Allah, Almighty and Exalted, promised me to admit into Paradise seventy thousand of my umma without reckoning, and He increased me three handfuls. It was said: Then what is the expanse of your Hawd? He said: Like between Aden and Oman; in it are two spouts of gold and silver. He said: Then what is your Hawd? He said: Whiter than milk, sweeter in taste than honey, and more fragrant in smell than musk; whoever drinks from it will never thirst thereafter, and his face will never darken ever."
"7699","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Safwan ibn Amr from Sulaym ibn Amir, who said: We used to sit with Abu Umama al-Bahili, and he would narrate to us much hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then when he fell silent he said: Have you comprehended? Convey as you were conveyed to."
"7700","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Sulayman ibn Salama al-Khabairi from Baqiyya from Safwan ibn Amr from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Do the people of Paradise marry? He said: Yes, with a member that does not tire, and desire that does not cease, thrusting, thrusting."
"7701","Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi from Sulaym ibn Amir. Narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ala ibn Zibriq al-Himsi from his father. And narrated Umara ibn Wathima al-Misri and Abd al-Rahman ibn Muawiya al-Utbi al-Misri, both saying: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi narrated to us from Amr ibn al-Harith from Abdullah ibn Salim from al-Zubaydi; Salim ibn Amir narrated to us that he heard Abu Umama saying: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, establish the hadd of Allah upon me, once or twice, but he turned away from him. Then the prayer was established, and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer, he said: Where is the one who says: Establish the hadd of Allah upon me? He said: It is I. He said: Did you complete ablution and pray the Isha with us? He said: Yes. He said: Then you are from your sin as your mother bore you, so do not repeat. And Allah, Almighty and Exalted, revealed: And establish prayer at the two ends of the day and at the approaches of the night."
"7702","Narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn al-Ala ibn Zibriq al-Himsi from his father. And narrated Umara ibn Wathima al-Misri from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi; both saying: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us from Abdullah ibn Salim from al-Zubaydi; Salim ibn Amir narrated to us that Abu Umama narrated to them that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the Farewell Pilgrimage, upon his she-camel al-Jada, having put his feet into the stirrups, placing one hand upon the front of the saddle and the other upon its back, stretching with that, saying: O people, be silent, for perhaps you will not see me after this year of yours. Then Allah, Almighty and Exalted, sent a man from the people, who said: What shall we do? He said: You worship your Lord, establish your five, give the zakat of your wealth, fast your month, and obey those in authority over you, you will enter the Paradise of your Lord."
"7703","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Musaffa from Baqiyya from al-Zubaydi from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7704","Narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ala ibn Zibriq al-Himsi from his father. And narrated Umara ibn Wathima al-Himsi from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi; both saying: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us from Abdullah ibn Salim from al-Zubaydi; Salim ibn Amir narrated to us that Abu Umama narrated to them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his Companions at the night prayer: Gather for prayer tomorrow, for I have a need with you. A group of them said: O so-and-so, take the first word the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) speaks, and you are the one next to him, lest anything of the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) escape them. When he finished the dawn prayer, he said: Have you gathered as I commanded you? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him; have you comprehended this? Have you comprehended this? Have you comprehended this? They said: Yes. He said: Establish prayer and give zakat; establish prayer and give zakat; establish prayer and give zakat; have you comprehended this? Have you comprehended this? Have you comprehended this? They said: Yes. He said: Hear and obey; hear and obey; have you comprehended this? Have you comprehended this? Have you comprehended this? They said: Yes. So we thought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would speak much speech, then his speech was examined, and behold, the whole matter had been gathered for him."
"7705","Hariz ibn Uthman from Sulaym ibn Amir. Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Abu al-Mughira. And narrated Abu Zayd Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi; both saying: Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us from Sulaym ibn Amir, that Abu Umama narrated to him that a young youth came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me adultery. The people shouted, so he said: Quiet. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring him near. So he drew near until he sat before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Would you like it for your mother? He said: No. He said: Likewise people do not like it for their mothers. Would you like it for your daughter? He said: No. He said: Likewise people do not like it for their daughters. Would you like it for your sister? He said: No. He said: Likewise people do not like it for their sisters. Would you like it for your paternal aunt? He said: No. He said: Likewise people do not like it for their paternal aunts. Would you like it for your maternal aunt? He said: No. He said: Likewise people do not like it for their maternal aunts. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand upon his chest and said: O Allah, expiate his sin, purify his heart, and guard his chastity."
"7706","Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Isa ibn Yunus. And narrated Hashim ibn Marthad al-Tabarani from Safwan ibn Salih from al-Walid ibn Muslim; both saying: Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us from Sulaym ibn Amir, who heard it from Abu Umama, who said: Nothing remained over from the household of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of barley bread."
"7707","Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir. Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Abu al-Mughira Abd al-Quddus ibn al-Hajjaj from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best slaughter is the horned ram, and the best shroud is the hulla."
"7708","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best slaughter is the horned ram, and the best shroud is the hulla."
"7709","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you comes to his wife, let him cover himself and his wife, and let them not strip as donkeys strip."
"7710","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of the five prayers is like the example of a sweet river flowing at the door of one of you, in which he bathes every day five times; what then remains upon him of dirt?"
"7711","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When you see a matter you cannot change, be patient until Allah is the One who changes it."
"7712","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is nothing in ham, and the evil eye is true, and the truest of birds is the good omen."
"7713","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When the imam says takbir, say takbir; when he bows, bow; when he prostrates, prostrate; when he raises his head from bowing, raise; and if he prays sitting, pray sitting all together."
"7714","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing interrupts prayer."
"7715","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes on Friday and bathes, and goes early and arrives early, and draws near and listens and is silent, he will have two shares of reward."
"7716","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The one hastening on Friday is like one offering a camel, and the one next to him like one offering a bull, and the one next to him like one offering a sheep, and the one next to him like one offering a chicken."
"7717","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab from Abu al-Mughira from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The angels are sent on Friday to the doors of the mosque, writing the first then the first; when the imam ascends the pulpit, the scrolls are folded."
"7718","Narrated Abu Zayd Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best rows of men are the first of them, and the worst rows of men are the last of them; and the best rows of women are the last of them, and the worst rows of women are the first of them."
"7719","Narrated Abu Zayd al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A man may stand in prayer and supplicate a supplication, and he is forgiven, along with whoever is behind him of people."
"7720","Narrated Abu Zayd al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Holy Spirit breathed into my heart that no soul will leave the world until it completes its term and exhausts its provision; so seek beautifully, and let not slowness of provision carry you to seek it through disobedience of Allah, for what is with Allah is not attained except through His obedience."
"7721","Narrated Abu Zayd al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah, Almighty and Exalted, disliked eloquence for you, all eloquence."
"7722","Narrated Abu Zayd from Abu al-Yaman from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed the loudmouthed braggarts are in the Fire."
"7723","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah, Almighty and Exalted, says to the angels: Go to My servant and pour affliction upon him abundantly. So they come to him and pour affliction upon him; then he praises Allah, and they return and say: O our Lord, we poured affliction upon him abundantly as You commanded us. He says: Return, for I love to hear his voice."
"7724","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah, Almighty and Exalted, surely tries one of you with affliction just as one of you tries his gold with fire. Of it is that which comes out like pure gold, and that is the one whom Allah, Almighty and Exalted, protected from doubts; and of it is that which comes out like gold below that, and that is the one who doubted somewhat; and of them is one who comes out like black gold, and that is the one who has been tempted."
"7725","Narrated Abu Zayd al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought al-Husayn, and he began kissing him while he was in his lap, then he urinated, and they went to take him, so he said: Do not interrupt his flow. So he left him until he finished his urination."
"7726","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, that a man entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a ring of brass, and he said: What is this ring? He said: Against al-wahina. He said: Verily it only increases weakness."
"7727","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed when the Muslim falls ill, Allah, Almighty and Exalted, reveals to His angels, saying: O My angels, I have shackled My servant with a shackle of My shackles; if I seize him, I forgive him, and if I heal him, then a body forgiven him, with no sin upon him."
"7728","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Prohibited by breastfeeding is what is prohibited by lineage."
"7729","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: He is not of us who does not honor our elder and have mercy on our young."
"7730","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Entrusted with the believer are one hundred and ninety angels defending him from what has not been decreed upon him; of that company are nine angels defending him as one defends a bowl of honey from flies on a hot summer day; and had it appeared to you, you would have seen them upon mountain and plain, all of them extending their hands, opening their mouths; and had the servant been entrusted to himself for the blink of an eye, the devils would have snatched him."
"7731","Narrated Abu Zayd al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman. And narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Ali ibn Ayyash; both saying: Ufayr ibn Madan narrated to us from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama al-Bahili, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Entrusted with the sun are nine angels hurling snow at it every day; were it not for that, it would not come upon anything without burning it."
"7732","Narrated Abu Zayd al-Hawti from Abu al-Yaman from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, and testifies that I am the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), let his house suffice him, and let him weep over his sin. And whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, and testifies that I am the Messenger of Allah, let him speak good to gain, or keep silent from evil to be safe."
"7733","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How God-fearing, how God-fearing, how God-fearing is a shepherd atop a mountain establishing prayer therein."
"7734","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed every nation has a tourism, and the tourism of my nation is jihad in the cause of Allah. And every nation has a monasticism, and the monasticism of my nation is guarding the frontiers in the faces of the enemy."
"7735","And with its isnad, from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man may attain by good character the rank of one standing in night prayer, thirsty in the midday heats."
"7736","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Iqbal al-Harrani from Abu Jafar al-Nufayli; Ufayr ibn Madan narrated to us from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the leather socks and the turban during the expedition of Tabuk."
"7737","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu Jafar al-Nufayli from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out on one of his expeditions and passed by dwellings of Arabs, and sent to them: Is there water for the ablution of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: We have no water except in the skin of a dead animal, which we tanned with milk. So he sent to them: Its tanning is its purification. Then some of it was brought and he performed ablution, then prayed."
"7738","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib from al-Hakam ibn Musa from al-Walid ibn Muslim from Abu Aidh Ufayr ibn Madan; Salim ibn Amir narrated to me from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that a woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him that her husband was on one of the expeditions, and she sought his permission to depict a palm tree in her house, but he refused her and forbade her."
"7739","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib from al-Hakam ibn Musa from al-Walid ibn Muslim from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who heard him narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The gates of heaven are opened and supplication is answered in four places: at the meeting of ranks in the cause of Allah, at the descent of rain, at the establishment of prayer, and at the sight of the Kaaba."
"7740","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Missisi from Amr ibn Uthman al-Himsi from al-Walid ibn Muslim from Ufayr ibn Madan, that he heard Sulaym ibn Amir narrating from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I saw the pillar of the Book taken from beneath my pillow, and I followed it with my sight, and behold, it was a radiant light until I thought it had descended with it; it was directed to Sham. And I interpreted that when tribulations occur, faith will be in Sham."
"7741","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi from Hisham ibn Ammar. And narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym from his father. And narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Dawud ibn Rashid; all saying: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The wife of Uthman ibn Mazun was a beautiful, perfumed woman who loved clothing and adornment for her husband. Aisha visited her while she was unkempt. She said: What is this state of yours? She said: A group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Ali ibn Abi Talib and Abdullah ibn Rawaha and Uthman ibn Mazun, have devoted themselves to worship, abstained from women and from eating meat, fasting the day and standing the night; so I disliked showing him any of my state that would call him to what is with me, to leave alone what he devotes himself to. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, Aisha informed him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his sandal and carried it by the forefinger of his left finger, then set out quickly until he entered upon them, and asked them about their state. They said: We intended good. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was only sent with the easy, tolerant hanifiyya, and I was not sent with innovated monasticism. Verily, people innovated monasticism and it was written upon them, but they did not observe it with its due observance. So eat meat, and come to women, and fast and break fast, and pray and sleep, for with that I have been commanded."
"7742","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yaqub al-Ahwazi from Isa ibn Abi Harb al-Saffar from Qays ibn Muhammad ibn Imran al-Kindi from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The martyr of the sea is like two martyrs of the land, and the seasick in the sea is like one wallowing in his blood on land; and what is between two waves in the sea is like crossing the world in obedience to Allah. And indeed Allah entrusted the Angel of Death with seizing souls except the martyrs of the sea, for He Himself takes their souls; and He forgives the martyrs of the land all sins except debt, and He forgives the martyrs of the sea all sins, including debt."
"7743","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym from his father. And narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi from Hisham ibn Ammar; both saying: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us from Ufayr ibn Madan from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Quran was revealed in three places: in Mecca, and Medina, and Sham."
"7744","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi from Ali ibn Hujr from al-Walid ibn Muslim; Ufayr ibn Madan narrated to us, that he heard Sulaym ibn Amir narrating from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Sham is Allah's elite portion of His lands; He selects His elite of His servants for it. Whoever leaves Sham for other than it, it is in wrath; and whoever enters it, it is in mercy."
"7745","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi from Hisham ibn Ammar from al-Walid ibn Muslim; Ufayr ibn Madan narrated to us from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The gates of heaven are opened and the supplication of the Muslim is answered at the establishment of prayer, at the descent of rain, at the advance of ranks, and at the sight of the Kaaba."
"7746","Yazid ibn Abi Malik from Sulaym ibn Amir. Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Khalaf al-Dimashqi from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman. And narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Haywa ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami; both saying: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Yazid ibn Abi Malik from Sulaym ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no Muslim in charge of ten or more except he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with his hand chained to his neck; his righteousness will free him, or his sin will bind him; its beginning is blame, its middle is regret, and its end is punishment on the Day of Resurrection."
"7747","Hashim ibn Zayd from Sulaym ibn Amir. Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi from Amr ibn Abi Salama; Sadaqa narrated to us from Hashim ibn Zayd from Sulaym Abu Yahya, that he heard Abu Umama narrating that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was asked: Do the people of Paradise marry? He said: Yes, with a member that does not tire, and desire that does not cease, thrusting, thrusting."
"7748","Abdullah ibn Dinar al-Bahrani al-Himsi from Abu Umama. Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal; al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to me from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Abdullah ibn Dinar from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Do not fast Saturday except as an obligation; and were you to find nothing but the bark of a tree, then break your fast upon it."
"7749","Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Himsi from Abu Umama. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Amr ibn Uthman from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Bahir ibn Sad from Khalid ibn Madan from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Abu Umama al-Bahili saying: On the Day of Resurrection there will emerge a radiant, white-marked company filling the horizon, their light like the sun. Then a caller will call: The unlettered Prophet. So every unlettered prophet will stir for it. Then it will be said: Muhammad and his umma. Then they will enter Paradise with neither reckoning nor punishment upon them. Then another radiant, white-marked company will emerge, their light like the moon on the night of full moon, filling the horizon with their light. Then a caller will call: The unlettered Prophet. So every unlettered prophet will stir for it. Then it will be said: Muhammad and his umma. Then they will enter Paradise with neither reckoning nor punishment. Then another radiant, white-marked company will emerge, their light like the greatest star in the sky, filling the horizon with their light. Then a caller will call: The unlettered Prophet. So every unlettered prophet will stir for it. Then it will be said: Muhammad and his umma. Then they will enter Paradise with neither reckoning nor punishment. Then your Lord, Almighty and Exalted, will come, then the Scale will be set and the Reckoning."
"7750","Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama. Narrated Abu Zura Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi from Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Yazid ibn Ayham from Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no man in charge of ten or more except he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with his hand chained to his neck; his righteousness will free him, or his sin will bind him; its beginning is blame, its middle is regret, and its end is disgrace on the Day of Resurrection."
"7751","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Musaffa; Yahya ibn Said al-Attar narrated to me from Yunus ibn Uthman from Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama, raised: When Allah, Almighty and Exalted, intends good for a servant, He honeys him before his death. It was said: What is His honeying him? He said: He provides him a righteous deed before his death."
"7752","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari from Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Mawsili from Faraj ibn Fadala from Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the snakes of houses except those with two stripes and the tailless, for they cause miscarriage and blind the eyes."
"7753","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari from Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Mawsili from Faraj ibn Fadala from Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row; straighten your rows, level your shoulders, be gentle to the hands of your brothers, and close the gaps, for Satan enters among you like al-hadhaf; and al-hadhaf is the young of small sheep."
"7754","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati from Said ibn Sulayman. And narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi ibn Thalaba; my father narrated to me from Faraj ibn Fadala from Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage, and he addressed the people, saying in his admonition: Perhaps you will not see me after this year of yours, three times. Then a tall, disheveled man stood, as if of the men of Shanua, and said: Then what shall we do, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Worship your Lord, pray your five, fast your month, and pay the zakat of your wealth with your souls content with it, you will enter the Paradise of your Lord."
"7755","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari from Asim ibn Ali. And narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati from Said ibn Sulayman; both saying: Faraj ibn Fadala narrated to us from Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what was the beginning of your matter? He said: The supplication of my father Ibrahim, and the glad tidings of Isa son of Maryam, and my mother saw that a light came out of her by which the palaces of Sham were illuminated."
"7756","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati from Said ibn Sulayman from Faraj ibn Fadala from Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama, who said: None of this umma will remain except he will enter Paradise, except one who strays from Allah like a bad camel strays from its people. Whoever believes me, then indeed Allah, Almighty and Exalted, says: None shall enter it but the most wretched, who denied and turned away; he denied what Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought and turned away from him."
"7757","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqa from al-Abbas ibn Abi Talib from Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Zabbar al-Kalbi from Sharqi ibn al-Qutami from Luqman ibn Amir, who said: I came to Abu Umama al-Bahili and said: Narrate to us what you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had a rock weighing ten pregnant camels been hurled from the edge of Hell, it would not reach its bottom for seventy autumns until it ends at Ghayy and Atham. It was said: And what are Ghayy and Atham? He said: Two wells in the lowest Hell from which flows the pus of the people of the Fire, and they are the two that Allah mentioned in His Book: They neglected prayer and followed desires, so they will meet Ghayy; and Whoever does that will meet Atham."
"7758","Narrated al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari from Sulayman ibn Salama al-Khabairi from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid; Salama ibn Umayr narrated to us from Luqman ibn Amir from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah said: Every outgoing and incoming pasturing upon a people is forbidden to others."
"7759","Al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid al-Shami, freedman of Muawiya, from Abu Umama; Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari from al-Qasim. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father from Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman, who said: I met one hundred of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7760","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abda al-Missisi from Abu Tawba al-Rabi ibn Nafi. And narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abdullah ibn Yusuf; both saying: al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama al-Bahili, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever walks to a prescribed prayer while purified, his reward is like the reward of the pilgrim in ihram; and whoever walks to the forenoon glorification, his reward is like the reward of one performing Umra. And a prayer upon the trace of a prayer, with no idle talk between them, is a book in Illiyyin."
"7761","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father from al-Walid ibn Muslim; Yahya ibn al-Harith and Abu Muayd narrated to us from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7762","Narrated Abdullah ibn Musa ibn Abi Uthman al-Anmati from al-Hakam ibn Musa from Maslama ibn Ali al-Khushani from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari from al-Qasim from Abu Umama: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to dislike seeing a man loud, raising his voice, and used to love seeing him soft-voiced."
"7763","Narrated Ishaq ibn Hassan al-Anmati from Hisham ibn Ammar from Sadaqa ibn Khalid from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever loves for Allah, hates for Allah, gives for Allah, and withholds for Allah, has perfected faith; and the nearest of you to me on the Day of Resurrection are the best of you in character."
"7764","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Hashim al-Balabakki from his father from Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7765","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Sari al-Asqalani from Muhammad ibn Shuayb from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Going and returning to the mosques is of jihad in the cause of Allah."
"7766","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father. And narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Ali ibn Bahr. And narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sarraj al-Askari from Dawud ibn Rashid; all saying: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us; Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari narrated to me from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bathe on Friday, for whoever bathes on Friday has expiation for what is between Friday and Friday, plus three days more."
"7767","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from al-Mughira ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Harrani from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman from Musa ibn Ulayy from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays the dawn prayer in congregation, then sits remembering Allah until the sun rises, then stands and bows two rakas, returns with the reward of a Hajj and Umra."
"7768","Narrated al-Husayn ibn al-Sumayda al-Antaki from Musa ibn Ayyub al-Nasibi. And narrated Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari from Sulayman ibn Salama al-Khabairi from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Ishaq ibn Malik al-Hadrami from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed the one who recites the Quran aloud is like the one who gives charity aloud, and the one who conceals the Quran is like the one who conceals charity."
"7769","Narrated Abdullah ibn al-Sabbah al-Asbahani from al-Walid ibn Shuja ibn al-Walid from Baqiyya ibn al-Walid from Ishaq ibn Malik from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever begins with salam is the most entitled to Allah and His Messenger."
"7770","Narrated Wathila ibn al-Husayn al-Irqi from Kathir ibn Ubayd al-Hadhdha from Baqiyya from Ishaq ibn Malik al-Hadrami from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The siwak is a purifier for the mouth, pleasing to the Lord, Exalted."
"7771","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Nadr al-Askari from Sulayman ibn Salama from Baqiyya from Maslama ibn Ali from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays Isha in congregation has taken his share of Laylat al-Qadr."
"7772","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Sulayman ibn Salama al-Khabairi from Muhammad ibn Shuayb from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah, Almighty and Exalted, says: O son of Adam, bow to Me four rakas from the beginning of the day, I will suffice you its end."
"7773","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym from his father. And narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Duhaym. And narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi al-Missisi from Amr ibn Uthman al-Himsi; all saying: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us; Yahya ibn al-Harith narrated to me from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever does not fight, nor equip a fighter, nor succeed a fighter among his family, Allah will afflict him with a calamity on the Day of Resurrection."
"7774","Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi from Jubara ibn Mughallis from Yahya ibn Uqba ibn Abi al-Ayzar from Muhammad ibn Juhada from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dimashqi from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites ten verses in a night is not written among the heedless; whoever recites one hundred verses, the standing of a night is written for him; whoever recites two hundred verses is written among the devout; whoever recites four hundred verses is written among the worshippers; whoever recites five hundred verses is written among the memorizers; whoever recites six hundred verses is written among the humble; whoever recites eight hundred verses is written among the penitent; whoever recites one thousand verses, he awakes with a qintar for him, and the qintar is one thousand two hundred uqiyyas, and the uqiyya is better than what is between heaven and earth, or he said: than what the sun has risen upon; and whoever recites two thousand verses is of the deserving."
"7775","Narrated Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Sufyan al-Raqqi from Ayyub ibn Muhammad al-Wazzan. And narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Khallal al-Dimashqi; both saying: al-Walid ibn al-Walid narrated to us; Ibn Thawban narrated to us from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sale of singing women is not lawful, nor their purchase, nor trade in them, and their price is unlawful. And he said: This verse was revealed concerning that: And of people is he who purchases idle talk, until the end of the verse. Then he followed it: By the One who sent me with the truth, no man raises his voice in song except Allah, Almighty and Exalted, sends at that two devils perching upon his shoulders, then they keep striking with their feet upon his chest, and he pointed to his own chest, until he is the one who falls silent."
"7776","Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari; both saying: Dawud ibn Rashid narrated to us from Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to join Shaban with Ramadan."
"7777","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Said ibn Amr al-Hadrami from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Yahya from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are no people who sit a sitting, then rise from it without remembering Allah and without sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except that sitting will be a regret upon them."
"7778","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father from al-Walid ibn Muslim; Yahya ibn al-Harith and Abu Muayd narrated to us from al-Qasim from Aisha from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: A prayer upon the trace of a prayer, with no idle talk between them, is a book in Illiyyin."
"7779","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Irq from his father from al-Walid from Yahya from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7780","Narrated Yahya ibn Abd al-Baqi from Muhammad ibn Musaffa from Baqiyya; al-Walid narrated to us from Yahya from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7781","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi from Amr ibn Uthman from Muhammad ibn Shuayb ibn Shabur from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever leaves his house purified has like the reward of the pilgrim in ihram; and whoever walks to the forenoon supererogatory prayer, nothing wearying him except that, has like the reward of one performing Umra."
"7782","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Hashim al-Balabakki from his father from Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz from Yahya ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Indeed of faith is good character, and the best of you in faith are the best of you in character."
"7783","Kathir ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim. Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abdullah ibn Salih; Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to me from Kathir ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu Umama, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The matter will only increase in severity, wealth will only increase in abundance, people will only increase in miserliness, and the Hour will not rise except upon the worst of people."
"7784","Ghaylan ibn Anas from al-Qasim. Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi; my father narrated to me from Amr ibn Abi Salama from Abu Muhammad Isa ibn Musa, that he heard Ghaylan ibn Anas narrating from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Greatest Name of Allah is in three suras of the Quran: al-Baqara, and Al Imran, and Ta Ha."
"7785","Al-Ala ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim. Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Muhammad ibn Aidh from al-Haytham ibn Humayd from al-Ala ibn al-Harith from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me adultery. The people shouted at him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring him near. He drew near until he sat before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Would you like it for your mother? He said: No. He said: Likewise people do not like it for their mothers. He said: Would you like it for your daughter? He said: No. He said: Likewise people do not like it for their daughters. He said: Would you like it for your sister? He said: No. He said: Likewise people do not like it for their sisters. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand upon his chest and said: O Allah, expiate his sin, purify his heart, and guard his chastity."
"7786","Narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Muhammad ibn Aidh from al-Haytham ibn Humayd; al-Ala ibn al-Harith narrated to us; al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to us from Abu Umama, that a man sought the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for wandering asceticism, and he said: Indeed the wandering of my nation is jihad in the cause of Allah, Almighty and Exalted."
"7787","Abu Muayd Hafs ibn Ghaylan from al-Qasim. Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi from Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Dimashqi from Sadaqa ibn Abdullah from Abu Muayd Hafs ibn Ghaylan from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman from Abu Umama from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: No contagion, no Safar, no Ham, and two months are not completed; and whoever violates a covenant of protection will not smell the fragrance of Paradise."
"7788","Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza al-Dimashqi from Amr ibn Hashim al-Bayruti from al-Haytham ibn Humayd from Abu Muayd from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: No Safar, no Hama, no contagion."
"7789","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym from his father. And narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Hisham ibn Ammar; both saying: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us; Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir and Abu Muayd Hafs ibn Ghaylan narrated to us from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: A prayer upon the trace of a prayer, with no idle talk between them, is a book in Illiyyin."
"7790","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Duhaym al-Dimashqi from his father from al-Walid ibn Muslim; Yahya ibn al-Harith and Abu Muayd narrated to us from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever walks to a prescribed prayer, it is in the standing of a Hajj; and whoever walks to a voluntary prayer, it is in the standing of an Umra; and a prayer upon the trace of a prayer, with no idle talk between them, is a book in Illiyyin."
"7791","Urwa ibn Ruwaym al-Lakhmi from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu Umama Sudayy ibn Ajlan. Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from his father. And narrated Ahmad ibn Ziyad ibn Zakariyya al-Iyadi from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti. And narrated Amr ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Zibriq al-Himsi from my uncle Muhammad ibn Ibrahim. And narrated Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ala al-Himsi; all saying: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywa from Urwa ibn Ruwaym from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Indeed the companion of the left raises the pen six hours from the erring or offending Muslim servant; if he regrets and seeks Allah's forgiveness for it, he casts it, otherwise one is written."
"7792","Narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Ismail ibn Hud from Muhammad ibn Yazid from Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywa from Urwa ibn Ruwaym from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever performs ablution, then comes to the mosque and prays two rakas before Fajr, then sits until he prays Fajr, his prayer that day is written in the prayer of the righteous, and he is written among the delegation of the Most Merciful."
"7793","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sarraj al-Askari from Ismail ibn Ibrahim al-Tarjuman from al-Hayyaj ibn Bistam from Utba ibn Humayd from Muhammad ibn Ubada from Urwa ibn Ruwaym from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever commits sins among people of whom he is one, and they do not prevent him from that until they change the evil, the covenant of Allah is free of them."
"7794","Narrated Muhammad ibn Ahmad Abu Jafar al-Tirmidhi from Hafs ibn Umar al-Juddi from Marwan ibn Muawiya from Muhammad ibn Said from Urwa ibn Ruwaym from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is humility, for humility is in the heart; let no Muslim harm a Muslim. How often a doubled one in tattered garments, had he sworn upon Allah, He would fulfill it."
"7795","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ahmad ibn Yunus al-Ahwazi from Hafs ibn Amr al-Rabbali from Muhammad ibn Umar al-Waqidi from Hisham ibn Sad from Urwa ibn Ruwaym from al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not wear silk nor gold."
"7796","Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywa from al-Qasim from Abu Umama. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami and al-Numan ibn Ahmad al-Wasiti; both saying: Muhammad ibn Harb al-Nasai narrated to us from Sulayman ibn Ziyad from Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywa from al-Qasim from Abu Umama from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I am a guarantor, for one who leaves disputation while he is right, of a house in the outskirts of Paradise, and a house in the middle of Paradise, and a house in the highest Paradise."
"7797","Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from al-Qasim. Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell fruit until its soundness appears."
"7798","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, on the day of Khaybar, that pregnant women be approached until they give birth."
"7799","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the woman who joins hair and the one joined to, and the tattooing woman and the tattooed."
"7800","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir from Makhul and al-Qasim from Abu Umama, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that shares be sold until they are divided."
